# Generalized symmetries, defects, gauging, and anomalies

Research note dated 2026-10-23 for the new physics category **advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry**. This is a source-backed prose design for the generalized-symmetry, defect, and anomaly strand. It records current local suppliers, candidate interfaces, proof obligations, and a bounded research frontier. It does not add production items, establish a physical model, certify a proof, or claim an exhaustive October 2026 literature review. The engine and publication steps remain separate.

The companion subject notes own the full non-Abelian gauge-theory and BRST/BV constructions, general EFT matching and experiment analyses, and the supersymmetry and localization/BPS models. This note gives those strands precise symmetry, defect, and anomaly interfaces. Reuse the EFT proposal **def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data** as the shared anomaly-matching interface if the builder keeps it; do not author a competing definition with a different scope. The gauge researcher proposed **def-qftx-nonabelian-brst-complex**, **lem-qftx-nonabelian-brst-nilpotence**, **def-qftx-bv-antibracket-master-action**, and **lem-qftx-bv-master-equation-brst-nilpotence**. These are planned suppliers, not current results, and a consumer may use them only after their exact contracts and proof status are checked.

## Content model and exact definitions needed before claims

Fix a spacetime dimension \(d\), a smooth \(d\)-manifold \(M\), and the actual tangential structure of the selected theory: for example, oriented, spin, or a specified structure incorporating spacetime and internal symmetries. State the signature, orientation, time orientation, boundary/collar conventions, and whether the path integral is defined on closed \(M\), on \(M\) with boundary, or on a restricted class of backgrounds. A claim proved on oriented manifolds cannot silently be applied to spin manifolds or to nonorientable manifolds. A Euclidean partition function used to study Lorentzian unitarity also needs its reflection-positivity and continuation assumptions stated.

A **defect insertion** is a specified operator or boundary/interface condition supported on a closed embedded submanifold \(\Sigma\hookrightarrow M\), with specified local field data, orientation or framing when required, and permitted junction operators. Its codimension, source/target theories, and insertion into correlation functions must be fixed. Call the defect **topological** only after specifying that its correlators are invariant under allowed isotopies of \(\Sigma\) in the complement of other insertions, with junction data transported coherently. A pictorial crossing or familiar name does not establish topologicality.

A \(p\)-form internal global symmetry \(G^{(p)}\) is an invertible topological symmetry whose generators are supported on codimension-\((p+1)\) submanifolds and whose charged genuine observables have \(p\)-dimensional support. For \(p>0\), \(G^{(p)}\) is Abelian under the usual group-like definition. A background is a \((p+1)\)-form connection, or for a finite discrete symmetry a specified cocycle/cochain with its gauge equivalences; its gauge parameter has degree \(p\). The charged operators, linking or intersection pairing, allowed backgrounds, and phase normalization are part of the definition. An extended charged operator can be non-genuine because it needs an attached lower-codimension defect; do not call it genuine without checking this dependence.

A 2-group with 0-form group \(G\), Abelian 1-form group \(A\), action \(\rho:G\to\mathrm{Aut}(A)\), and Postnikov class \([\beta]\in H^3_\rho(BG;A)\) has a coupled background. For a discrete model, a flat 1-cochain \(a\) and a 2-cochain \(B\) obey a twisted equation of the form \(\delta_a B=a^*\beta\). Its gauge transformations, cup-product convention, local coefficients, and cochain representatives must be supplied. The Postnikov class says that the two background gauge fields are not independent. It is not, by itself, a ’t Hooft anomaly. General \(n\)-groups and continuous backgrounds need their own definitions and should not be inferred from this 2-group contract.

A **noninvertible symmetry** is encoded by topological defects whose fusion is not group-like. In a finite semisimple example, simple defects \(D_a\) have fusion
\[
D_a\otimes D_b\simeq\bigoplus_c N_{ab}^{c}D_c,
\]
with specified nonnegative integer multiplicities, associator/junction maps, duality data, and coherence equations. An invertible simple object has a two-sided fusion inverse; the unit object, an invertible linear operator, a Grothendieck-ring unit, and an invertible defect are different notions. Finite semisimplicity and unitarity are not automatic. In dimensions above two, defects of several codimensions and their junctions generally require higher-categorical rather than ordinary fusion-category data.

Coupling a symmetry to a **background** means evaluating the theory with a nondynamical gauge field or defect network and keeping its gauge transformations visible. **Gauging** makes this background dynamical and sums/integrates over equivalence classes with the correct gauge-volume or automorphism weights and any allowed topological action. Gauging requires that the full background variation be removable by allowed local counterterms, or that the theory be specified relative to an inflow bulk. For finite group models, the cocycle action and the precise groupoid of fields/gauge transformations are part of the input. A finite-dimensional state sum does not prove existence of a continuum gauge theory.

A **’t Hooft anomaly** is the residual transformation of the partition function under background gauge transformations after all allowed \(d\)-dimensional local counterterms have been considered. This makes the partition function a section of an anomaly line, or equivalently a boundary object relative to an invertible \((d+1)\)-dimensional anomaly theory when such a description is available. It obstructs gauging the global symmetry and is preserved along a symmetry-preserving renormalization-group flow under the stated locality and background-coupling assumptions. It is different from a gauge anomaly of a dynamical gauge redundancy, which obstructs defining the proposed gauge theory unless a specified cancellation mechanism is supplied. It is also different from explicit symmetry breaking, a higher-group background constraint, and a nonconservation equation that is an operator-valued Ward identity rather than a c-number anomaly.

Separate at least four anomaly contracts:

1. **Perturbative/local anomalies** are infinitesimal background-gauge variations represented by local descent data. A selected \(d\)-dimensional chiral theory may have an invariant polynomial \(I_{d+2}\), a descent \(I_{d+2}=d I_{d+1}^{(0)}\), \(\delta I_{d+1}^{(0)}=d I_d^{(1)}\), and a variation proportional to \(\int_M I_d^{(1)}\). Fix anti-Hermitian versus Hermitian connections, trace normalization, chirality, \(2\pi\) and \(i\) conventions, the regulator, and admissible counterterms. In four dimensions the gauge and mixed gauge-gravitational terms live in degree six, but no coefficient follows from a name such as “chiral fermion” without the representations and the chosen normalization.
2. **Global anomalies** concern large or disconnected gauge transformations and can remain when the local anomaly polynomial vanishes. State the gauge-transformation group, spin/orientation data, determinant-line or eta-invariant convention, and exact background family. A perturbative descent calculation does not dispose of torsion or global phases.
3. **Mixed/higher-group anomalies** involve several backgrounds whose gauge transformations are coupled. Write the full background data and transformations before computing the variation. A characteristic differential form alone may miss torsion; ordinary \(H^{d+1}(BG;U(1))\) is a useful finite bosonic group-like model, not a universal classification of fermionic, higher-form, or categorical anomalies.
4. **Categorical/noninvertible anomalies** depend on defect fusion, associators, junctions, and background or gauging data for that exact higher category. Do not label every obstruction to a chosen gauging as an anomaly field theory until the local and global obstruction, counterterm equivalence, and possible inflow have all been specified.

Anomaly inflow is a conditional cancellation theorem: the boundary variation and the bulk variation must be evaluated in the same convention on manifolds with boundary, including the same tangential structure and background fields, and their product must be gauge invariant under all admitted transformations. Anomaly matching is likewise conditional: if the exact global symmetry is preserved along the RG flow, its anomaly class is unchanged modulo the declared counterterms. A unique trivially gapped symmetric IR phase is excluded only after the symmetry, anomaly, and allowed topological sectors have been fixed; gapless modes, symmetry breaking, topological order, or a relative boundary theory can match the anomaly.

For supersymmetry, distinguish fermionic super-Poincaré generators from internal 0-form symmetries and from higher-form charges. A physical supercharge requires a real Lorentzian form, spinor representation, adjoint operation, common operator domain, Hamiltonian, and a positive-energy Hilbert-space representation. Central charges and brane charges may yield BPS bounds but do not automatically define a generalized global symmetry. An \(R\)-symmetry is an internal global symmetry with its own background/anomaly data; the supercharges themselves are not gaugeable as an ordinary internal group. A supersymmetric anomaly multiplet, supersymmetric inflow action, defect-preserving superalgebra, Witten index, or localization formula needs its own exact model and hypotheses. In particular, a trace index requires trace-class or other controlled spectral hypotheses and a proof that continuum/boundary terms do not invalidate the pairing argument.

## Exact current suppliers and their limits

The table records actual local carriers inspected in this workspace. “Published” below is the repository’s local item/page metadata; it does not imply that the website has published the physics library. The physics README keeps website publication separate.

| Local page/items | Actual reusable content | Boundary that a new consumer must preserve |
|---|---|---|
| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory/qft-c01.md**: **def-qft-framework-units**, **post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement**; **qft-g01.md**: **post-qft-free-photon-model**, **post-qft-qed-local-coupling**; **qft-g02.md**: **post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients**, **post-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-model**, related finite/formal theorems | The selected Minkowski length-coordinate and SI/natural-unit dictionary; a positive transverse free photon model; a smooth classical charged Dirac/Maxwell coupling; and a separate finite graph/formal QED branch. | **post-qft-qed-local-coupling** explicitly uses compactly supported local gauge variations and does not cover large gauge transformations, nontrivial bundles, boundary charges, asymptotic sectors, or an interacting continuum Hilbert space. **qft-g02** does not assert formal-series convergence or a continuum limit. These pages are not suppliers for global anomalies. |
| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qftx-anomaly-cocycles.md**: **def-qftx-loop-current-cocycle**, **prop-qftx-loop-cocycle-jacobi**, **def-qftx-chiral-anomaly-interface** | A loop-current Lie-algebra central cocycle, its Jacobi property, and finite-dimensional representation-trace coefficients \(d^{abc}\) and \(\ell^a\) with fixed basis/normalization. | These are mathematical interfaces. **def-qftx-chiral-anomaly-interface** explicitly says that no spacetime, spinor field, action, gauge group integration, regulator, effective-action map, mixed-anomaly formula, or physical anomaly-cancellation criterion is supplied. Vanishing \(d^{abc}\) alone is not a general cancellation theorem. |
