{"id":"qftx-two-group-cochains","kind":"A","items":[{"id":"def-aqft-two-group-background","kind":"definition","domain":"mathematics","title":"Finite 2-group background cochains","reference":"items/def-aqft-two-group-background.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; current readiness is recorded in the run-local Step-1 receipt.","dependency_level":0,"deps":[],"dependency_roles":{},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Let $G$ be a finite group with identity $e$, let $A$ be a finite abelian group written additively, and let $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$ be a left action, so $\\rho_{gh}=\\rho_g\\rho_h$ and $\\rho_e=\\operatorname{id}_A$. A group $2$-cochain is a function $c:G^2\\to A$; it is normalized when it vanishes if either input is $e$. Its coboundary is\n\n$$\n(\\delta_\\rho c)(g,h,k)=\\rho_g c(h,k)-c(gh,k)+c(g,hk)-c(g,h).\n$$\n\nA normalized group $3$-cocycle is a function $\\beta:G^3\\to A$ that vanishes whenever any argument is $e$ and satisfies\n\n$$\n\\rho_g\\beta(h,k,l)-\\beta(gh,k,l)+\\beta(g,hk,l)-\\beta(g,h,kl)+\\beta(g,h,k)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $g,h,k,l\\in G$. Two normalized $3$-cocycles represent the same class when their difference is $\\delta_\\rho c$ for a normalized $2$-cochain $c$; this is an equivalence relation by additivity of $\\delta_\\rho$. The class $[\\beta]$ is the Postnikov class in this skeletal model.\n\nThe corresponding skeletal categorical $2$-group has objects $g\\in G$ and, for each $g$, one automorphism labelled by each $u\\in A$; there are no morphisms between distinct objects. Composition of automorphisms adds their labels, tensor product is\n\n$$\n(g,u)\\otimes(h,v)=(gh,u+\\rho_gv),\n$$\n\nand the associator from $(gh)k$ to $g(hk)$ has label $\\beta(g,h,k)$. The two pentagon path labels are $\\beta(gh,k,l)+\\beta(g,h,kl)$ and $\\rho_g\\beta(h,k,l)+\\beta(g,hk,l)+\\beta(g,h,k)$; equality of these labels is exactly the displayed $3$-cocycle equation.\n\nA finite ordered simplicial complex $K$ is a finite vertex set with a total order and a collection of nonempty finite vertex subsets, called simplices, closed under taking nonempty subsets. Write each simplex in increasing order. A $q$-cochain with values in a local coefficient system assigns an element to each ordered $q$-simplex at its first vertex. A flat $G$-valued edge cochain is a family $a_{ij}\\in G$ for each edge $i<j$ such that $a_{ij}a_{jk}=a_{ik}$ on every triangle. It defines the local coefficient system with fiber $A$ at each vertex and transport $\\rho_{a_{ij}}$ from vertex $j$ to vertex $i$.\n\nFor each integer $q\\geq0$ and local-coefficient $q$-cochain $c$, define the twisted coboundary on each ordered $(q+1)$-simplex by\n\n$$\n(\\delta_a c)_{i_0\\ldots i_{q+1}}=\\rho_{a_{i_0i_1}}c_{i_1\\ldots i_{q+1}}+\\sum_{r=1}^{q+1}(-1)^r c_{i_0\\ldots\\widehat{i_r}\\ldots i_{q+1}}.\n$$\n\nIn particular, for an $A$-valued $2$-cochain $B$,\n\n$$\n(\\delta_aB)_{ijkl}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}B_{jkl}-B_{ikl}+B_{ijl}-B_{ijk},\\qquad (a^*\\beta)_{ijkl}=\\beta(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl}).\n$$\n\nA finite $2$-group background on $K$ is a flat edge cochain $a$ and an $A$-valued $2$-cochain $B$ satisfying $\\delta_aB=a^*\\beta$ on every ordered tetrahedron. This fixes the cochain representative, local coefficient action, and sign convention.\n\nA $1$-gauge transformation consists of vertex labels $g_i\\in G$ and edge labels $\\lambda_{ij}\\in A$ at vertex $i$. Define\n\n$$\na^g_{ij}=g_i a_{ij}g_j^{-1},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\zeta^g_{ijk}=-\\beta(g_i,a_{ij},a_{jk})+\\beta(a^g_{ij},g_j,a_{jk})-\\beta(a^g_{ij},a^g_{jk},g_k),\n$$\n\n$$\nB^{g,\\lambda}_{ijk}=\\rho_{g_i}B_{ijk}+\\zeta^g_{ijk}+(\\delta_{a^g}\\lambda)_{ijk},\\qquad (\\delta_{a^g}\\lambda)_{ijk}=\\rho_{a^g_{ij}}\\lambda_{jk}-\\lambda_{ik}+\\lambda_{ij}.\n$$\n\nChanging the chosen representative to $\\beta'=\\beta+\\delta_\\rho c$ for a normalized $2$-cochain $c$ and simultaneously replacing $B$ by $B+a^*c$ changes the cochain presentation but preserves the background equation: on every tetrahedron, flatness gives\n\n$$\n(\\delta_a(a^*c))_{ijkl}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}c(a_{jk},a_{kl})-c(a_{ik},a_{kl})+c(a_{ij},a_{jl})-c(a_{ij},a_{jk})=(\\delta_\\rho c)(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl}).\n$$\n\nThis finite mathematical model makes no assertion that a physical theory realizes the symmetry or has an anomaly.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Francesco Benini, Clay Córdova, and Po-Shen Hsin, On 2-Group Global Symmetries and Their Anomalies, §2.3","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.09336","locator":"PDF pp. 18–19, equations (2.14)–(2.20)"},{"title":"John C. Baez and Aaron D. Lauda, Higher-Dimensional Algebra V: 2-Groups, §8.3","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0307200","locator":"PDF pp. 54–59, especially the associator-pentagon calculation on p. 58"},{"title":"Sakura Schäfer-Nameki, ICTP Lectures on (Non-)Invertible Generalized Symmetries, §§2.5 and 2.8.1","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.18296v2","locator":"Printed pp. 23–33, discrete backgrounds, Postnikov constraint, and its distinction from anomaly data"}]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"definition"},{"id":"thm-aqft-two-group-background-consistency","kind":"theorem","domain":"mathematics","title":"Finite 2-group background gauge consistency","reference":"items/thm-aqft-two-group-background-consistency.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; current readiness is recorded in the run-local Step-1 receipt.","dependency_level":1,"deps":["def-aqft-two-group-background"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-two-group-background":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex; let $G$ be a finite group, $A$ a finite abelian group written additively, $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$ a left action, and $\\beta:G^3\\to A$ a normalized group $3$-cocycle with the convention that its associator is directed from $(gh)k$ to $g(hk)$. Let $a_{ij}\\in G$ be flat on every ordered triangle, let $B_{ijk}\\in A$ be an $A$-valued $2$-cochain at the first vertex, and assume on every ordered tetrahedron that\n\n$$\n\\rho_{a_{ij}}B_{jkl}-B_{ikl}+B_{ijl}-B_{ijk}=\\beta(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl}).\n$$\n\nFor arbitrary vertex labels $g_i\\in G$ and edge labels $\\lambda_{ij}\\in A$, put $a'_{ij}=g_i a_{ij}g_j^{-1}$ and\n\n$$\n\\zeta_{ijk}=-\\beta(g_i,a_{ij},a_{jk})+\\beta(a'_{ij},g_j,a_{jk})-\\beta(a'_{ij},a'_{jk},g_k).\n$$\n\n$$\nB'_{ijk}=\\rho_{g_i}B_{ijk}+\\zeta_{ijk}+\\rho_{a'_{ij}}\\lambda_{jk}-\\lambda_{ik}+\\lambda_{ij}.\n$$\n\nThen $a'$ is flat and $(a',B')$ satisfies the same twisted tetrahedron equation. If $(g,\\lambda)$ is followed by $(h,\\mu)$, with $a''_{ij}=h_i a'_{ij}h_j^{-1}$, their composite is $(hg,\\nu)$ where\n\n$$\n\\nu_{ij}=\\beta(h_i,g_i,a_{ij})+\\rho_{h_i}\\lambda_{ij}-\\beta(h_i,a'_{ij},g_j)+\\mu_{ij}+\\beta(a''_{ij},h_j,g_j).\n$$\n\nEach transformation is invertible: for a fixed source background, put $h_i=g_i^{-1}$ and\n\n$$\n\\mu_{ij}=-\\beta(h_i,g_i,a_{ij})-\\rho_{h_i}\\lambda_{ij}+\\beta(h_i,a'_{ij},g_j)-\\beta(a_{ij},h_j,g_j);\n$$\n\nthen its composite has $\\nu_{ij}=0$. Treating a gauge arrow as its induced map on background cochains, sequential maps compose associatively, and the displayed formula gives a representative for each composite. This is a mathematical consistency result and does not assert a physical anomaly theorem.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Francesco Benini, Clay Córdova, and Po-Shen Hsin, On 2-Group Global Symmetries and Their Anomalies, §2.3","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.09336","locator":"PDF pp. 18–19, equations (2.14)–(2.20)"},{"title":"John C. Baez and Aaron D. Lauda, Higher-Dimensional Algebra V: 2-Groups, §8.3","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0307200","locator":"PDF pp. 54–59, associator-pentagon and functor-coherence calculations"}]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct"},{"id":"def-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid","kind":"definition",