{"id":"qftx-finite-symmetry-gauging","kind":"A","items":[{"id":"def-aqft-background-gauging-data","kind":"definition","domain":"mathematics","title":"Finite background groupoid and cocycle-weighted gauging data","reference":"items/def-aqft-background-gauging-data.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; conditional two-group prerequisite status and current readiness are recorded in the batch notes and Step-1 receipt.","dependency_level":4,"deps":["def-aqft-two-group-background","def-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid","thm-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid-coherence","def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-two-group-background":"mathematical-premise","def-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid":"mathematical-premise","thm-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid-coherence":"mathematical-premise","def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex. A **finite background field groupoid** $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a small category of declared fields and invertible gauge equivalences, with finitely many isomorphism classes and finite automorphism groups. Its automorphisms retain the multiplicities declared by the field model. A **cocycle/action weight** is a function $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}\\mathcal F(K)\\to U(1)$ constant on every gauge arrow. Its normalized gauging sum is\n\n$$\nZ_K(W_K)=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0\\mathcal F(K)}\\frac{W_K(x)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal F(K)}(x)|}.\n$$\n\nFor the finite $2$-group specialization, fix finite $(G,A,\\rho,\\beta)$ and let $\\mathcal B(K)$ be the explicit background gauge $2$-groupoid: objects are flat $G$-edge cochains and $A$-face cochains satisfying $\\delta_aB=a^*\\beta$; $1$-arrows retain raw vertex/edge parameters $(g,\\lambda)$, and $2$-arrows are vertex cochains $t$ with $\\lambda'-\\lambda=\\delta_{a^g}t$, for fixed $g$. The raw $1$-composition is the displayed $\\tau$-corrected composition; its associator is $\\beta(k_i,h_i,g_i)$. Write $\\pi_0$ for object equivalence classes, $\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)$ for $2$-isomorphism classes of $1$-automorphisms, and $\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)$ for $2$-automorphisms of the identity $1$-arrow. For a phase weight $W_K$ constant on all $1$-arrows, define the higher normalized gauging sum\n\n$$\nZ^{(2)}_K(W_K)=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0\\mathcal B(K)}W_K(x)\\frac{|\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)|}{|\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)|}.\n$$\n\nDistinct raw gauge labels are not identified by equality of their effects on fields. The higher sum retains $\\pi_2$; it is not ordinary groupoid integration of the $1$-truncation. In particular a point has higher weight $|A|/|G|$ for unit action, whereas an ordinary field groupoid $BG$ has weight $1/|G|$.\n\nFor a subcomplex $L\\subseteq K$ and fixed field $s$ on $L$, relative fields and arrows use exact restriction to $s$, identity vertex gauge labels on $L$, zero edge gauge labels on $L$, and zero vertex $2$-gauge labels on $L$. Their higher sum uses the same $\\pi_2/\\pi_1$ prescription. Ordinary groupoids are included as $2$-groupoids with only identity $2$-arrows, so this prescription reduces to the preceding ordinary sum. A higher cocycle supplies an admissible scalar action only after its evaluated weight is proved invariant; nontrivial boundary line-bundle actions require their own transgression data and are not silently treated as scalar functions.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Moore and Saxena, TASI Lectures on Topological Field Theories and Differential Cohomology","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.07408","locator":"§23.3 PDF97–100 / printed96–99, especially (23.34)–(23.38); alternating homotopy-group measure, not an explicit proof of the local cochain gluing formula."},{"title":"Daniel S. Freed and Frank Quinn, Chern-Simons Theory with Finite Gauge Group","url":"https://www.math.ru.nl/~mueger/TQFT/FQ.pdf","locator":"§2, PDF pp. 8–11, for the finite bundle groupoid, automorphism measure, and cocycle-weighted partition function."},{"title":"Robbert Dijkgraaf and Edward Witten, Topological Gauge Theories and Group Cohomology","url":"https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/sns/%5B126%5DCommMathPhys129-1990.pdf","locator":"§§6.2 and 6.4–6.5, PDF pp. 22–30, for the finite 3D cocycle action, ordered simplex weights, and gauge-invariance scope."}]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct","axiom_use":"No choice principle is used; all field, arrow, and automorphism sets are finite."},{"id":"thm-aqft-finite-group-gauging","kind":"theorem","domain":"mathematics","title":"Finite cocycle-weighted groupoid gauging and gluing","reference":"items/thm-aqft-finite-group-gauging.md","design_state":"Step 1 authored; conditional two-group prerequisite status and current readiness are recorded in the batch notes and Step-1 receipt.","dependency_level":5,"deps":["def-aqft-background-gauging-data","def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration","def-qftx-finite-homotopy-pullback"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-background-gauging-data":"mathematical-premise","def-qftx-finite-groupoid-integration":"mathematical-premise","def-qftx-finite-homotopy-pullback":"mathematical-premise"},"inventory_expansion_required":false,"statement":"Let $K=K_1\\cup_\\Sigma K_2$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex written as a union of two finite subcomplexes with common subcomplex $\\Sigma$. Let $\\mathcal F$, $\\mathcal F_1$, $\\mathcal F_2$, and $\\mathcal S$ be finite background field groupoids for $K$, $K_1$, $K_2$, and $\\Sigma$, with restriction functors $r_i:\\mathcal F_i\\to\\mathcal S$. The homotopy pullback $\\mathcal H=\\mathcal F_2\\times^h_{\\mathcal S}\\mathcal F_1$ has objects $(x_2,x_1,\\alpha)$ with $\\alpha:r_2(x_2)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}r_1(x_1)$ and morphisms $(u_2,u_1)$ satisfying $r_1(u_1)\\circ\\alpha=\\alpha'\\circ r_2(u_2)$.\n\nAssume that restriction induces an equivalence $J:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal H$. Let $W,W_1,W_2$ be $U(1)$-valued cocycle/action weights on these groupoids, and assume that under $J$ the weight factors as $W(x_2,x_1,\\alpha)=W_2(x_2)W_1(x_1)$. For each object $s$ of $\\mathcal S$, define the homotopy fiber $(\\mathcal F_i)_s$ to have objects $(x_i,\\eta_i:r_i(x_i)\\overset{\\cong}{\\longrightarrow}s)$ and morphisms $u_i$ satisfying $\\eta'_i\\circ r_i(u_i)=\\eta_i$, and put\n\n$$ Z_i(s):=\\sum_{[(x_i,\\eta_i)]\\in\\pi_0((\\mathcal F_i)_s)} \\frac{W_i(x_i)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{(\\mathcal F_i)_s}(x_i,\\eta_i)|}. $$\n\nThen the normalized gauging sums are finite, are invariant under replacement of a field by a gauge-isomorphic field, and satisfy\n\n$$ Z_K(W)= \\sum_{[s]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal S)} \\frac{Z_2(s)Z_1(s)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal S}(s)|}. $$\n\nThis is a finite groupoid theorem. A finite cocycle action is covered only when its evaluated weights obey the stated arrow-invariance and gluing-factorization hypotheses.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Gregory W. Moore and Vivek Saxena, TASI Lectures on Topological Field Theories and Differential Cohomology","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.07408","locator":"§§23.2–23.3, PDF pp. 92–99, for homotopy-pullback correspondences, finite pushforward, and the requirement to use homotopy cardinality for gluing."},{"title":"Daniel S. Freed and Frank Quinn, Chern-Simons Theory with Finite Gauge Group","url":"https://www.math.ru.nl/~mueger/TQFT/FQ.pdf","locator":"§2, PDF pp. 8–11, Lemma 2.4 and Theorem 2.13, for automorphism-weighted finite sums and the cocycle-weighted gluing law."},{"title":"Robbert Dijkgraaf and Edward Witten, Topological Gauge Theories and Group Cohomology","url":"https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/sns/%5B126%5DCommMathPhys129-1990.pdf","locator":"§§6.2 and 6.4–6.5, PDF pp. 22–30, for the finite 3D Dijkgraaf–Witten action and its ordered-triangulation conventions."}]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct","axiom_use":"No choice principle is used; all groupoids, morphism sets, and sums are finite."},{"id":"thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing","kind":"theorem","domain":"mathematics","title":"Finite 2-group homotopy gauging and cochain gluing","dependency_level":5,"deps":["def-aqft-background-gauging-data","def-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid","thm-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid-coherence"],"dependency_roles":{"def-aqft-background-gauging-data":"mathematical-premise","def-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid":"mathematical-premise","thm-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid-coherence":"mathematical-premise"},"provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Moore and Saxena, TASI Lectures on Topological Field Theories and Differential Cohomology","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.07408","locator":"§23.3 PDF97–100 / printed96–99, especially (23.34)–(23.38); alternating homotopy-group measure, not an explicit proof of the local cochain gluing formula."},{"title":"Baez and Lauda, Higher-Dimensional Algebra V: 2-Groups","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0307200","locator":"§8.3 pp.55–63; associator cocycle p.59 and monoidal coherence pp.60–61; explicit finite cochain 2-groupoid constructed locally."},{"title":"Benini, Cordova and Hsin, On 2-Group Global Symmetries and Their Anomalies","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.09336","locator":"§2.3, printed pp.18–20 / PDF19–21; background and gauge equations only, explicit coherence reconstructed locally."}]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct","reference":"items/thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing.md","statement":"Fix a finite group $G$, finite abelian group $A$, action $\\rho$, normalized $3$-cocycle $\\beta$, and a finite ordered simplicial complex $K$. Let $\\mathcal B(K)$ be the full background gauge $2$-groupoid, retaining its vertex $2$-gauge transformations, and let $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}\\mathcal B(K)\\to U(1)$ be constant on $1$-arrows. Let $V_K,E_K$ be the numbers of vertices and edges and define\n\n$$\nc(K)=\\frac{|A|^{V_K-E_K}}{|G|^{V_K}}.\n$$\n\nThen its higher gauging sum satisfies\n\n$$\nZ_K^{(2)}(W_K)=\\sum_{[x]}W_K(x)\\frac{|\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)|}{|\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)|}=c(K)\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{Ob}\\mathcal B(K)}W_K(x).\n$$\n\nFor any subcomplex $L$ and fixed field $s$ on $L$, the higher sum over the relative $2$-groupoid is\n\n$$\nZ_{K,L}^{(2)}(s)=\\frac{c(K)}{c(L)}\\sum_{x|_L=s}W_K(x).\n$$\n\nIn particular let $K=K_1\\cup_\\Sigma K_2$, where the intersection is the subcomplex $\\Sigma$ and all orders are induced. Assume invariant weights $W_i$ and $W_K$ obey $W_K(x)=W_2(x|_{K_2})W_1(x|_{K_1})$. Put $Z_i(s)=Z_{K_i,\\Sigma}^{(2)}(s)$. These boundary functions are invariant on $1$-arrows of $\\mathcal B(\\Sigma)$ and obey the actual finite $2$-group gluing law\n\n$$\nZ_K^{(2)}(W_K)=\\sum_{[s]\\in\\pi_0\\mathcal B(\\Sigma)}Z_2(s)Z_1(s)\\frac{|\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(\\Sigma),s)|}{|\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(\\Sigma),s)|}.\n$$\n\nThe empty complex has $c=1$ and one empty field. For a point and unit weight the sum is $|A|/|G|$. When $A$ is trivial these formulas reduce to ordinary finite flat $G$-connection normalization. The theorem assumes scalar invariant weights and their stated factorization; it does not assert that an arbitrary higher cocycle defines such boundary scalar data.","design_state":"Step 1 authored; owner round-two supplier/consumer repair complete, native readiness rehash remains owner-owned.","inventory_expansion_required":false}],"requires":["qftx-two-group-cochains"]}
