mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct","generation":{"role":"example"},"reference":"items/ex-aqft-two-group-point-higher-normalization.md","statement":"Let $K$ consist of one vertex. Take $G=\\mathbb Z_2$, $A=\\mathbb Z_3$, trivial action and $\\beta=0$, with unit phase weight. There is one background object, two distinct raw $1$-automorphisms and three $2$-automorphisms of each. The higher gauging sum is $3/2$. Identifying arrows by their effects on the singleton object set would give $1$; retaining $G$ but discarding $2$-arrows would give $1/2$.","design_state":"Step 1 authored; owner round-two supplier/consumer repair complete, native readiness rehash remains owner-owned.","inventory_expansion_required":false},{"id":"ex-aqft-split-two-group-interval-gluing","kind":"example","domain":"mathematics","title":"Split two-group interval gluing with a nontrivial action","dependency_level":6,"deps":["thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing"],"dependency_roles":{"thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing":"mathematical-premise"},"provenance":{"statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct","generation":{"role":"example"},"reference":"items/ex-aqft-split-two-group-interval-gluing.md","statement":"Let $G=\\mathbb Z_2$, $A=\\mathbb Z_3$, $\\rho_1(t)=-t$, and $\\beta=0$. Let $K$ be the ordered interval with vertices $0<1<2$ and edges $01,12$, decomposed into those two edges with intersection the vertex $1$. Give every field unit weight. Each edge and the full interval have higher gauging sum $3/2$. Each relative edge amplitude at the unique boundary field is $1$. The gluing contraction has boundary mass $3/2$ and gives $3/2$ again; discarding that second gauge stabilizer would change the contraction.","design_state":"Step 1 authored; owner round-two supplier/consumer repair complete, native readiness rehash remains owner-owned.","inventory_expansion_required":false},{"id":"ex-aqft-postnikov-two-group-tetrahedron-gauging","kind":"example","domain":"mathematics","title":"Nonzero Postnikov data retain higher gauge volume on a tetrahedron","dependency_level":6,"deps":["thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing"],"dependency_roles":{"thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing":"mathematical-premise"},"provenance":{"statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[]},"library":"mathematics","proof_strategy":"direct","generation":{"role":"example"},"reference":"items/ex-aqft-postnikov-two-group-tetrahedron-gauging.md","statement":"On the ordered tetrahedron $0<1<2<3$, take $G=A=\\mathbb Z_2$ with trivial action and $\\beta(g,h,k)=ghk$. This is a normalized $3$-cocycle of nonzero class. There are $64$ background objects, all gauge equivalent. For unit weight the higher sum is $1$, with $\\pi_1\\cong\\mathbb Z_2$ and $\\pi_2\\cong\\mathbb Z_2$ at any background. The nonzero Postnikov cocycle is retained in the gauge transformations and their associator.","design_state":"Step 1 authored; owner round-two supplier/consumer repair complete, native readiness rehash remains owner-owned.","inventory_expansion_required":false}],"requires":["qftx-finite-symmetry-gauging"]}
