    "thm-aqft-finite-two-group-gauging-gluing": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-aqft-background-gauging-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $K$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex. A **finite background field groupoid** $\\mathcal F(K)$ is a small category of declared fields and invertible gauge equivalences, with finitely many isomorphism classes and finite automorphism groups. Its automorphisms retain the multiplicities declared by the field model. A **cocycle/action weight** is a function $W_K:\\operatorname{Ob}\\mathcal F(K)\\to U(1)$ constant on every gauge arrow. Its normalized gauging sum is\n\n$$\nZ_K(W_K)=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0\\mathcal F(K)}\\frac{W_K(x)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal F(K)}(x)|}.\n$$\n\nFor the finite $2$-group specialization, fix finite $(G,A,\\rho,\\beta)$ and let $\\mathcal B(K)$ be the explicit background gauge $2$-groupoid: objects are flat $G$-edge cochains and $A$-face cochains satisfying $\\delta_aB=a^*\\beta$; $1$-arrows retain raw vertex/edge parameters $(g,\\lambda)$, and $2$-arrows are vertex cochains $t$ with $\\lambda'-\\lambda=\\delta_{a^g}t$, for fixed $g$. The raw $1$-composition is the displayed $\\tau$-corrected composition; its associator is $\\beta(k_i,h_i,g_i)$. Write $\\pi_0$ for object equivalence classes, $\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)$ for $2$-isomorphism classes of $1$-automorphisms, and $\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)$ for $2$-automorphisms of the identity $1$-arrow. For a phase weight $W_K$ constant on all $1$-arrows, define the higher normalized gauging sum\n\n$$\nZ^{(2)}_K(W_K)=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0\\mathcal B(K)}W_K(x)\\frac{|\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)|}{|\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)|}.\n$$\n\nDistinct raw gauge labels are not identified by equality of their effects on fields. The higher sum retains $\\pi_2$; it is not ordinary groupoid integration of the $1$-truncation. In particular a point has higher weight $|A|/|G|$ for unit action, whereas an ordinary field groupoid $BG$ has weight $1/|G|$.\n\nFor a subcomplex $L\\subseteq K$ and fixed field $s$ on $L$, relative fields and arrows use exact restriction to $s$, identity vertex gauge labels on $L$, zero edge gauge labels on $L$, and zero vertex $2$-gauge labels on $L$. Their higher sum uses the same $\\pi_2/\\pi_1$ prescription. Ordinary groupoids are included as $2$-groupoids with only identity $2$-arrows, so this prescription reduces to the preceding ordinary sum. A higher cocycle supplies an admissible scalar action only after its evaluated weight is proved invariant; nontrivial boundary line-bundle actions require their own transgression data and are not silently treated as scalar functions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite ordered simplicial complex $K$, a finite group $G$, a finite abelian group $A$ in additive notation, a left action $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$, and a normalized inhomogeneous $3$-cocycle $\\beta:G^3\\to A$. A background $x=(a,B)$ has flat edge labels $a_{ij}a_{jk}=a_{ik}$ and satisfies $\\delta_aB=a^*\\beta$, with coefficients at the first vertex. Here $(\\delta_a t)_{ij}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}t_j-t_i$ for a vertex cochain, $(\\delta_a\\lambda)_{ijk}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}\\lambda_{jk}-\\lambda_{ik}+\\lambda_{ij}$ for an edge cochain, and $(\\delta_aB)_{ijkl}=\\rho_{a_{ij}}B_{jkl}-B_{ikl}+B_{ijl}-B_{ijk}$. The cocycle convention is\n\n$$\nD(s,t,r,q):=\\rho_s\\beta(t,r,q)-\\beta(st,r,q)+\\beta(s,tr,q)-\\beta(s,t,rq)+\\beta(s,t,r)=0.\n$$\n\nThe **background gauge 2-groupoid** $\\mathcal B(K)$ has these backgrounds as objects. A $1$-arrow $f:x\\to y$ is a raw pair $(g,\\lambda)$ of vertex $G$-labels and edge $A$-labels with target $y=(b,C)$ given by\n\n$$\nb_{ij}=g_i a_{ij}g_j^{-1},\\qquad \\zeta^g_{ijk}(a)=-\\beta(g_i,a_{ij},a_{jk})+\\beta(b_{ij},g_j,a_{jk})-\\beta(b_{ij},b_{jk},g_k),\\qquad C=\\rho_gB+\\zeta^g(a)+\\delta_b\\lambda.\n$$\n\nRaw pairs remain distinct even when their induced functions on backgrounds coincide. For fixed $g$ and target edge labels $b$, a $2$-arrow $t:(g,\\lambda)\\Rightarrow(g,\\lambda')$ is a vertex $A$-cochain satisfying $\\lambda'-\\lambda=\\delta_b t$. There are no $2$-arrows between different vertex labels $g$. Vertical composition adds vertex cochains; the identity $2$-arrow is zero.\n\nFor consecutive $1$-arrows $(g,\\lambda):x\\to y$ and $(h,\\mu):y\\to z$, write $a,b,c$ for the successive edge labels. Their composite is $(hg,\\nu)$, where\n\n$$\n\\nu_{ij}=\\rho_{h_i}\\lambda_{ij}+\\mu_{ij}+\\tau(h,g;a)_{ij},\\qquad \\tau(h,g;a)_{ij}=\\beta(h_i,g_i,a_{ij})-\\beta(h_i,b_{ij},g_j)+\\beta(c_{ij},h_j,g_j).\n$$\n\nHorizontal composition of $t$ on the first arrow and $s$ on the second is $s+\\rho_h t$. Identity $1$-arrows have all vertex labels $e$ and edge labels zero. For three consecutive arrows $g,h,k$, the associator from $((k\\circ h)\\circ g)$ to $k\\circ(h\\circ g)$ is the vertex cochain $\\sigma_i=\\beta(k_i,h_i,g_i)$. Unit constraints are identity $2$-arrows.\n\nA $2$-groupoid here means these data form a bicategory: each pair of objects has a category of $1$-arrows and $2$-arrows; horizontal composition preserves identities and vertical composition; associators are natural and obey the pentagon; units obey the triangle identity; all $2$-arrows are invertible and every $1$-arrow has a reverse up to invertible $2$-arrows. Finiteness means all three sets of labels are finite. Coherence of the displayed data is a separate theorem.\n\nWrite $\\pi_0\\mathcal B(K)$ for the equivalence classes of objects under $1$-arrows. For an object $x$, let $\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)$ be the group of $2$-isomorphism classes of $1$-automorphisms of $x$, and let $\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)$ be the abelian group of $2$-automorphisms of its identity $1$-arrow. In particular, $\\pi_2$ retains vertex cochains with $\\delta_a t=0$ rather than discarding them. For a subcomplex $L\\subseteq K$ and fixed background $s$ on $L$, the **relative background 2-groupoid** has objects restricting exactly to $s$, $1$-arrows with $g=e$ on vertices of $L$ and $\\lambda=0$ on edges of $L$, and $2$-arrows with $t=0$ on vertices of $L$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-aqft-finite-background-two-groupoid-coherence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every finite ordered simplicial complex and finite data $(G,A,\\rho,\\beta)$ in the background gauge $2$-groupoid definition, its displayed operations define a finite $2$-groupoid, including the relative version for every subcomplex and fixed boundary field. Its raw $1$-arrows are not identified by their effects on background objects. For $x=(a,B)$,\n\n$$\n\\pi_2(\\mathcal B(K),x)=\\ker(\\delta_a:C^0(K;A)\\to C^1(K;A)).\n$$\n\nIf $S_x$ is the finite set of raw $1$-automorphisms at $x$, each $2$-isomorphism class in $S_x$ has $|\\operatorname{im}\\delta_a|$ elements, and\n\n$$\n|\\pi_1(\\mathcal B(K),x)|=|S_x|/|\\operatorname{im}\\delta_a|.\n$$\n\nThe same assertions hold relatively, using vertex and edge cochains vanishing on the subcomplex. On a single vertex, $\\pi_1=G$ and $\\pi_2=A$, with associator determined by $\\beta$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
