General Plan

This is a write-up based on lectures given by one of us at the Theoretical Advanced Study
Institute (TASI) 2023 summer school [TAS23]. The lectures are meant to be very elementary
introductions to two distinct (but closely related) subjects, both of which play an important
role in modern discussions of generalized notions of symmetry and topological effects in
quantum field theory, supergravity, and string/M-theory. Accordingly, the notes are divided
into two parts. The first part addresses topological field theory. This was the topic of the
first two TASI lectures. The videos are at [Moo23b, Moo23c]. The second part concerns
generalized Abelian gauge theories and differential cohomology. This was the topic of the
last two TASI lectures. The videos are at [Moo23d, Moo23e].
We comment here on some other pedagogical sources for this material. For the part on
topological field theory, some older reviews include [Tho91, BBRT91]. At some points, we
will closely follow the review [MS06] and the closely related chapter in [ABC+09]. Three
other useful reviews are [Bar05, DKR11, CR17, CDZR23]. A recent set of lectures, the
Simons Lectures On Categorical Symmetry [C+24], has much useful material.
For part two on differential cohomology, a key reference is the original paper by J. Cheeger
and J. Simons [CS85], which still makes for good reading. An important foundational paper
by M. Hopkins and I. Singer is [HS02]. Pedagogical material and reviews can be found
in [Fre00, FMS06a, FMS06b, Moo11, Sza12, CFLS19, Deb23].
There are two books on
the subject: [ADH21] and [BB14]. Some other references with summaries or alternative
viewpoints are [FN22, App. A], [Fre24, Sec. 4], [Bun12, Sch13, BNVar] and [HTY20, Sec.
2].
We supplement the text with several exercises for the reader. These exercises can be
found in gray bounded boxes labeled by the rather suggestive weightlifting icon (�). Nu-
merous important facts appear in red bounded boxes labeled by the book icon (�) – a green
signal for some reflection.

