Preliminary: Quantum Mechanics

Quantum mechanics is based on 
page /tmp/moore_saxena_2510_07408.pdf 8
page /tmp/moore_saxena_2510_07408.pdf 9
a pairing of states and observables known as the Born rule.
Physical states ρ are represented by traceclass positive operators ρ on a C-Hilbert space H
with trace tr(ρ) = 1. They are often referred to as density matrices. Physical observables O
are self-adjoint operators on H. The Born rule is a pairing of states and observables valued
in probability distributions on R. To define it, we need to recall the spectral theorem for
self-adjoint operators [Hal63, RS72, Rud90].
The spectral theorem asserts that for a self-adjoint operator O, there is an associated
projection-valued measure PO. If O has a discrete spectrum of eigenvalues {λi}, then for a
Borel-measurable set E ⊂R, we have

PO(E) =
