and, if we represent ρ by a nonzero vector ψ in Hilbert space, we have the evolution

ψ �→U(t)ψ .
(2.7)

In Euclidean signature, one “Wick-rotates” these operators to U(t) = e−tH, where t is real.
Topological field theory should be thought of as taking place in Euclidean signature, even
when there is no metric dependence and hence no choice of signature.
Because the action of time translation is linear in (2.7), quantum states can be super-
posed. The principle that physical states can be linearly combined is a fundamental aspect
of quantum information theory and leads to the distinctive quantum phenomenon of entan-
glement. On the other hand, quantum theory is projective, and projectivepage /tmp/moore_saxena_2510_07408.pdf 10
page /tmp/moore_saxena_2510_07408.pdf 11
page /tmp/moore_saxena_2510_07408.pdf 12 space is nonlinear,
so one might wonder how one describes linear superposition from the projective viewpoint.1

Given two pure states corresponding to linearly independent lines ℓ1, ℓ2 ⊂H, we obtain a
two-dimensional subspace S ⊂H spanned by the lines. A choice of linear superposition is
then simply a choice of a pure state corresponding to a line ℓin S. If we choose nonzero vec-
tors ψi ∈ℓi (for i = 1, 2), then the traditional textbook description of linear superposition
says that such a superposition “is” a vector z1ψ1 + z2ψ2, where zi ∈C. The same vector can
also be represented by �z1ψ1 + �z2ψ2 provided that zi = λ�zi for some λ ∈C×. Thus we recover
the set of linear combinations as points in CP1. Put another way, two linearly independent
pure states define points ℓ1, ℓ2 ∈P(H). Standard projective geometry tells us that two such
points determine a line CP1 ⊂P(H). This is the line of quantum superpositions. It is
also interesting to describe the linear combination of states in a choice-free way in terms
of rank-one projection operators. Suppose then that we are given two different rank-one
projection operators Pi, i = 1, 2. (Pi are the projectors onto the lines ℓi.) One then notes
that the equations
