Finally, an important aspect of quantum mechanics concerns how to “combine” two
physical systems described by operator algebras associated with two Hilbert spaces H1 and
H2. The “combined” system has Hilbert space H1 ⊗H2, and not H1 ⊕H2. This simple fact
will lead to many interesting aspects of topological field theory. More generally, the Hilbert
spaces of quantum mechanics should be super-Hilbert spaces, that is, they should be Z2-
graded. Then one uses the graded tensor product. The Z2-gradings are always present when
the physical system involves fermions, and conversely, a nontrivial Z2-grading is a hallmark
of the presence of fermions. In these lectures, for simplicity, we will generally restrict to the
ungraded case.

