Example: n = 2. See Figure 17.
Now we can change time-slicings by considering a path of smooth functions fs which
are excellent Morse functions for generic s. A crucial theorem of Cerf [Cer70] states that
in a natural topology12 on the set of infinitely differentiable real functions on Mn, i.e., on
C∞(Mn →R), the set of excellent Morse functions is open and dense, but disconnected.
Moreover, we can define a function f : Mn →R to be “good” if it is Morse everywhere
except for one or two critical points, and, (in the case that M is two-dimensional) one has:

• One critical point with a function locally of the form ±y2 + x3,

• Two critical points with the same value.

Then, a remarkable result [Cer68, Cer70] states that the set of excellent and good functions
is a connected set. The good but not excellent functions form a real codimension-one subset.

12The topology is known as the Whitney topology. See [Fre12a, Ch. 20].

