Sums And Products Of Theories

Products Of Theories. A familiar notion from Lagrangian field theory is that if one has two
sets of fields Φ1 and Φ2 with actions S[Φ1] and S[Φ2], one can define a new theory with
fields Φ1, Φ2 and exponentiated action:

e−S[Φ1]−S[Φ2] .
(9.1)

The amplitudes will, rather obviously, be products of the amplitudes from the constituent
theories. (Of course, this will not be true if we deform (9.1) by adding interaction terms
between Φ1 and Φ2.) This procedure defines a “multiplication” of Lagrangian theories. It
is also sometimes referred to as stacking theories. The procedure naturally generalizes to a
notion of a product of field theories. In the case of topological field theories, F1 and F2, we
can define F1 ⊗F2 by taking the tensor products of the statespaces and amplitudes in the
obvious way.
An important consequence of the notion of multiplication is the notion of an invertible
field theory. We define the trivial field theory F to have statespace F(N) = C on all spatial
manifolds and F(M) : C →C is the identity map on all bordisms M. (In particular F(N)
is a trivial representation of the diffeomorphism group of N.) We say F is invertible if
there is another field theory G such that F ⊗G is the trivial field theory. Note that G
inverts F. An immediate consequence is that for an invertible field theory, F(N) is a one-
dimensional vector space on any spatial manifold N, and F(M) is always invertible, hence
can be expressed as multiplication of C by a nonzero complex number, for any bordism M.
A natural source of invertible theories is given by classical actions of Lagrangian field
theories. These are not in general topological field theories, so our bordisms will be endowed
with field configurations we denote as Φ.
We can take the Hilbert space associated to
spatial manifolds to be C. A bordism Mn : N 0
n−1 →N 1
n−1 will be endowed with fields Φ
and the amplitude involves multiplication by the exponentiated action e−S[Φ]. Local actions
will satisfy the crucial locality condition (LOC 2). Nonlocal actions will not. Invertible
topological field theories are therefore local topological terms in field theory actions. It was
exactly in this context that the notion of invertible field theory was introduced in [FM04]. A
rather thorough classification of invertible theories has been described by Freed and Hopkins
[FH16].
Although classical actions furnish natural examples of invertible field theories, not all
invertible theories are of this type. A simple example is the exponentiated eta invariant of
the Dirac operator, which satisfies the locality properties of an invertible theory [DF94]. For
further discussion, see [Wit99a, Wit15b, WY19].

Sums of theories. A more subtle notion is the sum of field theories F1 and F2. If Nn−1 is a
connected spatial manifold, we define:

(F1 + F2)(Nn−1) := F1(Nn−1) ⊕F2(Nn−1) .
(9.2)

