Sums of theories naturally arise when describing long-distance limits, or IR limits of theo-
ries. Thus they appear naturally in discussions of spontaneous symmetry breaking. Another
example is in long-distance limits of renormalization group (RG) flow as in [HKMM01, Fig.
1]. The concept has also been useful in elucidating the long-distance behavior of 2d adjoint
QCD [KORS20]. Finally, we mention that the notion of sums of theories is closely related to
the subject of “decomposition” of theories, especially in the examples of theories admitting
a non-faithful action of a (generalized) symmetry group. For a sampling of the literature
on this, see [PS05, HHP+06, CDH+07, GS18, Sha19, TU19, ES20, KKSS22, Sha22, Sha23e,
Sha23g, SS24].

