13
Finite Group Gauge Theory

Let G be a finite group.
In general, the functor defining finite group gauge theory for
gauge group G is defined by “quantizing” in some way the groupoid of principal G-bundles
over X, denoted BunG(X), and introduced in Section 12. Here X is a smooth manifold,
of a dimension that depends on the physical quantity under consideration. The fact that
BunG(X) is a groupoid is very important because some principal G-bundles page /tmp/moore_saxena_2510_07408.pdf 53
have nontrivial
automorphisms, and we should “quotient” by that group of automorphisms in a gauge theory.
We can illustrate the general outlook of the previous paragraph by first writing the
formula for the partition function. This will be a sum over the set of isomorphism classes
