23A space X is weakly contractible if all its homotopy groups are trivial, i.e., πi(X) = {1} for all i. This is
equivalent to the statement that the canonical map X →pt is a weak homotopy equivalence. The space
X is contractible if the map X →pt is a homotopy equivalence. This technical distinction is important in
situations where the Whitehead theorem (introduced in footnote 40) may not apply. By the Whitehead
theorem, a weakly contractible CW complex is contractible. Two examples of spaces that are weakly
contractible but not contractible are the so-called long line, and the double comb space. Both these spaces
do not have the homotopy type of a CW complex. For discussions of such examples, see for example,
[SS78].
24Note that, if a space X satisfies properties (a) and (b), then so does any finite product of X with itself.
