A fourth way comes from thinking about the proper categorical interpretation of the
fundamental group: Let X be a topological space. We form a category whose objects are the
points of X. The morphisms x1 →x2 are paths in X, γ : x1 →x2 considered up to homotopy
with fixed endpoints: Hom(x1, x2) = P(x1, x2)/homotopy. Then π1(X, x0) = Hom(x0, x0) :=
Aut(x0). This (important) category is called the fundamental groupoid π≤1(X). But we
could make a more elaborate object if we decline to consider paths only up to homotopy.
We could consider π≤2(X) where 1-morphisms between objects (x1 →x2) P(x1, x2) and
“2-morphisms” are homotopies of paths. And so on, 
