Given the above heuristic ideas, one can give technically precise definitions of higher
categories. The simplest one uses an inductive procedure:

Definition 19.1 (Strict n-category). A strict n-category C is a collection of objects C0(C)
(the “0-morphisms”) and for each pair of objects x, y ∈C0(C), an (n−1)-category Hom(x, y),
such that, for all x, y, z ∈C0(C), there exist (strict) (n −1)-bifunctors:

cx,y,z : Hom(x, y) × Hom(y, z) →Hom(x, z) ,
(19.3)

which are (strictly) associative.26

If we understand a “0-category” to be a set, then a 1-category as defined in Section 8
satisfies (8.3), and is hence a strict 1-category. We can then proceed to define n-categories in-
ductively using the above definition once we agree that a strict functor between n-categories
C and D is a map of objects ϕ0 : C0 →D0, such that for all x, y ∈C0,

ϕ1 : Hom(x, y) →Hom(ϕ0(x), ϕ0(y)) ,
(19.4)

which is a (strict) (n −1)-functor.

