2. The adjective “strict” used above refers to the associativity of the compositions. It
means that all the many associativity relations between k-morphisms hold as equalities.
This notion turns out to have limited utility. One should relax the strict associativity
conditions to allow for suitable equivalences between different orders of compositions

(much like one allows for an associator in a tensor category). When this is done, much
new data and many new conditions are introduced. The subject of higher categories
can get quite technical. For a sampling of the literature, see the works of Barwick et
al. [BDG+16], Bergner [Ber06], Lurie’s kerodon.net [Lur25] and [Lur09b], Schommer-
Pries [SPar], Rezk [Rez00], Riehl et al. [Rie17, Rie14, RV22] and references in the
surveys by Freed [Fre12b, Fre19], and Teleman [Tel16].

3. It makes sense to take n = ∞in the definition of an n-category. There is then a useful
refinement of the notion of a higher category to a (p, q) category:

All k-morphisms with k > p are the identity.

All k-morphisms with k > q are invertible.

In this notation, a (0, 0) category is a set. A (1, 1) category is a category in the normal
sense. One commonly encounters the term “(∞, n)-category”. So it is an ∞-category
where all k-morphisms for k > n are invertible. A good example is π∞(X), which is
an (∞, 0)-category. An important notion when discussing the cobordism hypothesis
below is the idea of truncating to invertible morphisms. If C is an (∞, n)-category
and k < n then Gk(C) is the (∞, k) category where we simply delete the noninvertible
ℓ-morphisms with ℓ> k.

Example 1: A good example of a 2-category is the category C = ALG(VECT). This is the

