metric on M2 defines a complex structure.) Another way identifies it as the Arf invariant
of the quadratic function q : H1(M2; Z2) →Z2, where q on a circle is 0 or 1, according
to whether the circle inherits bounding or nonbounding spin structure from s, respectively.
The Hilbert space on the circle with bounding spin structure is the super-Hilbert space C1|0,
and on the circle with non-bounding spin structure is the super-Hilbert space C0|1. For
much more information about this theory, see [Gun12, DG18, KTT19].30

20.2
Freed-Hopkins-Teleman Definition Of A Field

In all the above examples we have encountered various fields. These include traditional
fields like the scalar field ξ : Mn →X and the Riemannian metric gMn in Example 1, but,
as has been argued by D. Freed, M. Hopkins and C. Teleman, in [FT12, FH13] that, for
example, the orientation o of Example 2, and the spin structure s of Example 3 should
equally be considered to be “fields.” Moreover, the parameters like I and B in Example 2
could also be rendered spacetime dependent – an old idea known as the Janus construction
– and these two should be considered to be “fields.” Usually, they are constrained to be
constant as functions of spacetime. Quite generally, given any symmetry, one should specify
background fields associated with that symmetry. For example, if a theory has a global
symmetry G, then it is natural to make the theory equivariant by defining the theory
coupled to a principal G-bundle with connection over spacetime.
Some general locality properties we wish our fields to satisfy are:

1. They should pull back under smooth maps. In order to incorporate nondegenerate

