This is not enough data to capture the two inequivalent double covers of the circle. If on
the other hand, the target space of the field is the groupoid of principal Z2-bundles, then
associated to D± is a category with one object but two invertible morphisms between the
objects: The identity map of the double cover and the deck transformation. Now on D±, we
can compare these invertible morphisms with a 2-morphism and recover the distinct double
covers of the circle.
Moving on to gauge theories, for a topological group G, we can define a field F that
assigns to an n-manifold Mn the groupoid PG(Mn) of principal G-bundles over Mn. In order
to fit the FHT definition of a field, we should take F to be the nerve of that category so we
take:
F(Mn) = N(PG(Mn)) .
(20.10)

This will suffice as the definition of a field in G-gauge theory for a finite group G, since
principal G-bundles come with a unique connection. In general, for G-gauge theory, we
wish to include the connection as one of the fields. Therefore, we consider P ∇
G (Mn), the
groupoid of principal G-bundles over Mn equipped with connection. So, in general the field
of a gauge theory in the FHT definition is:

