The most
primitive level of locality says that we can “insert a complete set of states along a spatial
slice,” if we cut an n-dimensional spacetime into “past” and “future” parts by cutting along
an (n −1)-dimensional spatial slice. Now, we can consider the space of states on an (n −
1)-dimensional spatial slice and divide that into two pieces by cutting along an (n −2)-
dimensional manifold, as in the discussion around (19.2) above. And so on. This is the
reason for the terminology fully local used above.
One of the main results in the study of extended TFTs is the proof of the “cobordism
hypothesis.”
The “cobordism hypothesis” is an idea going back to J. Baez and J. Dolan
[BD95]. It states, very roughly, that a fully extended TFT is “completely determined by its
value on a point”. Recall that F(pt) ∈Obj(C) is a 0-morphism in an n-category. A good
example is the case of one dimensional oriented TFT where all amplitudes are determined
by a single piece of data: F(pt+) = V . A precise version of the cobordism hypothesis was
stated and proved by J. Lurie [Lur09c] generalizing some important unpublished work of
M. Hopkins and J. Lurie. Here is a very brief sketch of that result. We follow here the
expositions [Fre12b, Tel16, Fre19].
An important part of the cobordism hypothesis is to study all the fully extended theories
at once, so we should study the category of theories with fixed dimension n, background
fields F, and codomain C. Thus, we consider the category of theories, which we denote as

