Dualizability has a generalization to symmetric monoidal (∞, n)-categories known as full
dualizability. The basic idea is that we demand that the dualizability data itself is dualizable
in the appropriate category. More technically, recall that for an (∞, n)-category C, we let
Gk(C) be the (∞, k)-category where we delete the noninvertible ℓ-morphisms with ℓ> k.
Then, if C is a symmetric monoidal (∞, n)-category an object x (“0-morphisms”) of C is said
to be fully dualizable if:

1. x is dualizable in G1(C).

2. The evaluation and coevaluation morphisms e, c of x are dualizable in G2(C).

3. And so forth up to, but not including, the n-morphisms.

A key observation is that the image of a TFT only contains fully-dualizable objects. This
follows from the same style of argument we used in the S-diagram of Figure 11 above, to
show that statespaces in a TQFT are finite-dimensional. Indeed, “fully dualizable” is just a
generalization of this finite-dimensional property. Thus, the field theory functor factorizes
through a functor to the subcategory Cfd of fully dualizable objects and morphisms.
