nsity statement= 'Specialize the adopted model to the trivial bundle\n$P=\\mathbb R^{1,3}\\times SU(2)$ with its product section. Let $T_1,T_2,T_3$\nbe the normalized basis of the model, so $[T_1,T_2]=T_3$ and\n$\\kappa(T_3,T_3)=1$. For a constant $a\\ne0$ with unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, take\nthe smooth connection potential\n$A=a(T_1\\,dx^1+T_2\\,dx^2)$. Its curvature is\n$F=a^2T_3\\,dx^1\\wedge dx^2$, and its Lorentzian action density is\n$-\\frac{a^4}{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}\\,\ndx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$. This is a field configuration;\nno stationarity or field-equation claim is made.' design_state= 'Step 1 authoring and scoped checks complete; readiness is recorded in the native per-item Step 1 record.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-19.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-pure-gauge-yang-mills-action statement= 'Specialize the adopted model to the trivial bundle\n$P=\\mathbb R^{1,3}\\times SU(2)$ with canonical section $s$. Let\n$T_3=-i\\sigma_3/2$ and choose a length $\\ell>0$. Change section to\n$s^u(x)=s(x)u(x)$ with\n$u(x)=\\exp(T_3\\sin(x^0/\\ell))$. The connection whose potential in $s$ is\n$A=0$ has potential in $s^u$\n$A^u=u^{-1}du=T_3\\ell^{-1}\\cos(x^0/\\ell)\\,dx^0$, which is nonzero. Its\nCartan curvature and Yang–Mills action density are zero. This is a change\nof gauge description of a classical connection, not a distinct physical\nobservation.' design_state= 'Step 1 authoring and scoped checks complete; readiness is recorded in the native per-item Step 1 record.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-19.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-null-wave-yang-mills-solution statement= 'Specialize the adopted model to the trivial bundle\n$P=\\mathbb R^{1,3}\\times SU(2)$ with its canonical section. Let\n$q=x^0-x^3$, choose a length $\\ell>0$ and a nonzero constant $b$ with unit\n$\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and set\n$A=T_3 b\\sin(q/\\ell)\\,dx^1$. Its curvature is\n$F=T_3(b/\\ell)\\cos(q/\\ell)(dx^0-dx^3)\\wedge dx^1$, which is not identically\nzero. With orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$ and metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, this connection satisfies\n$D_A(*_\\eta F)=0$ and $\\kappa(F\\wedge *_\\eta F)=0$. It is therefore a\nstationary source-free solution of the adopted classical model; no\nuniqueness, quantum, or empirical claim is made.' design_state= 'Step 1 authoring and scoped checks complete; readiness is recorded in the native per-item Step 1 record.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data statement= 'Assume the axiom of countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. A **non-Abelian gauge datum on $M$** consists of the following specified objects.\n\nFirst choose a finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$ that is compact in its manifold topology (every open cover has a finite subcover) and noncommutative (there are $g,h\\in G$ with $gh\\ne hg$). The actual group $G$, including its global form, is part of the datum. Its Lie algebra is $\\mathfrak g=\\operatorname{Lie}(G)=T_eG$ with the bracket transported from left-invariant smooth vector fields.\n\nChoose a smooth right principal $G$-bundle $\\pi:P\\to M$: the projection and right action are smooth, the action is free and transitive on each fibre, and there are local trivializations $\\pi^{-1}(U)\\cong U\\times G$ that are equivariant diffeomorphisms over each open $U\\subseteq M$.\n\nChoose a specified set $I$ of representation labels and, for each $a\\in I$, a finite-dimensional complex vector space $V_a$, a positive-definite Hermitian form $h_a$, and a smooth group homomorphism $\\rho_a:G\\to U(V_a,h_a)$. Here $U(V_a,h_a)$ is the group of complex-linear isometries of $h_a$, and a representation is the corresponding finite-dimensional group action. Its associated vector bundle is the quotient $E_a=P\\times_{\\rho_a}V_a$ with relation $[pg,v]=[p,\\rho_a(g)v]$.\n\nTwo smooth right principal $G$-bundles over $M$ are isomorphic over $M$ when a smooth diffeomorphism between their total spaces covers $\\operatorname{id}_M$ and commutes with the right $G$-actions. A **bundle sector** is one equivalence class under this relation. The datum specifies a set $\\Sigma$ of admitted bundle sectors and requires $[P]\\in\\Sigma$; a model may take $\\Sigma=\\{[P]\\}$. The Lie algebra alone does not replace the chosen group or sector. This definition does not choose a connection, an action functional, or a quantum theory.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json def-qftx-principal-connection-form statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, and let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle for a finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, with $\\mathfrak g=\\operatorname{Lie}(G)=T_eG$. A supplied principal connection is a smooth, right-invariant complement $TP=VP\\oplus H$, where $VP=\\ker d\\pi$; write $v:TP\\to VP$ for the projection along $H$, and let $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$ be its vertical-valued curvature. For $Z\\in\\mathfrak g$ and $p\\in P$, define the fundamental vertical vector $Z_P(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}p\\exp_G(tZ)$. The orbit differential $\\zeta_p: \\mathfrak g\\to V_pP$, $Z\\mapsto Z_P(p)$, is a linear isomorphism, as proved below. Define the **principal connection 1-form** by\n\n$$\\omega_p(X):=\\zeta_p^{-1}(v_pX),\\qquad X\\in T_pP.$$\n\nThen $\\omega$ is a smooth $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form satisfying $\\omega(Z_P)=Z$, $\\ker\\omega=H$, and $R_g^*\\omega=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\\omega$ for every fixed $g\\in G$. Conversely, a smooth $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form with the reproduction and equivariance properties determines the principal connection $H=\\ker\\omega$. The converted vertical curvature $\\kappa_H:=\\omega\\circ K_H$ is a $\\mathfrak g$-valued alternating 2-form; its equality with the Cartan curvature is established in the curvature lemma.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket statement= 'Let $M$ be a smooth manifold and $\\mathfrak g$ a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra. For homogeneous $\\alpha\\in\\Omega^p(M,\\mathfrak g)$ and $\\beta\\in\\Omega^q(M,\\mathfrak g)$, define their **graded bracket** by\n\n$$[\\alpha\\wedge\\beta](X_1,\\ldots,X_{p+q}) := \\frac{1}{p!q!}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_{p+q}}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)[\\alpha(X_{\\sigma(1)},\\ldots,X_{\\sigma(p)}),\\beta(X_{\\sigma(p+1)},\\ldots,X_{\\sigma(p+q)})].$$\n\nHere $\\Omega^p(M,\\mathfrak g)$ is the space of smooth alternating $p$-linear forms with values in $\\mathfrak g$, the inner bracket is the Lie-algebra bracket, and the sum is over all permutations. This is a smooth $\\mathfrak g$-valued $(p+q)$-form. It obeys graded skew-symmetry $[\\alpha\\wedge\\beta]=-(-1)^{pq}[\\beta\\wedge\\alpha]$, graded Jacobi\n\n$$\\left[\\alpha\\wedge\\left[\\beta\\wedge\\gamma\\right]\\right]=\\left[\\left[\\alpha\\wedge\\beta\\right]\\wedge\\gamma\\right]+(-1)^{pq}\\left[\\beta\\wedge\\left[\\alpha\\wedge\\gamma\\right]\\right],$$\n\nand the exterior-derivative rule\n\n$$d[\\alpha\\wedge\\beta]=[d\\alpha\\wedge\\beta]+(-1)^p[\\alpha\\wedge d\\beta].$$\n\nIn particular, for $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-forms $A,B$, $[A\\wedge B](X,Y)=[A(X),B(Y)]-[A(Y),B(X)]$, so $\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A](X,Y)=[A(X),A(Y)]$. This fixes the normalization used by the Cartan curvature formula.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, and let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle with a principal connection form $\\omega$ associated to $H$, using $Z_P(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_0p\\exp_G(tZ)$ for the right action. For Lie-algebra-valued forms use the graded bracket defined by alternating shuffle and the Lie bracket; in degree one, $[A\\wedge B](X,Y)=[A(X),B(Y)]-[A(Y),B(X)]$. Define\n\n$$\\Omega_\\omega:=d\\omega+\\frac12[\\omega\\wedge\\omega],\\qquad D_\\omega\\eta:=d\\eta+[\\omega\\wedge\\eta].$$\n\nThen $\\Omega_\\omega$ is horizontal and $\\operatorname{Ad}$-equivariant, and $\\Omega_\\omega=\\omega\\circ K_H$ for the imported vertical-valued curvature $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$. For any trivializing cover with smooth sections $s_i:U_i\\to P$, set $A_i=s_i^*\\omega$ and $F_i=s_i^*\\Omega_\\omega$. On an overlap, let $g_{ij}:U_i\\cap U_j\\to G$ be the smooth map determined by $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, and write $\\theta_L(g_{ij})=g_{ij}^*\\theta_L$ for the left Maurer–Cartan form. Then\n\n$$A_j=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+\\theta_L(g_{ij}),\\qquad F_i=dA_i+\\frac12[A_i\\wedge A_i],\\qquad F_j=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}F_i,\\qquad D_{A_i}F_i=0,$$\n\nwhere $D_{A_i}\\eta:=d\\eta+[A_i\\wedge\\eta]$. The transition equations concern changes of local sections; they do not identify an arbitrary map $M\\to G$ with a global automorphism of a nontrivial bundle.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, $G$ a finite-dimensional real Lie group, $\\pi:P\\to M$ a smooth right principal $G$-bundle, and $\\omega$ the principal connection 1-form of a supplied horizontal distribution. Fix a finite-dimensional complex representation $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{GL}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$ that is smooth, and let $E=P\\times_\\rho V$ be its associated vector bundle with relation $[pg,v]=[p,\\rho(g)v]$. Define $\\rho_*:=d\\rho_e:\\mathfrak g\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, where the differential is taken after viewing $\\rho$ as a smooth matrix-valued map. For any open cover with smooth local sections $s_i:U_i\\to P$, write $A_i=s_i^*\\omega$. A smooth section $\\sigma$ of $E$ has unique local components $\\psi_i:U_i\\to V$ given by $\\sigma(x)=[s_i(x),\\psi_i(x)]$. Define the **associated covariant derivative** $\\nabla^\\omega\\sigma$ by its local components\n\n$$\n(\\nabla^\\omega\\sigma)_i=d\\psi_i+\\rho_*(A_i)\\psi_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $(\\rho_*(A_i)\\psi_i)(X)=\\rho_*(A_i(X))\\psi_i$ for $X\\in TU_i$. These expressions patch to a smooth $E$-valued 1-form on $M$, independently of the chosen local sections, and satisfy $\\nabla^\\omega(f\\sigma)=df\\otimes\\sigma+f\\nabla^\\omega\\sigma$ for every smooth scalar function $f$ on $M$.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-flat-pure-gauge-connection statement= 'Let $M=\\mathbb R^2$ with coordinates $(x,y)$, let $G=SU(2)$, and let $P=M\\times G$ have the right action $(z,g)h=(z,gh)$. Use the defining representation on $V=\\mathbb C^2$ with its standard positive-definite Hermitian form $h(v,w)=v^*w$, let $E=P\\times_G\\mathbb C^2$, and take the admitted sector set to be $\\Sigma=\\{[P]\\}$. Set\n\n$$T_3=-\\frac{i}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad u(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-ix/2}&0\\\\0&e^{ix/2}\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad A=u^{-1}du=T_3\\,dx.$$\n\nEquip $P$ with the principal form\n\n$$\n\\omega_{(z,g)}(X,\\xi)=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}A_z(X)+(\\theta_L)_g(\\xi),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta_L$ is the left Maurer–Cartan form. This is a smooth principal connection form with local potential $A$ along the canonical section $s(z)=(z,e)$. Its Cartan curvature $F=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$ vanishes identically although $A$ is nonzero; in this sense $A=u^{-1}du$ is a nonzero pure-gauge potential on the trivial bundle.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-2.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-noncommuting-curvature statement= 'Let $M=\\mathbb R^2$ with coordinates $(x,y)$, let $G=SU(2)$, let $P=M\\times G$ carry the right action $(z,g)h=(z,gh)$, and take the admitted sector set to be $\\Sigma=\\{[P]\\}$. Use the defining representation $\\rho:SU(2)\\to U(\\mathbb C^2,h)$ for the standard positive-definite Hermitian form $h(v,w)=v^*w$. With the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, set $T_a=-\\tfrac i2\\sigma_a\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and $A=T_1dx+T_2dy$. The form\n\n$$\n\\omega_{(z,g)}(X,\\xi)=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}A_z(X)+(\\theta_L)_g(\\xi)\n$$\n\nis a principal connection form whose canonical-section potential is $A$. Its curvature is $F=T_3\\,dx\\wedge dy$. On the associated bundle with fibre $\\mathbb C^2$, every smooth section represented by $\\psi:M\\to\\mathbb C^2$ has $\\nabla_{\\partial_x}\\psi=(\\partial_x+T_1)\\psi$ and $\\nabla_{\\partial_y}\\psi=(\\partial_y+T_2)\\psi$; the commutator operator is $[\\nabla_{\\partial_x},\\nabla_{\\partial_y}]=T_3$. Thus the exterior-derivative part of the curvature vanishes, while its nonzero value is the Lie-bracket contribution.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-20.pages.json def-qftx-formal-perturbative-yang-mills-data statement= 'Use the adopted pure $SU(2)$ Yang–Mills model: $G=SU(2)$, $\\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak{su}(2)$, the selected smooth right-principal bundle sector has no matter fields, and the positive invariant form is $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ with $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$ for $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, extended bilinearly to the graded coefficient algebra. Work in a nonempty local trivialization chart of the selected bundle over oriented, time-oriented Minkowski space with signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and coordinates $x^0=t$ in natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. With the alternating wedge–Lie bracket fixed by the principal-curvature supplier, write $F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$. The adopted Lorentzian gauge action is $S_0[A]=-(2g_{\\rm YM}^2)^{-1}\\int\\kappa(F_A\\wedge *F_A)$, with fixed positive dimensionless $g_{\\rm YM}$. In the perturbative formulas $A_\\mu$ has canonical engineering dimension $1$ and $g_{\\rm YM}$ dimension $0$ in mass units; the ghost $c$ has dimension $0$, the antighost $\\bar c$ and auxiliary field $b$ have dimension $2$, and the BRST differential has dimension $0$. Assign each antifield the dimension making its product with the corresponding BRST variation have density dimension $4$. The model postulate supplies the physical units, bundle sector, action, and boundary conditions; the classical gauge-invariance result supplies invariance under the declared bundle gauge transformations. This definition does not prove either supplier.\n\nIn the chosen local trivialization write $D_\\mu c=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$ and choose the linear covariant gauge condition $G(A)=\\partial^\\mu A_\\mu=0$ with gauge parameter $\\xi>0$. The nonminimal BRST variables are an odd antighost $\\bar c$ of ghost number $-1$ and an even auxiliary field $b$ of ghost number $0$, with $s\\bar c=b$ and $sb=0$. The gauge-fixing fermion and gauge-fixing contribution are\n\n$$\\Psi_\\xi=\\int d^4x\\,\\kappa(\\bar c,\\partial^\\mu A_\\mu+\\tfrac{\\xi}{2}b),\\qquad S_{\\rm gf}:=s\\Psi_\\xi.$$\n\nHere $s$ is the odd BRST derivation on the finite-jet field algebra, extended to the nonminimal variables and their antifields using the specified BV convention. The gauge condition is a local perturbative choice; it does not assert a global slice or remove all gauge copies. A finite-dimensional regulated configuration space has an ordinary Faddeev–Popov Jacobian only when it supplies the finite-dimensional group action, gauge condition, and transverse slice data in the preceding finite-dimensional definition. Such a Jacobian is not a continuum functional determinant and does not define a continuum measure.\n\nA **formal perturbative renormalization datum** consists of the selected pure SU(2) classical BV action $\\Gamma_0$, the above gauge-fixing choice, the dimensional-regularization prescription $D=4-2\\epsilon$, modified minimal subtraction of the $1/\\epsilon$ poles together with $-\\gamma_E+\\log(4\\pi)$, and a local counterterm space $\\mathcal C_{\\leq d_*}$. Here $d_*$ is a finite engineering-dimension bound fixed by the selected power-counting prescription; $\\mathcal C_{\\leq d_*}$ consists of integrated Lorentz-scalar polynomials of ghost number zero and engineering dimension at most $d_*$ in $A,c,\\bar c,b$, their antifields, and finitely many spacetime derivatives. The usual renormalizable four-dimensional Yang–Mills choice is $d_*=4$. The minimal BV action is the extension of $S_0$ by the antifield couplings to $sA_\\mu=D_\\mu c$ and $sc=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$; when the nonminimal pair is included it also has the term $-\\int_U\\bar c^*b$. The datum is admissible only when $\\Gamma_0$ satisfies the classical master equation and the chosen local BV functional domain has no nonzero central element of ghost number one. Its loop-counting variable $\\lambda$ is formal and dimensionless; a formal effective action is a coefficient sequence $\\Gamma(\\lambda)=\\Gamma_0+\\sum_{n\\geq1}\\lambda^n\\Gamma_n$ and no convergence at any nonzero $\\lambda$ is asserted.\n\nFor a local BV functional $\\Gamma$, define the Slavnov functional by $\\mathfrak S(\\Gamma):=\\tfrac12(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)$ using the chosen odd antibracket, and define the linearized operator $B_{\\Gamma_0}X:=(\\Gamma_0,X)$. The datum has the **local QAP property** when, at each first order $n$ where the coefficientwise Slavnov identity fails, its defect has a finite-jet local four-form representative $\\delta_n$ of ghost number one and a finite-jet local three-form $\\rho_n$ of ghost number two satisfying\n\n$$\\mathfrak S(\\Gamma^{(<n)})=\\lambda^n\\int_U\\delta_n+O(\\lambda^{n+1}),\\qquad B_{\\Gamma_0}\\delta_n+d_H\\rho_n=0.$$\n\nThis local representative and consistency condition are part of the QAP input; take $\\delta_n$ to have engineering dimension at most $d_*$ and $\\rho_n$ at most $d_*-1$. The definition does not prove the QAP. The finite-jet local BRST cohomology $H^{g,p}(s\\mid d_H)$ uses the local forms, ghost grading, and horizontal differential of the preceding cohomology definition; $d_H$-exact top forms are identified with zero only under the declared compact-support or boundary condition. The coefficientwise Slavnov–Taylor identity is $\\mathfrak S(\\Gamma(\\lambda))=0$ as an equality of formal power series.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-20.pages.json pthm-qftx-perturbative-yang-mills-st-identity statement= "In the axiom base inherited from the adopted model, fix the classical pure $SU(2)$ Yang–Mills model on oriented, time-oriented Minkowski spacetime $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, its selected smooth right-principal bundle sector $P\\to M$, no matter fields, invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ on $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$, and fixed $g_{\\rm YM}>0$. Let $U\\subset M$ be a nonempty local trivialization chart, use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, and use the right-action curvature convention $F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$. The adopted local classical action density is $-\\kappa(F_A\\wedge *_\\eta F_A)/(2g_{\\rm YM}^2)$. The local gauge potential $A_\\mu$ is even with ghost number $0$ and engineering dimension $1$; the odd ghost $c$ has ghost number $1$ and dimension $0$. Define $D_\\mu c=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$ and the minimal BRST transformations $sA_\\mu=D_\\mu c$, $sc=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$. When the nonminimal pair is included, let $\\bar c$ be odd with ghost number $-1$ and dimension $2$, and $b$ even with ghost number $0$ and dimension $2$, with $s\\bar c=b$ and $sb=0$. Antifields have the opposite field parity and ghost number $-1-\\operatorname{gh}(\\Phi)$, with engineering dimensions chosen so $\\Phi^*s\\Phi$ has density dimension $4$.\n\nUse the canonical BV antibracket and let the classical BV functional be the pure-gauge extension\n$$\\Gamma_0=S_0+\\int_U d^4x\\left(A^{*\\mu}_a(D_\\mu c)^a+\\tfrac12c^*_a[c,c]^a-\\bar c^*_ab\\right),$$\nwhere the nonminimal term is omitted if that pair is not included, and $S_0$ denotes the adopted classical action on the chosen domain. Assume $\\Gamma_0$ is even, has ghost number zero, satisfies $\\tfrac12(\\Gamma_0,\\Gamma_0)=0$, and the chosen local BV functional domain has no nonzero central element of ghost number one. The model postulate and its classical gauge-invariance theorem are explicit inputs fixing and interpreting $S_0$; neither is proved by this theorem.\n\nChoose the local Lorenz gauge $\\partial^\\mu A_\\mu=0$ with constant $\\xi>0$, dimensional regularization $D=4-2\\epsilon$, modified minimal subtraction of $1/\\epsilon$ poles together with $-\\gamma_E+\\log(4\\pi)$, and a finite engineering-dimension bound $d_*$. The allowed counterterms are integrated Lorentz-scalar finite-jet polynomials of ghost number zero and dimension at most $d_*$ in $A,c,\\bar c,b$, their antifields, and their spacetime derivatives. The formal loop variable $\\lambda$ is dimensionless; $\\Gamma(\\lambda)=\\Gamma_0+\\sum_{n\\geq1}\\lambda^n\\Gamma_n$ is a formal coefficient sequence with no convergence at nonzero $\\lambda$ asserted. Write $\\Gamma^{(<n)}$ for a partial action whose coefficients below order $n$ already satisfy the Slavnov identity.\n\nAssume the local quantum action principle (QAP): at each first failing order $n$ there are a local ghost-number-one four-form $\\delta_n$ of engineering dimension at most $d_*$ and local ghost-number-two three-form $\\rho_n$ of dimension at most $d_*-1$ such that\n$$\\mathfrak S(\\Gamma^{(<n)})=\\lambda^n\\int_U\\delta_n+O(\\lambda^{n+1}),\\qquad B_{\\Gamma_0}\\delta_n+d_H\\rho_n=0,$$\nwhere $\\mathfrak S(\\Gamma)=\\tfrac12(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)$, $B_{\\Gamma_0}X=(\\Gamma_0,X)$, and $d_H=dx^\\mu D_\\mu$ is the horizontal differential on finite-jet local forms. Assume local anomaly cancellation in the explicit form of a supplied sequence of allowed ghost-number-zero four-forms $\\Xi_n$ and ghost-number-one three-forms $\\Theta_n$ satisfying\n$$\\delta_n=B_{\\Gamma_0}\\Xi_n+d_H\\Theta_n$$\nat every first failing order; put $\\Xi_n=\\Theta_n=0$ at nonfailing orders. Compact support or the model's boundary conditions make the integral of each $d_H$-exact four-form vanish. Then the counterterm series $C(\\lambda)=-\\sum_{n\\geq1}\\lambda^n\\int_U\\Xi_n$ yields $\\mathfrak S(\\Gamma(\\lambda)+C(\\lambda))=0$ coefficientwise.\n\nFor $g\\in\\{0,1\\}$, restrict $H^{g,4}(s\\mid d_H)$, with $s=B_{\\Gamma_0}$, to local cocycles of engineering dimension at most $d_*$; identify two allowed representatives when their difference is $sm+d_Hn$ for local forms of the indicated ghost numbers and the difference remains in the allowed power-counting space. The resulting ghost-number-zero quotient is exactly the local counterterm freedom modulo trivial counterterms and total derivatives. The ghost-number-one quotient classifies local obstruction classes in that space; a nonzero class cannot be removed by an allowed local counterterm. This is a quotient statement and asserts no finite basis of representatives. The conclusion is conditional on the local QAP and supplied anomaly-cancellation sequence; it proves neither premise and asserts no empirical result, continuum functional measure, convergence, or nonperturbative existence. Any finite-dimensional Faddeev–Popov Jacobian remains the ordinary Jacobian of its specified regulated group action and transverse slice; it is not a continuum functional determinant." design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-20.pages.json ex-qftx-finite-orbit-faddeev-popov-jacobian statement= "Let $X=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}$ have positive density $|dx\\,dy|$, and let $K=S^1$ have angle coordinate $\\theta$ and right-invariant density $|d\\theta|$. Let $K$ act on the right by\n$$(x,y)\\cdot e^{i\\theta}=(x\\cos\\theta-y\\sin\\theta,x\\sin\\theta+y\\cos\\theta).$$\nFor $r>0$, set $s_r=(r,0)$, choose an open neighborhood $U_r\\subset\\{x>0\\}$ of $s_r$, let $S_r=U_r\\cap\\{y=0\\}$ have density $|dx|$, and set $\\chi(x,y)=y$ on $U_r$. The unit angular generator $1\\in\\mathfrak k=T_eK\\cong\\mathbb R$ has infinitesimal orbit vector\n$$R_{s_r}(1):=\\left.\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\right|_{0}(s_r\\cdot e^{i\\theta})=(0,r).$$\nThe Faddeev–Popov operator is the linear map $M_{s_r}:=d\\chi_{s_r}\\circ R_{s_r}:\\mathfrak k\\to\\mathbb R$, and its density Jacobian relative to $|d\\theta|$ and $|dy|$ is $\\Delta_{\\rm FP}(s_r)=|\\det M_{s_r}|=r>0$. The full orbit $K\\cdot s_r$ nevertheless meets the global condition $y=0$ at both $(r,0)$ and $(-r,0)$. Thus the local nonzero Jacobian proves transversality near $s_r$ but does not select one global representative for the orbit. The regulator group $K$ in this finite-dimensional toy is not identified with the model's non-Abelian structure group $G$." design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-20.pages.json ex-qftx-brst-exact-lorenz-gauge-term statement= 'Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ be a nonempty open region with compact support or boundary conditions that remove total-derivative terms. Work in the adopted pure $SU(2)$ model, with Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and normalized positive $\\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, extended bilinearly to the graded coefficient algebra. In the finite-jet algebra take $A_\\mu$ to be an even $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued field of ghost number $0$ and dimension $1$, $c$ an odd $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued field of ghost number $1$ and dimension $0$, $\\bar c$ an odd $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued field of ghost number $-1$ and dimension $2$, and $b$ an even $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued field of ghost number $0$ and dimension $2$. Let $\\xi>0$ be dimensionless. Use the Lie-algebra bracket and odd left BRST derivation\n\n$$sA_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c],\\qquad sc=-\\tfrac12[c,c],\\qquad s\\bar c=b,\\qquad sb=0,$$\n\nextended to jets by commuting with total derivatives. Define the Lorenz-gauge fermion and its BRST-exact functional by\n\n$$\\Psi_\\xi=\\int_U d^4x\\,\\kappa(\\bar c,\\partial^\\mu A_\\mu+\\tfrac{\\xi}{2}b),\\qquad S_{\\rm gf}:=s\\Psi_\\xi.$$\n\nThen\n\n$$S_{\\rm gf}=\\int_U d^4x\\,\\left(\\kappa(b,\\partial^\\mu A_\\mu)+\\tfrac{\\xi}{2}\\kappa(b,b)-\\kappa(\\bar c,\\partial^\\mu D_\\mu c)\\right),\\qquad D_\\mu c:=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c],$$\n\nis a local, even, ghost-number-zero functional of density dimension $4$, and it is BRST-closed. It is an exact gauge-fixing term in the formal local algebra; this example does not claim a global representative for every gauge orbit or a continuum functional measure.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-21.pages.json post-qftx-euclidean-yang-mills-model statement= 'Assume countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, meaning that every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function, for the specified smooth gauge-data and principal-connection constructions. Let $(X,g_X)$ be a connected, compact, oriented, finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth four-manifold without boundary, with a supplied Riemannian metric. The alternative flat-space branch is standard oriented Euclidean $M=\\mathbb R^4$ together with a fixed smooth trivialization outside a ball and a specified smooth extension $(\\bar P,\\bar A)$ over $X=S^4$ with its unit round metric, which is conformal to the flat metric in the stereographic chart. In the compact branch set $M=X$ and $(\\bar P,\\bar A)=(P,A)$. The extension and its transition map are part of the field data. Finite action alone is not asserted to imply such an extension. Write $g=g_X$ in the compact branch and $g=\\delta$ in the flat branch.\n\nFix the global structure group $G=SU(2)$, not its quotient, and let $\\bar P\\to X$ be a smooth right principal $SU(2)$-bundle: the right action is smooth, free, and transitive on each fibre, and local trivializations are equivariant. Its isomorphism class belongs to the model-specified set $\\Sigma$ of admitted bundle sectors. In the flat branch the physical bundle is $P=\\bar P|_M$. An isomorphism here is a smooth equivariant diffeomorphism over $\\operatorname{id}_X$, and $\\Sigma$ may be a singleton. Let $\\bar\\omega$ be a smooth $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued principal connection one-form satisfying $R_g^*\\bar\\omega=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\\bar\\omega$ and $\\bar\\omega(\\xi_{\\bar P})=\\xi$, where $\\xi_{\\bar P}(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_0p\\exp_{SU(2)}(t\\xi)$. In a local section $s_i$ of $\\bar P$, write $A_i=s_i^*\\bar\\omega$ and $F_i=dA_i+\\tfrac12[A_i\\wedge A_i]$; the alternating wedge–Lie bracket is normalized by $\\tfrac12[A_i\\wedge A_i](Y,Z)=[A_i(Y),A_i(Z)]$. The Hodge star is determined by $\\alpha\\wedge *_{g_X}\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_{g_X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_X}$ on two-forms, acts on the form part of $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued forms, and satisfies $*_{g_X}^2=1$ there.\n\nAdopt, independently of any Lorentzian theory, the source-free pure Euclidean $SU(2)$ model on exactly these supplied geometric data. The representation used for normalization is the defining representation on $\\mathbb C^2$; no matter fields are present. In an anti-Hermitian basis $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, let $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(XY)$, so $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$. Fix a dimensionless coupling $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$. In natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, $A_\\mu$ has mass dimension one and $F_{\\mu\\nu}$ mass dimension two. Adopt the dimensionless action\n\n$$S_E[A]=\\frac{1}{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}\\int_M\\kappa(F_A\\wedge *_gF_A).$$\n\nIn the flat-space branch require finite action in addition to the specified smooth compactification extension. Smooth gauge transformations are equivariant bundle automorphisms covering the identity; the action is evaluated on the supplied admissible connection. Classical stationarity means vanishing first variation for smooth compactly supported adjoint-bundle-valued one-form variations preserving the supplied extension/boundary data. On a closed base every smooth variation has compact support.\n\nThis is a classical Euclidean model assumption. It neither derives a Euclidean theory by Wick rotation from a Lorentzian postulate nor asserts empirical confirmation, reflection positivity, a functional measure, gauge fixing, a convergent quantum integral, or a continuum quantum theory. The closed-manifold formulation is the explicitly adopted geometric extension of the cited flat Euclidean action; finite action alone is not claimed to prove removable singularities or compactification.' design_state= 'Owner repair: independently adopted source-reviewed Euclidean action model.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-21.pages.json def-qftx-euclidean-yang-mills-sector statement= 'Assume countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, meaning that every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function, for the specified smooth gauge-data and principal-connection constructions. Let $(X,g_X)$ be a connected, compact, oriented, finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth four-manifold without boundary, with a supplied Riemannian metric. The alternative flat-space branch is standard oriented Euclidean $M=\\mathbb R^4$ together with a fixed smooth trivialization outside a ball and a specified smooth extension $(\\bar P,\\bar A)$ over $X=S^4$ with its unit round metric, which is conformal to the flat metric in the stereographic chart. In the compact branch set $M=X$ and $(\\bar P,\\bar A)=(P,A)$. The extension and its transition map are part of the field data. Finite action alone is not asserted to imply such an extension. Write $g=g_X$ in the compact branch and $g=\\delta$ in the flat branch.\n\nFix the global structure group $G=SU(2)$, not its quotient, and let $\\bar P\\to X$ be a smooth right principal $SU(2)$-bundle: the right action is smooth, free, and transitive on each fibre, and local trivializations are equivariant. Its isomorphism class belongs to the model-specified set $\\Sigma$ of admitted bundle sectors. In the flat branch the physical bundle is $P=\\bar P|_M$. An isomorphism here is a smooth equivariant diffeomorphism over $\\operatorname{id}_X$, and $\\Sigma$ may be a singleton. Let $\\bar\\omega$ be a smooth $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued principal connection one-form satisfying $R_g^*\\bar\\omega=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\\bar\\omega$ and $\\bar\\omega(\\xi_{\\bar P})=\\xi$, where $\\xi_{\\bar P}(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_0p\\exp_{SU(2)}(t\\xi)$. In a local section $s_i$ of $\\bar P$, write $A_i=s_i^*\\bar\\omega$ and $F_i=dA_i+\\tfrac12[A_i\\wedge A_i]$; the alternating wedge–Lie bracket is normalized by $\\tfrac12[A_i\\wedge A_i](Y,Z)=[A_i(Y),A_i(Z)]$. The Hodge star is determined by $\\alpha\\wedge *_{g_X}\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_{g_X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_X}$ on two-forms, acts on the form part of $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued forms, and satisfies $*_{g_X}^2=1$ there.\n\nAdopt the independent Euclidean model of [[post-qftx-euclidean-yang-mills-model]] with precisely the geometric data specified above. Its action is an explicit physical assumption and is not derived by Wick rotation from a Lorentzian model. Use anti-Hermitian defining-representation generators with $\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(T_aT_b)=-\\tfrac12\\delta_{ab}$, the positive invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(XY)$, and a fixed dimensionless coupling $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$. In natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, $A_\\mu$ has mass dimension one, $F_{\\mu\\nu}$ has mass dimension two, and $S_E/\\hbar$ is dimensionless. The conditional Euclidean action is\n\n$$S_E[A]=\\frac{1}{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}\\int_M\\kappa(F_A\\wedge *_gF_A).$$\n\nIn the flat-space branch admissibility also requires $S_E[A]<\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^4$. Define $B_{\\mathrm{top}}(X,Y)=-\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(XY)=\\kappa(X,Y)/2$. The mathematical charge used here is the supplied trace-normalized charge of the compactified connection,\n\n$$\\nu(\\bar P,\\bar A)=\\frac{1}{8\\pi^2}\\int_X B_{\\mathrm{top}}(F_{\\bar A}\\wedge F_{\\bar A})=-\\frac{1}{8\\pi^2}\\int_X\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(F_{\\bar A}\\wedge F_{\\bar A})=-\\langle c_2(\\bar E_{\\mathbf2}),[X]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\n\nwhere $\\bar E_{\\mathbf2}=\\bar P\\times_{\\mathbf2}\\mathbb C^2$ and the final equality uses the supplied instanton-number definition with its full-Axiom-of-Choice and Chern–Weil hypotheses. This sign makes a positive-charge self-dual configuration satisfy $F_{\\bar A}=*_{g_X}F_{\\bar A}$ and a negative-charge anti-self-dual configuration satisfy $F_{\\bar A}=-*_{g_X}F_{\\bar A}$ on the compactified base. The charge label is not asserted to classify every connected component or every gauge orbit.\n\nLet $\\mathcal A_\\nu(\\Sigma)$ denote the disjoint union, over the bundle sectors in $\\Sigma$, of their admissible smooth connections whose compactified charge is $\\nu$. In the flat-space branch, admissible connections are required to have the specified smooth extension and finite Euclidean action.\n\nA classical Euclidean saddle is a smooth admissible connection whose first variation of $S_E$ vanishes for every smooth compactly supported connection variation preserving the boundary data. An instanton (respectively anti-instanton) saddle is a nonzero-charge saddle satisfying the positive (respectively negative) self-duality equation. A source observable is a gauge-invariant function $O$ on the admissible connection data; gauge transformations are smooth principal-bundle automorphisms over $\\operatorname{id}_M$.\n\nThe notation\n\n$$Z_{\\mathrm{formal}}(\\theta;O)=\\sum_{\\nu}e^{i\\theta\\nu}\\int_{\\mathcal A_{\\nu}(\\Sigma)}\\mathcal D A\\;O[A]e^{-S_E[A]}$$\n\ndenotes only a formal continuum sector expression over connections, with gauge redundancy left untreated. Here $\\theta$ is dimensionless and has period $2\\pi$ when $\\nu$ is integral. The expression defines no functional measure, gauge fixing or orbit-volume prescription, determinant, convergent integral, Hilbert-space sector, or continuum quantum field theory, and selects no finite regulator or limit.' design_state= 'Owner repaired: independent Euclidean postulate supplies the model assumption.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-21.pages.json ex-qftx-euclidean-trivial-vacuum-sector statement= 'Let $M=S^4$ be a connected compact oriented smooth four-manifold with any smooth Riemannian metric, let $P=S^4\\times SU(2)$ be the trivial principal bundle, and take the zero connection $A=0$ in its global trivialization. With the SU(2) trace and charge convention of the Euclidean Yang–Mills sector definition, this configuration has $F_A=0$, $\\nu(P,A)=0$, and $S_E[A]=0$. It is a classical saddle in the trivial sector, and it is not a nonzero-charge instanton saddle.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness escalated with the assigned page Euclidean model prerequisite unresolved.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-21.pages.json ex-qftx-bpst-instanton-formal-sector-weight statement= 'Let $S^4=\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^5:|z|=1\\}$ have its unit round metric. Let $p_\\infty$ be the point omitted by the north stereographic chart, let $p_0$ have north-chart coordinate $x=0$, and orient $S^4$ so $dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3\\wedge dx^4>0$. In the north chart put $r^2=\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(x^\\mu)^2$; the metric is $4(1+r^2)^{-2}\\sum_\\mu(dx^\\mu)^2$. In the south chart put $y=x/r^2$; the metric is $4(1+|y|^2)^{-2}\\sum_\\mu(dy^\\mu)^2$. Define $SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\\det U=1\\}$. Use the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$ and anti-Hermitian generators $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$ with $\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(T_aT_b)=-\\tfrac12\\delta_{ab}$. Let $\\epsilon_{ijk}$ be totally antisymmetric with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, set $\\eta_{aij}=\\epsilon_{aij}$, $\\eta_{ai4}=\\delta_{ai}$, $\\bar\\eta_{aij}=-\\epsilon_{aij}$, and $\\bar\\eta_{ai4}=\\delta_{ai}$ for $i,j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, and extend both arrays antisymmetrically in their last two indices. For any $\\rho>0$, put $u(x)=(x^4I_2-i x^j\\sigma_j)/r$ on the overlap, and glue two trivial right $SU(2)$ bundles with local sections satisfying $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$. The local connection potentials are\n\n$$A_N=\\frac{2\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}x^\\nu}{r^2+\\rho^2}T_a\\,dx^\\mu,\\qquad A_S=-\\frac{2\\rho^2\\bar\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}y^\\nu}{1+\\rho^2|y|^2}T_a\\,dy^\\mu.$$\n\nOn the overlap the potentials obey $A_S=\\operatorname{Ad}_uA_N+u\\,d(u^{-1})$, with the right-action section convention above. Use the conditional Euclidean action $S_E=(2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2)^{-1}\\int_{S^4}\\kappa(F\\wedge *F)$ with $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(XY)$, and the compactified charge $\\nu=-(8\\pi^2)^{-1}\\int_{S^4}\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(F\\wedge F)$, for fixed $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$. For this specified smooth two-chart connection, the charge is $\\nu=1$, the curvature is self-dual, and $S_E=8\\pi^2/g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2$; it is a classical instanton saddle. If the formal sector expression includes the dimensionless theta coupling $\\theta$, the corresponding formal single-saddle factor is $e^{-8\\pi^2/g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2+i\\theta}$. This factor is not an evaluated continuum path integral.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness escalated for the Euclidean model mismatch and upstream B-only BPST dependency placement.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-22.pages.json lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-group statement= 'Assume the axiom of countable choice. Let\n\n$$SU(2):=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\\ \\det U=1\\}$$\n\nwith matrix multiplication and the topology induced by the eight real and imaginary matrix-entry coordinates. Equip it with its standard real Lie-group structure as the special unitary matrix group. Then $SU(2)$ is a compact Hausdorff non-Abelian finite-dimensional real Lie group. Its defining representation is the map $\\rho_2:SU(2)\\to U(2)$, $\\rho_2(V)=V$, acting on $\\mathbb C^2$ with the standard Hermitian form $h(v,w)=v^*w$; it is a smooth unitary group homomorphism.\n\nEvery $V\\in SU(2)$ has the form $V=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&\\beta\\\\-\\overline{\\beta}&\\overline{\\alpha}\\end{pmatrix}$ with $|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1$; consequently $\\tfrac12\\operatorname{ReTr}_2V=\\operatorname{Re}\\alpha\\in[-1,1]$.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored as a local mathematical prerequisite; readiness follows scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-22.pages.json lem-qftx-su2-wilson-transfer-well-defined statement= "Assume full AC and use the finite non-Abelian lattice-data interface with global group $SU(2)$ and its smooth defining unitary representation on standard $\\mathbb C^2$. Let $N_s,N_t\\geq3$ be finite integers and let $a>0$, $\\beta>0$. Use the finite periodic Euclidean lattice $\\Lambda=(\\mathbb Z/N_s\\mathbb Z)^3\\times(\\mathbb Z/N_t\\mathbb Z)$, with its finite oriented two-complex of vertices, positive spatial edges $(x,i,t)$, temporal edges $(x,t)$, and oriented plaquette boundary words. On every positive edge put an independent matrix in the compact Hausdorff Lie group\n\n$$SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\\ \\det U=1\\},$$\n\nand assign its inverse to reverse traversal. A vertex function $\\Omega:\\Lambda\\to SU(2)$ acts on an oriented link $U_{vw}$ by $U_{vw}\\mapsto\\Omega_vU_{vw}\\Omega_w^{-1}$. For each oriented plaquette $p$, let $U_p$ be the ordered product of its four boundary links, using inverse matrices for reverse traversals. Put\n\n$$S_\\Lambda(U)=\\beta\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_\\Lambda}\\left(1-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{ReTr}_2U_p\\right).$$\n\nLet $dU$ be the unique normalized left Haar Borel probability measure on $SU(2)$, which is also right invariant, and use it as the bi-invariant link measure in the finite lattice-data interface. Let $d\\mu_\\Lambda$ be its finite product over all positive links, formed by the finite-product measure theorem. Define\n\n$$Z_\\Lambda=\\int e^{-S_\\Lambda(U)}\\,d\\mu_\\Lambda(U).$$\n\nFor every bounded measurable function $O$ on the finite link configuration space, the integral $\\int Oe^{-S_\\Lambda}\\,d\\mu_\\Lambda$ is finite; moreover $0<Z_\\Lambda\\leq1$, so its normalized expectation is defined.\n\nFor a spatial slice $u=(U_{x,i})$, define the spatial plaquette action $S_s(u)$ by restricting the displayed action to spatial plaquettes. For two adjacent slices $u',u$ and temporal links $\\eta_x$, define $S_t(u',u;\\eta)$ by summing the same plaquette term over $\\eta_xU'_{x,i}\\eta_{x+\\hat i}^{-1}U_{x,i}^{-1}$. Let $d\\mu_s,d\\mu_V$ be the corresponding finite product Haar probabilities, let $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}=L^2(SU(2)^{E_s},d\\mu_s)$, and for $h\\in SU(2)^{V_s}$ set $(\\mathcal U(h)\\psi)(u)=\\psi(h^{-1}\\cdot u)$, with equality in $L^2$. Define $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\{\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}:\\mathcal U(h)\\psi=\\psi\\text{ for every }h\\in SU(2)^{V_s}\\}$.\n\n$$K(u',u)=e^{-\\frac12S_s(u')-\\frac12S_s(u)}\\int e^{-S_t(u',u;\\eta)}\\,d\\mu_V(\\eta),\\qquad (T\\psi)(u')=\\int K(u',u)\\psi(u)\\,d\\mu_s(u).$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ is closed and contains the nonzero constant function. $T$ is a bounded operator on $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ with $\\|T\\|\\leq1$ and maps $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ into $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$. The cyclic product of $N_t$ kernels integrates to $Z_\\Lambda$; insertion of any bounded slice observable gives the corresponding finite-volume unnormalized expectation. No self-adjointness, positivity as a quadratic form, Hamiltonian limit, or continuum statement is asserted." design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally on the actual batch-6 finite-lattice-data interface; current upstream readiness records are verified.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-22.pages.json post-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-regulator statement= "Adopt the following pure-gauge Euclidean regulator. Assume full AC for normalized Haar-probability existence and for the L² transfer-operator framework. Let $N_s,N_t\\geq3$ be integers, $a>0$ a common lattice spacing, and $g_0>0$ a dimensionless bare coupling. Use natural units $\\hbar=c=1$. The finite periodic lattice is\n\n$$\\Lambda=(\\mathbb Z/N_s\\mathbb Z)^3\\times(\\mathbb Z/N_t\\mathbb Z),$$\n\nwith spatial extent $L_s=N_sa$ in each spatial direction and Euclidean time extent $L_t=N_ta$. The global gauge group supplied by the finite non-Abelian lattice datum is\n\n$$SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\\ \\det U=1\\},$$\n\nthe compact Hausdorff non-Abelian Lie group established in the preceding local lemma, with its smooth defining two-dimensional unitary representation. Use that representation; $U^*$ denotes conjugate transpose and $\\operatorname{Tr}_2$ its matrix trace. All link variables are dimensionless.\n\nFor each positive spatial direction $i\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, assign a link matrix $U_{x,i,t}\\in SU(2)$ from $(x,t)$ to $(x+\\hat i,t)$. Assign a temporal link matrix $\\eta_{x,t}\\in SU(2)$ from $(x,t)$ to $(x,t+1)$, with time understood modulo $N_t$. A reversed link carries the inverse matrix. Vertex gauge transformations are all functions $\\Omega:\\Lambda\\to SU(2)$, acting by\n\n$$U_{x,i,t}\\mapsto \\Omega_{x,t}U_{x,i,t}\\Omega_{x+\\hat i,t}^{-1},\\qquad \\eta_{x,t}\\mapsto\\Omega_{x,t}\\eta_{x,t}\\Omega_{x,t+1}^{-1}.$$\n\nFor $i<j$, define the spatial plaquette product\n\n$$U_{x,ij,t}=U_{x,i,t}U_{x+\\hat i,j,t}U_{x+\\hat j,i,t}^{-1}U_{x,j,t}^{-1},$$\n\nand define the temporal-spatial plaquette product\n\n$$Q_{x,i,t}=\\eta_{x,t}U_{x,i,t+1}\\eta_{x+\\hat i,t}^{-1}U_{x,i,t}^{-1}.$$\n\nSet $\\beta=4/g_0^2$, a positive dimensionless parameter. For a spatial slice $u=(U_{x,i})$, set\n\n$$S_s(u)=\\beta\\sum_{x,\\ i<j}\\left(1-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{ReTr}_2 U_{x,ij}\\right),$$\n\nand for adjacent slices $u',u$ and temporal links $\\eta=(\\eta_x)$, set\n\n$$S_t(u',u;\\eta)=\\beta\\sum_{x,\\ i}\\left(1-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{ReTr}_2(\\eta_xU'_{x,i}\\eta_{x+\\hat i}^{-1}U_{x,i}^{-1})\\right).$$\n\nThe adopted Wilson action is $S_\\Lambda=\\sum_{t=0}^{N_t-1}\\big(S_s(u_t)+S_t(u_{t+1},u_t;\\eta_t)\\big)$, with $u_{N_t}=u_0$. The compact Hausdorff group $SU(2)$ has the unique normalized left Haar probability measure $dU$, which is also right invariant under the assumed Axiom of Choice. On each positively oriented link use this measure; $d\\mu_\\Lambda$ is its finite product over the link set. The exact finite-volume partition function and observable expectation for every bounded gauge-invariant function $O$ are\n\n$$Z_\\Lambda=\\int e^{-S_\\Lambda}\\,d\\mu_\\Lambda,\\qquad \\langle O\\rangle_\\Lambda=Z_\\Lambda^{-1}\\int Oe^{-S_\\Lambda}\\,d\\mu_\\Lambda.$$\n\nFor the transfer convention, let $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}=L^2(SU(2)^{E_s},d\\mu_s)$, where $E_s$ is the finite set of positive spatial links in one slice and $d\\mu_s$ is product normalized Haar probability. Define $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\{\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}:\\mathcal U(h)\\psi=\\psi\\text{ for every }h\\in SU(2)^{V_s}\\}$, where $V_s$ is the finite vertex set of the slice and $\\mathcal U(h)\\psi(u)=\\psi(h^{-1}\\!\\cdot u)$ in $L^2$. The finite-lattice well-definedness result establishes that this fixed subspace is closed. It is the group-invariant finite-slice Gauss sector. Define\n\n$$K(u',u)=e^{-\\frac12S_s(u')-\\frac12S_s(u)}\\int_{SU(2)^{V_s}}e^{-S_t(u',u;\\eta)}\\,d\\mu_V(\\eta),\\qquad (T\\psi)(u')=\\int K(u',u)\\psi(u)\\,d\\mu_s(u).$$\n\nThe finite Euclidean time direction is assembled by iterating this one-step transfer operator, with the periodic boundary identification stated above. This is a Euclidean transfer convention only: no self-adjoint or positive-spectrum claim for $T$, no Lorentzian Hamiltonian identification, and no continuum or infinite-volume limit are postulated. The model has no dynamical matter fields, so it makes no matter-screening, confinement, or string-breaking claim. The regulator is adopted, not derived from a continuum theory or empirical evidence.\n\nThe quantities $a,L_s,L_t$ have units of length; $g_0,\\beta,U_{x,i,t},\\eta_{x,t},S_\\Lambda,Z_\\Lambda$ are dimensionless in the stated natural-unit convention. No measured parameter is supplied." design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally on the actual batch-6 finite-lattice-data interface; current upstream readiness records are verified.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-22.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-wilson-lattice-transfer-sector statement= 'Take $N_s=N_t=3$, $a>0$, and $g_0>0$ in the adopted pure SU(2) Euclidean Wilson regulator. Let $C$ be the elementary spatial plaquette at the origin in the $(1,2)$-plane at $t=0$, and set $W_C(u)=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{ReTr}_2(U_C)$. At the configuration with every link matrix equal to $I_2$, the Wilson action is zero and $W_C=1$. For every link configuration, $W_C$ is a bounded gauge-invariant multiplication observable. Its normalized expectation exists; the one-step transfer operator is bounded and maps the group-invariant Gauss sector into itself, which contains the nonzero constant wavefunction; and cyclic iteration of its kernel reproduces the finite periodic Euclidean integral. These are exact finite-regulator statements; they assert no continuum area law or Lorentzian energy spectrum.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored as a finite-regulator verification conditional on the A-page postulate and its documented inputs.'
