research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-24.pages.json def-aqft-model-and-scale-data statement= 'Use natural units c=ℏ=1, with physical dimensions assigned using the QFT unit convention of the selected model. A finite-regulator effective-field-theory model-data record consists of a finite undirected graph Γ=(X,EΓ) with nonempty vertex set X, the graph distance dΓ, retained real coordinates ℓ∈ℝ^X, integrated real coordinates χ∈ℝ^X, a Borel ultraviolet action SUV(ℓ,χ)∈ℝ, and a specified Borel probability measure νH on ℝ^X for the integrated coordinates. The full ultraviolet data are these objects before any integration. This definition does not assert that the integral of exp(−SUV) exists; finiteness and positivity are checked when the Wilsonian action is defined.\n\nFor the positive heavy-scalar Gaussian submodel, include a real symmetric matrix A on ℝ^X satisfying uᵀAu≥0 for every u and Axy=0 when dΓ(x,y)>1. Its entries have mass-squared units and its operator norm satisfies √‖A‖≤Λ. Choose M>0 and put K=M²I+A. The heavy coordinate χ has mass-inverse units, so χᵀKχ is dimensionless. A source J(ℓ)∈ℝ^X has mass units, so J(ℓ)ᵀχ is dimensionless. When the source is restricted to external energy E, require J(ℓ)=PEJ(ℓ), where PE is the orthogonal spectral projection of A onto eigenvalues in [0,E²].\n\nThe scale data are a positive Wilsonian matching scale $\\mu_W$, a heavy mass $M>0$, an external energy bound $E\\ge0$, and a regulator cutoff $\\Lambda>0$, with $\\sqrt{\\|A\\|}\\le\\Lambda$ in the Gaussian submodel. The Wilsonian scale $\\mu_W$ records where the retained low-coordinate action is specified; $M$ is the heavy quadratic mass; $E$ bounds the admitted source spectrum; and $\\Lambda$ bounds the finite regulator. These are separately named parameters, and this definition imposes no relation among their numerical values. A small-remainder theorem may require an additional hierarchy such as $E<M$.\n\nLight and heavy name the roles in the declared split: ℓ is retained at μW and χ is integrated in the finite measure νH. They do not assert that every retained field is massless or that every integrated field is heavy in an unrestricted continuum theory. If the model includes non-Abelian gauge data, record the actual global group, Lie algebra, principal bundle, representations, and admitted bundle sectors as one supplied gauge-data object. That object selects no connection, action, or quantum measure by itself. The Gaussian submodel below keeps any such gauge data as fixed context and makes no gauge-field integration claim.\n\nThis is a finite-dimensional regulator description. It makes no assertion that a general interacting functional measure exists, that a cutoff can be removed, or that any selected model describes nature.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-24.pages.json def-aqft-wilsonian-action statement= 'Take a finite-regulator model datum with retained coordinates $\\ell$, integrated coordinates $h$, a normalized Borel probability measure $\\nu_H$ on the finite-dimensional $h$-space, and a real Borel ultraviolet action $S_{\\rm UV}(\\ell,h)$. For each retained configuration $\\ell$ define\n\n$$\nZ_H(\\ell):=\\int e^{-S_{\\rm UV}(\\ell,h)}\\,d\\nu_H(h).\n$$\n\nOn the domain of $\\ell$ for which $0<Z_H(\\ell)<\\infty$, the finite-regulator Wilsonian action at scale $\\mu_W$ is\n\n$$\nS_W(\\ell;\\mu_W):=-\\log Z_H(\\ell).\n$$\n\nThus “integrating out” the high sector means this specified finite integral over $h$ while $\\ell$ is held fixed. The measure, action, regulator, and low/high split are inputs; the definition does not assert that every interacting model has a finite or well-defined integral. Replacing $\\nu_H$ by a positive constant multiple changes $S_W$ only by an additive constant independent of $\\ell$, which is fixed by the chosen normalization convention.\n\nA one-particle-irreducible effective action, when it exists, is the Legendre transform of a connected source-generating functional for the retained fields. It is a different construction from the high-coordinate integral above. No 1PI functional is needed or asserted here.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-24.pages.json def-aqft-derivative-expansion-and-truncation statement= 'In the finite graph model, a matrix $B$ on $\\mathbb R^X$ has range at most $r$ when $B_{xy}=0$ whenever $d_\\Gamma(x,y)>r$. The positive heavy-sector matrix $A$ from the model data has range at most one. A derivative expansion of its inverse kernel is the formal operator series\n\n$$\n(M^2I+A)^{-1}\\sim M^{-2}\\sum_{r=0}^{\\infty}(-A/M^2)^r.\n$$\n\nIts order-$N$ truncation, for an integer $N\\ge0$, is the finite operator\n\n$$\nS_N:=M^{-2}\\sum_{r=0}^{N}(-A/M^2)^r.\n$$\n\nIt has range at most $N$ in this regulator. For a source $J$ supported in the spectral interval $[0,E^2]$ of $A$, the dimensionless power-counting parameter is $E/M$. This definition is an organized finite-order approximation; it does not assert convergence for unrestricted sources, a generic error bound, or a continuum local-operator expansion. The positive Gaussian theorem supplies a remainder bound only under its exact hypotheses.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-24.pages.json prop-aqft-finite-gaussian-shell-composition statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge1$, let $C_1,C_2$ be real symmetric positive-semidefinite $d\\times d$ covariance matrices, and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty]$ be Borel. For a covariance matrix $C$, let $\\gamma_C$ denote the centered Gaussian probability with covariance $C$, including when $C$ is singular, and define\n\n$$\nT_Cf(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f(x+z)\\,d\\gamma_C(z).\n$$\n\nFor a finite nonnegative Borel potential $V$, $0<T_C(e^{-V})(x)\\le1$; define the finite nonnegative potential $V_C(x):=-\\log T_C(e^{-V})(x)$. Then\n\n$$\nT_{C_2}(T_{C_1}f)(x)=T_{C_1+C_2}f(x)\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^d)\n$$\n\nas an equality in $[0,\\infty]$, and\n\n$$\ne^{-(V_{C_1})_{C_2}(x)}=e^{-V_{C_1+C_2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe Gaussian measures are normalized probabilities, so no determinant or vacuum constant appears. This is a finite-regulator composition identity; it asserts no derivative equation along a covariance path and no continuum limit.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; local A-page mathematical supplier required for the finite shell composition; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-24.pages.json texp-aqft-finite-heavy-scalar-matching statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Choice and the finite-regulator model data in the definitions below. Let $d\\ge1$, let $A$ be a real symmetric positive-semidefinite $d\\times d$ matrix, let $M>0$, and put $K=M^2I+A$. Use natural units with $\\chi$ in mass-inverse units, $A$ and $K$ in mass-squared units, and a source $J\\in\\mathbb R^d$ in mass units. Let $E\\ge0$, let $P_E$ be the spectral projection of the complexification of $A$ onto $[0,E^2]$, and assume $J=P_EJ$. In the finite Gaussian sector, let $\\gamma_{K^{-1}}=N_d(0,K^{-1})$ and define\n\n$$ Z_H(J):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}e^{J^T\\chi}\\,d\\gamma_{K^{-1}}(\\chi) $$\n\n$$ \\Delta S_W(J):=-\\log Z_H(J) $$\n\nFor each integer $N\\ge0$, put\n\n$$\nS_NJ:=M^{-2}\\sum_{r=0}^{N}(-A/M^2)^rJ.\n$$\n\nThen both finite Gaussian integrals are finite and\n\n$$ Z_H(J)=\\exp\\!\\left(\\tfrac12J^TK^{-1}J\\right) $$\n\n$$ \\Delta S_W(J)=-\\tfrac12J^TK^{-1}J $$\n\nThe inverse-kernel and action remainders satisfy\n\n$$\n\\|K^{-1}J-S_NJ\\|\\le M^{-2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}\\|J\\|,\n$$\n\n$$ \\left|\\Delta S_W(J)+\\tfrac12J^TS_NJ\\right| \\le \\frac{\\|J\\|^2}{2M^2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1} $$\n\nThese are exact conditional statements for a finite positive quadratic heavy sector. The remainder is small in the stated band when $E<M$; no claim is made for unrestricted sources, arbitrary interactions, a continuum measure, or a differentiated Wilsonian flow.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-24.pages.json ex-aqft-gaussian-shell-matching statement= 'Assume AC. Use one positive mass unit $m_0$ and a one-site finite regulator with $M=2m_0$, $A=(m_0^2)$, $E=m_0$, $\\mu_W=\\tfrac32m_0$, $\\Lambda=3m_0$, source $J=(m_0)$, and truncation order $N=0$. Then $K=M^2I+A=(5m_0^2)$. Let $\\gamma_{K^{-1}}=N_1(0,K^{-1})$ be the centered normalized Gaussian with covariance $K^{-1}$. For $Z_H(J)=\\int e^{J^T\\chi}\\,d\\gamma_{K^{-1}}(\\chi)$ and the action increment $\\Delta S_W=-\\log Z_H(J)$, the exact increment is $-1/10$, the order-zero approximation is $-1/8$, the vector remainder is $1/(20m_0)$, and the action remainder is $1/40\\le1/32$.\n\nFor a separate dimensionless shell coordinate, let $\\gamma_C=N_1(0,C)$ be the centered normalized Gaussian of variance $C$, define $T_Cf(x)=\\int f(x+z)\\,d\\gamma_C(z)$, and take $f(x)=x^2$, $C_1=1$, and $C_2=3$. The successive normalized shell value at zero and the combined-shell value are both $4$.\n\nThis is a model-specific finite calculation. It reports no observation and asserts no continuum limit or general interacting matching rule.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned B-page worked example; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-25.pages.json def-aqft-operator-basis-physical-data statement= "Use four-dimensional Minkowski space $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, coordinates $x^0=ct$ and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ for mass-dimension counting. A **physical EFT operator contract** is a record of the following selected model data; none of the selections is inferred from an operator's name.\n\nHere an EFT is an adopted low-energy model whose local Lagrangian is organized by specified fields, symmetries, coefficients, and a finite retained order; this definition implies no ultraviolet completion or physical realization. First choose a finite set $I$ of field species. For each $i\\in I$, specify a finite-dimensional complex graded space $\\Phi_i=\\Phi_i^{\\bar 0}\\oplus\\Phi_i^{\\bar 1}$, its bosonic or fermionic parity, its smooth field domain on $M$, any reality condition, and its assigned rational engineering weight $\\Delta_i$. Even fields are smooth maps $M\\to\\Phi_i^{\\bar 0}$; odd field components take values in a fixed Grassmann algebra with smooth coefficient functions. A spurion is a fixed, non-dynamical parameter assigned a representation and engineering weight, used to record a declared symmetry-breaking insertion. The proper orthochronous Lorentz group is $SO^+(1,3)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb R):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\ \\det L=1,\\ L^0{}_0\\ge1\\}$; specify its finite-dimensional action on all spacetime indices and require each Lagrangian density to be a Lorentz scalar. State the full gauge and flavor actions on every field, index, and spurion. A flavor symmetry that is absent is recorded as trivial; an approximate or explicitly broken symmetry requires its breaking spurions and weights to be listed.\n\nFor a non-Abelian gauge sector, specify the compact real gauge group with its actual global form, smooth right principal bundle, admitted bundle sectors, unitary representations, and a smooth principal connection. The connection is a Lie-algebra-valued one-form reproducing fundamental vertical vectors and transforming by the adjoint action; its local curvature is $F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$, and the covariant derivative in a representation $\\rho$ is $D_\\mu\\psi=\\partial_\\mu\\psi+\\rho_*(A_\\mu)\\psi$. The field and connection data must include their regularity and spacetime and internal index representations. These formulas use the right-principal-bundle convention fixed by the supplied gauge-geometry definitions.\n\nThe local operator algebra uses a separate finite-dimensional rational representation of a complex reductive affine algebraic group $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ on the finite jet generators. A contract must supply the maps and representations relating $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ to the selected compact gauge group and flavor group. It must either verify, on each retained finite jet space, that the algebraic invariant subspace is exactly the intended physical invariant space, or label the quotient as a formal algebraic selection only. Compactness of a real gauge group alone does not identify its invariant polynomials with those of an algebraic complexification.\n\nChoose nonnegative integer cutoffs $r$ and $q$, a coefficient field (here $\\mathbb C$) and its conjugation data, and a finite rational maximum engineering dimension $D_{\\max}$. With derivatives assigned weight one, the local field-jet definition supplies the supercommutative polynomial module $P_r=\\operatorname{Sym}(J_{r,\\bar 0}^*)\\otimes\\Lambda(J_{r,\\bar 1}^*)$ and its fixed-weight subspaces. For each weight $D$, let $W_D$ be the finite raw polynomial space, $V_D=W_D^{G_{\\mathrm{alg}}}$ its invariant scalar subspace, and $\\widetilde R_D$ the explicitly declared $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$-stable linear relation subspace. The selected operator quotient is $\\mathcal Q_D=V_D/(\\widetilde R_D\\cap V_D)$: it identifies exactly the declared relations and makes no completeness claim about all EFT redundancies. For each retained $D$, choose a vector-space basis of $\\mathcal Q_D$ and one representative $\\mathcal O_\\alpha\\in V_D$ of each basis class; write $\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{op}}$ for the resulting finite set of representative labels and $\\Delta_\\alpha$ for the weight of $\\mathcal O_\\alpha$. The finite jet, field-degree, and dimension cutoffs give a finite-dimensional retained space, and the fixed-cutoff monomial set gives finitely many retained weights and representatives.\n\nFor each $\\widetilde R_D$, record each generator as a polynomial or Fierz identity, a total divergence under compact-support or stated boundary conditions, or a leading-equation or field-redefinition relation tied to an explicit base action and retained order. An equation-of-motion entry records its variational derivative and field multiplier; a field-redefinition entry records the local map, its inverse through the retained order, and all induced higher-order terms. The quotient is a definition of the declared relation space; its existence alone does not establish action, off-shell Green-function, quantum-measure, or scattering equivalence.\n\nChoose the canonical-dimension power-counting scheme, coefficient truncation, and a nonnegative integer loop order $L_{\\max}$. The formal loop label $\\zeta$ is dimensionless bookkeeping and is not a convergence claim. If $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}>0$ is the coefficient-suppression scale, the retained local Lagrangian has the convention\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{tr}}=\\sum_{\\substack{\\alpha\\in\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{op}}\\\\\\Delta_\\alpha\\le D_{\\max}}}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{L_{\\max}}\\zeta^\\ell C_{\\alpha,\\ell}(\\mu_W)\\frac{\\mathcal O_\\alpha}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^{\\Delta_\\alpha-4}},$$\n\nwhere each $\\mathcal O_\\alpha$ has engineering weight $\\Delta_\\alpha$, each $C_{\\alpha,\\ell}$ is dimensionless in the stated natural units, and the chosen reality conditions make $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{tr}}$ real. This is canonical-dimension counting for the selected linear EFT; HEFT chiral counting and multi-scale SCET counting are different schemes and are not implied. A truncation order is not a numerical error bound, a coefficient prediction, or a derived renormalization-group flow.\n\nIf the same application also uses a finite-regulator data record, retain its graph variables, measure, Wilsonian scale $\\mu_W$, heavy mass $M$, external-energy bound $E$, and regulator cutoff $\\Lambda$ with their supplied meanings. The operator-suppression scale $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}$, regulator cutoff $\\Lambda$, $M$, $E$, and $\\mu_W$ are distinct parameters unless an additional model input identifies them. A map from finite-graph variables to the continuum finite-jet operator data is also additional input; the finite-regulator definition supplies no such map or continuum limit." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; finite-jet contract, exact quotient representatives, supplier scale conventions, sources, and current dependency interfaces recorded; readiness ready.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-25.pages.json ex-aqft-scalar-gauge-operator-power-counting statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the supplied gauge datum. Use $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $c=\\hbar=1$. Let the gauge group be the compact group $SU(2)$ on the trivial principal bundle with the product connection, whose global-section potential is $A_\\mu=0$. Gauge transformations are smooth maps $u:M\\to SU(2)$ acting by $H(x)\\mapsto u(x)H(x)$ and $\\widetilde H(x)\\mapsto\\widetilde H(x)u(x)^{-1}$. Take one even Lorentz-scalar complex doublet $H$ in the standard unitary representation. In the complex-algebraic polynomial model, use $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}=SO(\\eta,\\mathbb C)\\times SL(2,\\mathbb C)$, where $SO(\\eta,\\mathbb C)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb C):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\det L=1\\}$ acts trivially on these scalar fields, and treat $H\\in\\mathbb C^2$ and an independent dual variable $\\widetilde H\\in(\\mathbb C^2)^*$ with the standard $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ and dual actions. The physical Lorentz and gauge groups map to $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ by $(L,u)\\mapsto(L,u)$, viewing the real proper orthochronous Lorentz matrix $L$ as a complex matrix and the same $SU(2)$ matrix $u$ as an element of $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$; the standard and dual actions restrict to the specified physical actions. Impose $\\widetilde H=H^\\dagger$ for the real action. Take no flavor symmetry, field weight one, derivative cutoff $r=0$, maximum field degree $q=6$, retained engineering dimension $D_{\\max}=6$, loop order $L_{\\max}=0$, and a positive coefficient scale $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}$. For this illustration the declared relation subspace is zero. Then $\\mathcal O_2=\\widetilde H H$, $\\mathcal O_4=(\\widetilde H H)^2$, and $\\mathcal O_6=(\\widetilde H H)^3$ are gauge-invariant scalar polynomials of engineering dimensions $2$, $4$, and $6$. With real dimensionless $C_6$, the term $C_6\\mathcal O_6/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^2$ has Lagrangian density dimension $4$ and belongs to this dimension-six truncation. For $a,b\\in\\{1,2\\}$, define $\\varepsilon_{12}=1$, $\\varepsilon_{21}=-1$, and $\\varepsilon_{11}=\\varepsilon_{22}=0$; then the antisymmetric polynomial $\\varepsilon_{ab}H^aH^b$ vanishes because the even field components commute. This is a finite scalar-sector illustration, not a complete $SU(2)$ EFT basis or a claim that these coefficients describe nature.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; complete physical example, conditional proof, source limits, and current dependency interfaces recorded; readiness ready.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-25.pages.json ex-aqft-scalar-eom-operator-reduction statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the supplied gauge datum. Use $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, $x^0=ct$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and $c=\\hbar=1$. Let $\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$ be an even real Lorentz-scalar of engineering weight one, a singlet of a fixed $SU(2)$ gauge datum on the trivial bundle with the product connection, and odd under the global group $\\mathbb Z_2=\\{1,-1\\}$ acting by $\\phi\\mapsto-\\phi$. The finite-jet module is the complexification of this real scalar field with conjugation fixing $\\phi$. For the finite-jet quotient use $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}=SO(\\eta,\\mathbb C)\\times SL(2,\\mathbb C)\\times\\mu_2$, where $SO(\\eta,\\mathbb C)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb C):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\det L=1\\}$ and $\\mu_2=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:z^2=1\\}$; the Lorentz factor acts on derivative indices, $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ acts trivially on $\\phi$, and $\\mu_2$ acts by sign. At the finite field-representation level, the physical group $SO^+(1,3)\\times SU(2)\\times\\mathbb Z_2$ maps to $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$ by $(L,u,\\sigma)\\mapsto(L,I_2,\\sigma)$, where $L$ is included as the same real matrix in $SO(\\eta,\\mathbb C)$, the gauge factor maps trivially, and $\\sigma=\\pm1$ is included as the same element of $\\mu_2$. Thus each scalar jet transforms by the Lorentz action on its derivative indices, trivially under $SU(2)$, and by sign under $\\mathbb Z_2$. Let $m\\ge0$ be a constant $\\mathbb Z_2$-invariant mass spurion of weight one, let $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}>0$ have weight one, and let $C\\in\\mathbb R$ be dimensionless. Define $\\Box=\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu\\partial_\\nu$ and adopt the base action\n\n$$S_0[\\phi]=-\\frac12\\int_M\\bigl(\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi+m^2\\phi^2\\bigr)\\,d^4x$$\n\nand add the dimension-six correction\n\n$$S_6[\\phi]=\\frac{C}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^2}\\int_M\\phi^2\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi\\,d^4x.$$\n\nFor compactly supported $h$, define the leading Euler derivative by $DS_0[\\phi](h)=\\int_Mh\\mathcal E_0(\\phi)\\,d^4x$; the calculation below verifies $\\mathcal E_0(\\phi)=(\\Box-m^2)\\phi$. Take derivative cutoff $r=2$, maximum field degree $q=4$, retained engineering dimension $D_{\\max}=6$, and loop order $L_{\\max}=0$. Let the exact declared dimension-six relation subspace be the $G_{\\mathrm{alg}}$-invariant span of $\\partial_\\mu(\\phi^3\\partial^\\mu\\phi)$ and $\\phi^3\\mathcal E_0(\\phi)$. All fields and variations have compact support, so the displayed integrations by parts have no boundary term. Retain terms through formal order $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^{-2}$, with $O(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^{-4})$ denoting terms at least quadratic in the formal bookkeeping parameter $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^{-2}$, not a numerical error bound. Then $\\phi^2\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi$ represents $-\\frac{m^2}{3}\\phi^4$ in this quotient, and the local field substitution $\\phi_{\\mathrm{old}}=\\phi+\\frac{C}{3\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^2}\\phi^3$ changes $S_0+S_6$ to $S_0-\\frac{Cm^2}{3\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^2}\\int_M\\phi^4\\,d^4x+O(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}^{-4})$. This is an action-level statement at the retained order only.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; complete physical example, conditional proof, source limits, and current dependency interfaces recorded; readiness ready.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-26.pages.json def-aqft-threshold-matching-map statement= 'Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and Minkowski signature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$. Fix a reference renormalization scale $\\mu_0>0$ and a common open interval $J\\subset(0,\\infty)$ of renormalization scales $\\mu$, with $\\mu_0\\in J$. Write $t=\\log(\\mu/\\mu_0)$, so $t$ is dimensionless. A threshold datum consists of a high-energy description, denoted $+$, and a lower-energy effective description, denoted $-$. The descriptions have a common declared light-field sector and differ by the degrees of freedom retained across a specified threshold of mass $M>0$. The external-energy domain and the range of validity of the local expansion are supplied separately; in a heavy-particle expansion they ordinarily require external energy $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}<M$. The Wilsonian matching scale $\\mu_W$, the renormalization scale $\\mu$, the threshold mass $M$, the external-energy bound $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, and the regulator cutoff $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ (denoted $\\Lambda$ in the finite-regulator model data) have mass dimension one and are distinct data; no equality between $\\mu_W$ and $\\mu$ is part of this definition.\n\nFor each sign $\\sigma\\in\\{+,-\\}$, supply a finite coefficient-coordinate space $\\mathbb C^{N_\\sigma}$ with $N_\\sigma\\ge1$, a declared ordered operator basis and power-counting truncation, and a renormalization-scheme coordinate map. Any identification of the Wilsonian coefficient data at $\\mu_W$ with these scheme coordinates is supplied input; this definition does not identify $\\mu_W$ with $\\mu$. The vector $C_\\sigma(t)\\in\\mathbb C^{N_\\sigma}$ is the column of dimensionless Wilson coefficients in that basis. The corresponding finite-order operator expression uses the supplied convention $C_{\\sigma,i}O_{\\sigma,i}/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op},\\sigma}^{d_i-4}$, with each $O_{\\sigma,i}$ of engineering dimension $d_i$ and $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op},\\sigma}>0$ the coefficient-suppression scale for that theory, denoted $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}$ in its physical operator contract and $\\Lambda$ in the generic scheme convention. It is distinct from $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{reg}}$, $M$, $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, $\\mu_W$, and $\\mu$ unless additional model data identify some of them. Write $\\rho_{N_\\sigma}$ for the realification that lists the real and imaginary parts of each coefficient. A supplied RG vector field is a continuous map $\\beta_\\sigma:\\Omega_\\sigma\\to\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$ on a supplied open domain $\\Omega_\\sigma\\subset J\\times\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$; an RG trajectory satisfies\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\rho_{N_\\sigma}(C_\\sigma(t))=\\beta_\\sigma(t,\\rho_{N_\\sigma}(C_\\sigma(t))).$$\nFor each sign, the physical operator datum is an instance of the selected physical EFT operator contract. It specifies finitely many field species and jet generators, finite-dimensional graded field spaces, smooth field domains and regularity, reality data, and rational engineering weights. Even fields are smooth maps; odd components take values in a fixed Grassmann algebra with smooth coefficient functions.\n\nThe Lorentz action is that of the proper orthochronous group\n$SO^+(1,3)=\\{L\\in GL_4(\\mathbb R):L^T\\eta L=\\eta,\\ \\det L=1,\\ L^0{}_0\\ge1\\}$,\nand each Lagrangian density is a Lorentz scalar. The datum gives the full gauge\nand flavor actions on fields, indices, and spurions. A spurion is a fixed\nnon-dynamical parameter used to record a declared symmetry-breaking insertion;\nabsent flavor symmetry is recorded as trivial, and broken symmetry requires\nits spurions and weights.\n\nThe datum fixes finite derivative, field-degree, dimension, and loop cutoffs,\nthe relation subspace and representatives, and a complex reductive affine\nalgebraic group with maps relating its representations to the physical gauge\nand flavor groups. Here reductive means there is no nontrivial closed normal\nunipotent subgroup; unipotent means every nonzero finite-dimensional rational\nmodule has a nonzero fixed vector. On each retained jet space the data either\nverify that the algebraic invariant subspace equals the intended physical\ninvariant space or label the quotient as a formal algebraic selection only.\n\nEach relation generator is recorded as a polynomial or Fierz identity, a total\ndivergence with compact-support or stated boundary conditions, or a\nleading-equation/field-redefinition relation tied to a base action and retained\norder. The latter records its variational derivative, multiplier, inverse\nthrough the retained order, and induced higher-order terms. This quotient alone\ndoes not establish action, off-shell Green-function, quantum-measure, or\nscattering equivalence.\n\nIf a non-Abelian gauge sector is selected, its actual compact real global group\n(compact meaning every open cover has a finite subcover), smooth right\nprincipal bundle (with smooth free and transitive right action and equivariant\nlocal trivializations), admitted bundle sectors, and unitary representations\n(actions preserving specified positive-definite Hermitian forms) are part of\nthe data. A selected smooth principal connection uses the right-action\nconvention $Z_P(p):=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_0p\\exp_G(tZ)$,\n$\\omega_p(Z_P(p))=Z$, and $R_g(p)=pg$,\n$R_g^*\\omega=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\\omega$, where\n$\\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e$ and $C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}$. Locally, for\n$A=s^*\\omega$ and a representation $\\rho$ with $\\rho_*:=d\\rho_e$, its\ncurvature and associated covariant derivative are\n$F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$ and\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+\\rho_*(A_\\mu)$, using the supplied graded Lie-bracket\nconvention. Each finite basis represents only this supplied fixed-order\nquotient; no completeness claim about all physical relations or an infinite\noperator tower is made. These field and counting inputs are not inferred from\ncoefficient coordinates.\nFor $x\\in\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$, write $\\Phi_\\sigma(t,s)x\\in\\mathbb R^{2N_\\sigma}$ for the value at $t$ of the unique realified coefficient solution with value $x$ at $s$, whenever a unique such solution exists on the interval between $s$ and $t$.\n\nA declared matching calculation supplies a finite list of renormalized amplitudes or Green-function values $A_{\\sigma,j}(t,c;\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}})$, where $j$ indexes the selected external states and kinematics, $c$ is a coefficient vector, and $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}}$ denotes the common infrared regulator used in both descriptions. Let $U_\\sigma\\subset\\mathbb C^{N_\\sigma}$ be open coefficient domains for $\\sigma\\in\\{+,-\\}$. The supplied matching family is a jointly continuously differentiable real map $\\widehat{\\mathcal M}:\\mathcal V\\to\\rho_{N_-}(U_-)$ on an open domain $\\mathcal V\\subset J\\times\\rho_{N_+}(U_+)$. Its admissible domain is the open subset $\\mathcal U=\\{(t,x)\\in\\mathcal V:(t,x)\\in\\Omega_+,\\ (t,\\widehat{\\mathcal M}(t,x))\\in\\Omega_-\\}$. It defines $M_t(c):=\\rho_{N_-}^{-1}(\\widehat{\\mathcal M}(t,\\rho_{N_+}(c)))$ for each admitted $(t,c)$, and $\\widehat M_t(x):=\\widehat{\\mathcal M}(t,x)$ on the corresponding slice. Thus the scale derivative and coefficient derivative in the compatibility condition below both exist and are continuous. It is a matching map through the declared loop order, operator dimension, and mass-expansion order when, for every selected $j$ and every admitted pair $(t,c)$ with $c\\in U_+$, the prescribed truncations of $A_{+,j}(t,c;\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}})$ and $A_{-,j}(t,M_t(c);\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IR}})$ agree. The truncation projection and its retained orders are part of the datum. This equality records only the selected amplitudes and orders; it neither estimates discarded terms nor asserts that a matching calculation exists for every model.\n\nA matching map is RG-compatible on these domains when, in realified coefficient coordinates,\n$$\\partial_t\\widehat M_t(x)+D_x\\widehat M_t(x)\\,\\beta_+(t,x)=\\beta_-\\!\\left(t,\\widehat M_t(x)\\right)$$\nfor every admitted $(t,x)$. Here $D_x$ is the ordinary real derivative with respect to the coefficient coordinates. RG-compatibility is an additional condition to be checked from the supplied beta functions and matching calculation; it does not follow merely from defining $M_t$ by amplitude equality.\n\nFor several thresholds, let $t_1>\\cdots>t_k$ be their logarithmic scales, let $\\Phi_j$ be the realified evolution in the theory active between thresholds $t_j$ and $t_{j+1}$, and let $\\widehat M_j(t_j)$ map realified coefficient data from the theory above threshold $j$ to the theory below it. The ordered high-to-low transport is\n$$\\Phi_k(t_f,t_k)\\circ\\widehat M_k(t_k)\\circ\\Phi_{k-1}(t_k,t_{k-1})\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\widehat M_1(t_1)\\circ\\Phi_0(t_1,t_i),$$\nwhenever all displayed flows and matching maps are defined. This definition specifies supplied finite-order data and their conventions; it computes no beta-function entry or matching coefficient and asserts no continuum limit.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-26.pages.json pthm-aqft-matching-and-running-scale-independence statement= 'Let $K=[t_i,t_f]$ be a compact interval contained in the common open logarithmic-scale domains of a finite-order high-energy EFT and its lower-energy EFT. Use the realified coefficient coordinates, RG vector fields $\\beta_+$ and $\\beta_-$, flow maps $\\Phi_\\pm$, and realified matching map $\\widehat M_t$ of the finite-order threshold-matching definition. Assume each beta field is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the coefficient coordinates, locally uniformly in scale, and assume every flow used below exists on $K$. Assume the high-energy trajectory remains in the matching-family domain throughout $K$ and its matched image lies in the low-energy beta-field domain. Assume the matching family is jointly continuously differentiable in scale and coefficient coordinates and RG-compatible throughout these domains. For an initial high-energy realified coefficient vector $x_i$ with $(t_i,x_i)\\in\\Omega_+$ and any matching scale $s\\in K$, define the low-energy vector at the fixed final scale by\n$$x_-^{(s)}(t_f):=\\Phi_-\\!\\left(t_f,s\\right)\\!\\left(\\widehat M_s\\!\\left(\\Phi_+(s,t_i)x_i\\right)\\right).$$\nThen $x_-^{(s)}(t_f)$ is independent of $s$. Consequently, each selected renormalized low-energy amplitude or Green-function value, evaluated at the same final scale, external states, kinematics, infrared regulator, and operator truncation, is independent of the intermediate matching scale through the retained orders. The matching definition makes that low-energy amplitude agree with its selected high-energy counterpart through those same orders. The same conclusion holds for any finite ordered sequence of thresholds when each matching map satisfies the stated compatibility condition and the required flows exist. Contributions beyond the retained loop order, operator dimension, and mass expansion remain separate theoretical uncertainties; this result assigns them no numerical bounds. This is a conditional deduction from the supplied RG and matching data; it neither calculates beta functions or matching coefficients nor establishes a continuum QFT.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-26.pages.json ex-aqft-qed-threshold-matching statement= 'Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and Minkowski signature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-,+,+,+)$. Take QED with a real one-form photon potential $A=A_\\mu dx^\\mu$ on $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, field strength $F=dA$ with components $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, and two Dirac fields $\\psi_e,\\psi_\\mu$, each a four-component complex spinor-valued field, of charge $-e$ and positive masses $m_e<M_\\mu$. Use the minimal-coupling convention $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ieA_\\mu$ for charge $-e$, and the gauge kinetic term $-\\tfrac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}$. Define the dimensionless running coupling by $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)=e_{\\mathrm{DS}}(\\mu)^2/(4\\pi)$. The decoupling-subtraction (DS) convention uses minimal subtraction within each interval of active charged fields, removes a charged field from the low-energy theory below its threshold, and matches the coupling continuously at that threshold. Set $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(m_e)=\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}>0$ as the low-energy boundary condition. The source-reported one-loop DS flow has two active Dirac fields for $\\mu>M_\\mu$, one for $m_e<\\mu<M_\\mu$, and none for $\\mu<m_e$. If the one-loop solution remains in its positive-coupling domain, then for positive evaluation scale $Q$ of mass dimension one,\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{m_e^2}\\right)\\qquad(m_e<Q<M_\\mu),$$\nand\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{DS}}(Q)}=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{phys}}}-\\frac{1}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{M_\\mu^2}{m_e^2}\\right)-\\frac{2}{3\\pi}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{Q^2}{M_\\mu^2}\\right)\\qquad(Q>M_\\mu).$$\nThe two expressions agree continuously at $Q=M_\\mu$. These are solutions of the supplied one-loop renormalization-group equations; they are not measured values, exact all-orders predictions, or numerical error estimates.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; planned item contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-27.pages.json post-aqft-smeft-model statement= 'Adopt four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^0=ct$ and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ for engineering dimensions, so $\\partial_\\mu$ has mass dimension one, a scalar field has dimension one, a spinor field has dimension $3/2$, and a gauge field strength has dimension two.' design_state= 'Adopts the linearly realized three-family Standard Model field and gauge data and a finite B- and L-conserving canonical-dimension-six local model, with explicit scales and physical limits.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-27.pages.json post-aqft-heft-model statement= "Use the same four-dimensional Minkowski spacetime, direct-product global gauge group, gauge connection, fermion representations, family content, and the preceding model's exact baryon- and lepton-number selection rules on formal local interactions. No anomaly-free quantum-current claim is made. In particular, the hypercharge-one-half Higgs doublet of that model is replaced in this realization by three angular Goldstone coordinates and a real scalar singlet; this is a distinct adopted model interface, not a deduction that the two models have identical field spaces." design_state= 'Adopts the nonlinear SU(2)-valued Goldstone field and independent scalar singlet with the exact right hypercharge action and separate HEFT scales.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-27.pages.json def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract statement= 'Use the fields, direct-product gauge group, hypercharge normalization, three-family content, exact $U(1)_B\\times U(1)_L$ symmetries, natural units and coefficient scale of the preceding linearly realized model. A local operator is a finite polynomial in these fields and their covariant derivatives at one spacetime point, with all Lorentz, gauge and family indices contracted to a Lorentz and gauge scalar. Its canonical dimension assigns one to $H$ and each derivative, $3/2$ to a fermion, and two to a field strength.' design_state= 'Defines the source-listed B- and L-conserving dimension-six Warsaw coefficient representatives and their family and conjugation conventions without claiming a local classification proof.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-27.pages.json def-aqft-chiral-power-counting statement= 'Use the HEFT fields $U$, $h$, gauge curvatures, fermions and arbitrary smooth coefficient functions of $h/v$ from the preceding HEFT model. The canonical engineering dimension $\\Delta$ is the mass weight of the local polynomial in fields and derivatives; it is the weight supplied by the finite-jet operator definition. The **chiral/loop count** is separate data and is not inferred from $\\Delta$.' design_state= 'Defines the source-convention HEFT chiral and loop grading and finite NLO classes separately from canonical-dimension counting.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-27.pages.json pthm-aqft-smeft-as-restricted-heft-branch statement= 'Let $v>0$, let $J=(-v,\\infty)$, and use the linearly transforming complex doublet $H$ with hypercharge $Y=1/2$ and the nonlinear fields $h\\in J$, $U\\in SU(2)$, with the gauge transformations and mostly-plus Minkowski conventions of the two adopted electroweak models. Here $\\sigma^2=\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$ is the second Pauli matrix. Put $e_2=(0,1)^T$, $\\widetilde H=i\\sigma^2H^*$, and define' design_state= 'Proves the smooth equivariant nonzero-doublet patch map and the finite polynomial and coefficient-image restrictions of the selected SMEFT branch.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-27.pages.json ex-aqft-smeft-heft-observable-map statement= 'Use the selected mostly-plus, four-dimensional electroweak model with $v>0$, dimensionless gauge coupling $g>0$, and coefficient scale $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{op}}>0$. Choose a real dimensionless coefficient $C_{H\\Box}$, set all other dimension-six coefficients to zero, and retain' design_state= 'Computes the local Q_HBox scalar kinetic coefficient and charged-vector coupling under the stated positivity and boundary assumptions.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-28.pages.json def-aqft-eft-scattering-channel statement= "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ on four-dimensional Minkowski space $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Let $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$ be a real, Grassmann-even (commuting) scalar field of engineering dimension one, with a stable one-particle state of pole mass $m>0$; here stable means an isolated real mass pole with zero decay width. Stability and the existence of this state are physical input data; no construction of a state is asserted. An effective field theory (EFT) here is the selected local low-energy representative organized in powers of its cutoff; its exact amplitude is separate supplied model data. For the finite-jet local-operator convention, use the complexification for the finite-jet algebra and impose the usual reality condition on $\\phi$. Take one field species, maximum jet order $r=1$, polynomial degree at most $q=4$, weight $D=8$, trivial internal group, and the standard Lorentz tensor action with contractions formed using $\\eta$. The real operator $\\mathcal O_8=(\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi)^2$ is then a local scalar of engineering dimension eight. Its coefficient in a chosen local representative is written\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{EFT}}=-\\frac12\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi-\\frac12m^2\\phi^2+\\frac{C_8(\\mu_*)}{\\Lambda^4}\\mathcal O_8+\\cdots,$$\n\nwhere $\\Lambda>2m$ is the positive energy cutoff of the EFT description, $0<\\mu_*<\\Lambda$ is the chosen scale at which the coefficient and matching identity are specified, and $C_8(\\mu_*)\\in\\mathbb R$ is dimensionless in the fixed coefficient convention and local representative. No basis-independence under field redefinitions, equations of motion, or integration by parts is asserted for $C_8$ itself.\n\nThe amplitude data consist of an identical-scalar $2\\to2$ amplitude $\\mathcal A(s,t,u)$ and real on-shell momenta $p_j$ with $p_j^2=-m^2$, $p_1+p_2=p_3+p_4$, and\n\n$$s=-(p_1+p_2)^2,\\qquad t=-(p_1-p_3)^2,\\qquad u=-(p_1-p_4)^2,\\qquad s+t+u=4m^2.$$\n\nThe forward slice is $A(s)=\\mathcal A(s,0,4m^2-s)$ and assume $A(s)=A(4m^2-s)$ wherever both sides are defined. When the full $t$-dependent amplitude is supplied, use the partial-wave normalization\n\n$$\\mathcal A(s,t,u)=16\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{s}{s-4m^2}}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{\\infty}(2\\ell+1)P_\\ell\\!\\left(1+\\frac{2t}{s-4m^2}\\right)a_\\ell(s),\\qquad P_\\ell(1)=1.$$\n\nHere $\\ell=0,1,2,\\ldots$, $P_\\ell$ is the Legendre polynomial, and $a_\\ell(s)$ is the partial-wave coefficient. The formula is a normalization convention when a full amplitude is supplied; it does not assert convergence or unitarity by itself.\n\nThe $S$-matrix convention is $S=1+i(2\\pi)^4\\delta^4(p_{\\mathrm{out}}-p_{\\mathrm{in}})\\mathcal A+\\cdots$. The channel adopts the forward optical-theorem input $\\operatorname{Im}A(s+i0)\\ge0$ for $s>4m^2$ in this normalization. This sign is an assumption on the supplied amplitude data, not a deduction from the EFT truncation.\n\nPut $\\nu_0=2m^2$ and $v=s-2m^2$, so $u-2m^2=-v$. Let the specified finite list contain every isolated variable simple pole in the subthreshold interval, with real locations $p_j\\in(0,4m^2)\\setminus\\{2m^2\\}$ and real residues $r_j$. For the dimensionless amplitude convention above, $r_j$ has mass dimension two. Define the crossing-symmetric pole subtraction\n\n$$P(s)=\\sum_j\\left(\\frac{r_j}{s-p_j}+\\frac{r_j}{4m^2-p_j-s}\\right),\\qquad B(v)=A(2m^2+v)-P(2m^2+v).$$\n\nThe exact amplitude data are required to be crossing even and real analytic, with $B(-v)=B(v)$, $B(\\overline v)=\\overline{B(v)}$, and $B$ holomorphic on\n\n$$\\Omega_{\\nu_0}=\\mathbb C\\setminus\\bigl(( -\\infty,-\\nu_0]\\cup[\\nu_0,\\infty)\\bigr).$$\n\nThere is no massless pole or cut at the forward point $t=0$. Require finite upper and lower cut-boundary values except at $\\pm\\nu_0$. Also require $B$ to extend continuously from each open half-plane onto every compact segment of either real cut bank avoiding $\\pm\\nu_0$. This is an extra admissibility assumption on the supplied data, not a consequence of finite pointwise boundary values or a claim about every quantum field theory. Require the uniform high-energy condition $B(w)=o(|w|^2)$ as $|w|\\to\\infty$ in the closed upper half-plane including upper cut-bank limits, and vanishing endpoint arcs for the cut integrals. For $\\nu>\\nu_0$, let $\\rho(\\nu)=\\operatorname{Im}B(\\nu+i0)$ and assign $\\rho(\\nu_0)=0$ by convention, without asserting a threshold boundary limit; changing this one endpoint value changes no Riemann integral; require $\\rho\\ge0$, local Riemann integrability on every compact subinterval of $[\\nu_0,\\infty)$, and finite convergence of $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-3}\\,d\\nu$. For every real $|v|<\\nu_0$, the upper- and lower-bank integrals of $[B(x)-B(0)]/[x^2(x-v)]$ are required to exist as finite improper Riemann limits. The positive gap $m>0$ and these analytic and spectral assumptions are IR and UV input conditions; they are not derived from a finite regulator.\n\nDefine the first nonconstant even forward coefficient by $c_1=B''(0)/2!$, of mass dimension $-4$. Exact matching at $\\mu_*$ is a supplied datum of the form\n\n$$c_1=\\frac{4C_8(\\mu_*)}{\\Lambda^4}+\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}},$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}\\in\\mathbb R$ has mass dimension $-4$ and contains every other contribution to this exact $v^2$ coefficient, including other operators, loops, and matching or truncation effects. The identity is an assumption to be checked for a selected model, not a conclusion of this definition. Setting $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}=0$ is an additional exact retained-order matching condition; power counting alone does not set it to zero." design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; exact assigned contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-28.pages.json pthm-aqft-forward-positivity-wilson-coefficient statement= "Let $m>0$, $\\Lambda>2m$, and $0<\\mu_*<\\Lambda$. Use natural units and the four-dimensional Minkowski metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Let $\\phi$ be a real scalar of engineering dimension one with a stable one-particle pole of mass $m$, where stable means an isolated real mass pole with zero decay width. In the convention $S=1+i(2\\pi)^4\\delta^4(p_{\\mathrm{out}}-p_{\\mathrm{in}})\\mathcal A+\\cdots$, let $A(s)=\\mathcal A(s,0,4m^2-s)$ be the forward amplitude for identical on-shell external momenta $p_j^2=-m^2$, $p_1+p_2=p_3+p_4$, with $s=-(p_1+p_2)^2$, $t=-(p_1-p_3)^2$, $u=-(p_1-p_4)^2$, $s+t+u=4m^2$, and crossing $A(s)=A(4m^2-s)$. For a finite crossing-paired sum containing every stable variable simple pole in the open subthreshold interval\n\n$$P(s)=\\sum_j\\left(\\frac{r_j}{s-p_j}+\\frac{r_j}{4m^2-p_j-s}\\right),\\qquad r_j\\in\\mathbb R,\\quad p_j\\in(0,4m^2)\\setminus\\{2m^2\\},$$\n\nPut $\\nu_0=2m^2$, $v=s-2m^2$, and $B(v)=A(2m^2+v)-P(2m^2+v)$. Require $B(-v)=B(v)$, $B(\\overline v)=\\overline{B(v)}$, and holomorphy on $\\mathbb C\\setminus(( -\\infty,-\\nu_0]\\cup[\\nu_0,\\infty))$. Require finite upper and lower cut-boundary values except at $\\pm\\nu_0$, and require $B$ to extend continuously from each open half-plane onto every compact cut-bank segment avoiding $\\pm\\nu_0$. This compact-bank continuity is an explicit admissibility input. Also require vanishing endpoint arcs and $B(w)=o(|w|^2)$ uniformly as $|w|\\to\\infty$ in the closed upper half-plane including upper cut-bank limits. Let $\\rho(\\nu)=\\operatorname{Im}B(\\nu+i0)$ for $\\nu>\\nu_0$, extended by $\\rho(\\nu_0)=0$ without asserting a threshold boundary limit. Require $\\rho$ to be nonnegative and Riemann integrable on every compact subinterval of $[\\nu_0,\\infty)$, with finite $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-3}\\,d\\nu$. For every real $|v|<\\nu_0$, require the upper/lower boundary-bank integrals of $[B(x)-B(0)]/[x^2(x-v)]$ to exist as finite improper Riemann limits. Assume no massless pole or cut reaches $t=0$. Define $c_1=B''(0)/2!$.\n\nAt the fixed matching scale $\\mu_*$, suppose the selected local representative contains the real dimension-eight interaction $C_8(\\mu_*)(\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi)^2/\\Lambda^4$, with $C_8(\\mu_*)$ dimensionless, and the exact matched coefficient identity is $c_1=4C_8(\\mu_*)/\\Lambda^4+\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}$, where $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}$ has mass dimension $-4$ and includes every other contribution to the exact $v^2$ coefficient. Then\n\n$$C_8(\\mu_*)+\\frac{\\Lambda^4}{4}\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}\\ge0.$$\n\nIf the exact retained-order matching condition additionally gives $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}=0$, then $C_8(\\mu_*)\\ge0$. The result is only a necessary bound on this specified matched channel combination; it asserts neither that a full amplitude exists for every coefficient passing the bound nor that every off-shell operator coefficient has a universal sign." design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; exact assigned contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-28.pages.json ex-aqft-eft-positivity-application statement= "Fix one hypothetical channel satisfying all amplitude hypotheses in this page's channel definition and the exact matching identity $c_1=4C_8/\\Lambda^4+\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}$. Take $\\Lambda=4m$ and $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{match}}=0$. Compare $C_8=+1/2$ with $C_8=-1/2$ under these same assumptions. The first coefficient passes the necessary forward-positivity inequality; the second is excluded by it. Passing the inequality is not sufficient for a full amplitude." design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; exact assigned contract; readiness recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-29.pages.json pthm-qftx-n2-bps-saturation-kernel-equivalence statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Use the four-dimensional rigid N=2 super-Poincare convention with metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\epsilon_{12}=1$, and natural units $\\hbar=c=1$. Let $\\mathcal K\\ne\\{0\\}$ be a positive Hilbert rest-fiber representation with $P^\\mu=(M,0,0,0)$, $M>0$, scalar central charges $Z^{12}=zI$ and $Z^{21}=-zI$, and a common dense invariant domain $\\mathcal D$ on which the supercharges, their actual Hilbert adjoints, and every product below are defined. Thus $P_0=-M$, each supercharge has mass dimension one-half, and $M,z$ have mass dimension one. Choose $\\phi\\in\\mathbb R$ so that $e^{-i\\phi}z=|z|$, with arbitrary $\\phi$ when $z=0$, and on $\\mathcal D$ put\n\n$$q=Q^1_1,\\qquad r=Q^2_2,\\qquad A=q-e^{i\\phi}r^*,\\qquad A^\\sharp=q^*-e^{-i\\phi}r.$$\n\nFor every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$,\n\n$$\\langle(AA^\\sharp+A^\\sharp A)\\psi,\\psi\\rangle=\\|A\\psi\\|^2+\\|A^\\sharp\\psi\\|^2=4(M-|z|)\\|\\psi\\|^2.$$\n\nConsequently, for every nonzero $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$, $M=|z|$ if and only if $A\\psi=A^\\sharp\\psi=0$. The operator $A^\\sharp$ is the displayed sum of the individual charge adjoints; for unbounded charges it is not asserted to be the maximal-domain adjoint of the sum $A$. This conditional statement neither asserts that such a vector or representation occurs in a physical model nor proves its dynamical stability.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft with complete conditional argument or definition; current prerequisite interfaces and page inventories reconciled in the owner resolution evidence. Root records readiness separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-29.pages.json def-qftx-bps-state-and-saturation statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and use natural units $\\hbar=c=1$. Let $N\\ge2$, and let a selected four-dimensional rigid super-Poincare representation have a nonzero Hilbert space with positive inner product, future-cone joint momentum spectrum, and closed supercharges with actual Hilbert adjoints on the common invariant product domain specified here. A **massive rest fiber** is a nonzero Hilbert space $\\mathcal K$ carrying the restricted supercharge and central-charge operators on a common dense invariant domain $\\mathcal D$, with $P^\\mu=(M,0,0,0)$, $M>0$, and scalar central-charge matrix $Z=(z^{IJ})$ acting by $Z^{IJ}=z^{IJ}I$, where $z^{IJ}=-z^{JI}$. Use metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, so $P_0=-M$, and the convention $\\epsilon_{12}=1$ in the supercharge algebra.\n\nFor $v\\in\\mathbb C^N$, set $\\|v\\|_2^2=\\sum_I|v_I|^2$ and define $\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=\\sup_{\\|v\\|_2=1}\\|Zv\\|_2$. The unitary massive-fiber bound is $M\\ge\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$. A **BPS-saturated state** in this item is a nonzero vector $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$ in a specified massive rest fiber for which $M=\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$. This term records algebraic saturation only; it does not assert that any specified charge is represented by a state, or that a saturating state exists, is stable, or occurs in a physical spectrum.\n\nFor $N=2$, write $z=z^{12}$, so $Z=\\begin{pmatrix}0&z\\\\-z&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=|z|$. Choose $\\phi$ such that $e^{-i\\phi}z=|z|$ and define $A=Q^1_1-e^{i\\phi}(Q^2_2)^*$ and $A^\\sharp=(Q^1_1)^*-e^{-i\\phi}Q^2_2$ on $\\mathcal D$. In this $N=2$ case, a nonzero domain vector saturates the bound if and only if both displayed combinations annihilate it. For unbounded charges, $A^\\sharp$ denotes the displayed sum of individual adjoints and is not assumed to be the maximal-domain adjoint of $A$.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft with complete conditional argument or definition; current prerequisite interfaces and page inventories reconciled in the owner resolution evidence. Root records readiness separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-29.pages.json ex-qftx-n2-short-rest-fiber-algebra statement= 'Let $M>0$ and let $\\mathcal K$ have orthonormal basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle,|2\\rangle,|12\\rangle$. Give $\\mathcal K$ the grading for which $|0\\rangle,|12\\rangle$ are even and $|1\\rangle,|2\\rangle$ are odd. Define operators $c_1,c_2$ by\n\n$$c_1|0\\rangle=0,\\quad c_1|1\\rangle=|0\\rangle,\\quad c_1|2\\rangle=0,\\quad c_1|12\\rangle=|2\\rangle,$$\n\n$$c_2|0\\rangle=0,\\quad c_2|1\\rangle=0,\\quad c_2|2\\rangle=|0\\rangle,\\quad c_2|12\\rangle=-|1\\rangle,$$\n\nand let $c_1^\\dagger,c_2^\\dagger$ be their actual Hilbert adjoints. Set\n\n$$Q^1_1=\\sqrt{2M}\\,c_1,\\quad Q^2_2=\\sqrt{2M}\\,c_1^\\dagger,\\quad Q^1_2=\\sqrt{2M}\\,c_2,\\quad Q^2_1=-\\sqrt{2M}\\,c_2^\\dagger.$$\n\nTake $P^\\mu=(M,0,0,0)$, $Z^{12}=M I$, $Z^{21}=-M I$, $Z^{11}=Z^{22}=0$, and $\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I}=(Q^I_\\alpha)^\\dagger$. On $\\mathcal D=\\mathcal K$ these matrices satisfy the massive N=2 rest-frame supercharge relations with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\epsilon_{12}=1$. The central-charge bound is saturated and the corresponding combination $A=Q^1_1-(Q^2_2)^\\dagger$ vanishes identically. This is a finite-dimensional module for the rest-frame supercharge algebra; no extension to the full Poincare group or physical particle is asserted.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft with complete conditional argument or definition; current prerequisite interfaces and page inventories reconciled in the owner resolution evidence. Root records readiness separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-29.pages.json ex-qftx-n2-long-rest-fiber-algebra statement= 'Let $M>0$ and let $\\mathcal K=\\Lambda\\mathbb C^4$ with orthonormal basis $|S\\rangle$ indexed by subsets $S\\subseteq\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ and grading $(-1)^{|S|}$. For $j\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ define $c_j|S\\rangle=0$ when $j\\notin S$ and\n$c_j|S\\rangle=(-1)^{|\\{s\\in S:s<j\\}|}|S\\setminus\\{j\\}\\rangle$ when $j\\in S$. Let $c_j^\\dagger$ be the actual Hilbert adjoint. For $I,\\alpha\\in\\{1,2\\}$ put $j=2(I-1)+\\alpha$ and set $Q^I_\\alpha=\\sqrt{2M}\\,c_j$, with $\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I}=(Q^I_\\alpha)^\\dagger$. Take $P^\\mu=(M,0,0,0)$ and $Z^{IJ}=0$. On $\\mathcal D=\\mathcal K$, these operators satisfy the massive N=2 rest-frame supercharge relations with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\epsilon_{12}=1$. Since $\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=0<M$, the bound is strict and no nonzero vector in this fiber is BPS-saturated. This finite module is a representation of the rest-frame supercharge algebra only; it is not asserted to extend to a full Poincare representation or a physical particle.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft with complete conditional argument or definition; current prerequisite interfaces and page inventories reconciled in the owner resolution evidence. Root records readiness separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.pages.json def-qftx-gauge-slice-and-faddeev-popov-operator statement= "Assume the axiom of countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. Fix a compact noncommutative finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, whose Lie algebra is $\\mathfrak g=T_eG$ with bracket transported from left-invariant vector fields. Let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal bundle: the right action is smooth, free and transitive on each fibre, and each point has a local equivariant principal chart. Let $H\\subset TP$ be a supplied smooth right-invariant horizontal complement to $VP=\\ker(d\\pi)$, and write $v$ for projection onto $VP$ along $H$. For $Z\\in\\mathfrak g$, the fundamental vertical vector is $Z_P(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}p\\exp_G(tZ)$. The principal connection form $\\omega$ is a smooth $\\mathfrak g$-valued one-form satisfying $\\omega(Z_P)=Z$, $\\ker\\omega=H$, and $R_g^*\\omega=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\\omega$, where $\\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e$ and $C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}$. In a local section $s:U\\to P$ (a smooth map with $\\pi\\circ s=\\operatorname{id}_U$), the potential $A=s^*\\omega$ changes under a section change $s'=su$, for smooth $u:U\\to G$, by $A'=\\operatorname{Ad}_{u^{-1}}A+\\theta_L(u)$, where $\\theta_L(u)=u^*\\theta_L$, $(\\theta_L)_g=d(L_{g^{-1}})_g$, and $L_g(h)=gh$. The full gauge group consists of smooth diffeomorphisms $\\Phi:P\\to P$ satisfying $\\pi\\circ\\Phi=\\pi$ and $\\Phi(pg)=\\Phi(p)g$; this definition gives it no finite-dimensional Lie-group structure.\n\nA **finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice datum** consists of integers $1\\le k\\le n$; a finite-dimensional Lie group $K$ of dimension $k$, with Lie algebra $\\mathfrak k:=T_eK$ and bracket transported from left-invariant vector fields; a chosen smooth positive left-invariant density $\\mu_K$, meaning $(L_h^K)^*\\mu_K=\\mu_K$ for $L_h^K(g)=hg$; a finite-dimensional smooth manifold $X$ of dimension $n$ with a chosen smooth positive density $\\mu_X$; and a smooth right action $\\alpha:X\\times K\\to X$, written $x\\cdot g$, with $x\\cdot e=x$ and $(x\\cdot g)\\cdot h=x\\cdot(gh)$. A smooth positive density on an $m$-manifold has coordinate form $\\rho(x)|dx^1\\cdots dx^m|$ with smooth $\\rho>0$, and transforms by the absolute coordinate Jacobian. The group $K$ and its action on $X$ are separate regulator data. They are not identified with the full gauge group or the full space of connections unless a particular finite model explicitly constructs such an action and verifies that it preserves $X$.\n\nChoose an embedded smooth submanifold $S\\subset X$ of codimension $k$, a smooth positive density $\\mu_S$, and an open neighborhood $U\\subset X$ of a point $s\\in S$. Embedded means that near each point of $S$, a smooth chart on $X$ identifies $S$ with $\\mathbb R^{n-k}\\times\\{0\\}$. A **gauge condition** on $U$ is a smooth map $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R^k$ such that $U\\cap S=\\chi^{-1}(0)$ and, for every $s'\\in U\\cap S$, $d\\chi_{s'}$ is surjective with kernel $T_{s'}S$. For every $x\\in U$, the **infinitesimal orbit map** is $R_x:=d(\\alpha_x)_e:\\mathfrak k\\to T_xX$, where $\\alpha_x(g)=x\\cdot g$; call its kernel the infinitesimal stabilizer at $x$. The **Faddeev–Popov operator** at $x$ is the ordinary linear map $M_x:=d\\chi_x\\circ R_x:\\mathfrak k\\to\\mathbb R^k$. At a slice point $s'\\in U\\cap S$, call the condition **transverse** when $M_{s'}$ is an isomorphism. Choose a positive density $\\nu$ on $\\mathfrak k$ equal to $(\\mu_K)_e$, and the standard positive density $\\lambda$ on $\\mathbb R^k$. For every $x\\in U$, the finite-dimensional Faddeev–Popov Jacobian $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(x)$ is the nonnegative density ratio defined by $M_x^*\\lambda=\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(x)\\nu$; in chosen bases it is $|\\det M_x|$, and it is zero when $M_x$ is singular.\n\nThis datum defines only finite-dimensional Jacobians and local coordinate slices. It neither defines a determinant of an infinite-dimensional operator nor constructs a functional measure or a continuum path integral. A finite-mode or lattice application must supply the regulator manifold $X$, the finite-dimensional group $K$, their smooth action, and the domains and densities above." design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; item readiness is recorded separately. The planned Singer theorem has no carrier and remains explicitly escalated in batch evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.pages.json thm-qftx-local-faddeev-popov-slice-jacobian statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and let $(X,K,\\alpha,S,\\chi,\\mu_X,\\mu_K,\\mu_S)$ be finite-dimensional regulated gauge-slice data as defined above, with $\\dim X=n$, $\\dim K=k$, $1\\le k\\le n$, and $s_0\\in S\\cap U$. Thus $K$ acts smoothly on the right, $S$ is an embedded codimension-$k$ submanifold, $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R^k$ is a smooth defining submersion with $\\ker d\\chi_s=T_sS$, and the Faddeev–Popov operator is $M_s=d\\chi_s\\circ R_s$. Suppose $M_{s_0}$ is an isomorphism. For $\\Psi(g,s)=s\\cdot g$, there are neighborhoods $V$ of the identity in $K$, $W$ of $s_0$ in $S$, and $O$ of $s_0$ in $X$ such that $\\Psi:V\\times W\\to O$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism. Consequently, each orbit has exactly one representative in $W$ among the representatives reached using $V$.\n\nFix group coordinates $\\xi$ near the identity, slice coordinates $z$ near $s_0$, and adapted target coordinates $(y,z)=(\\chi,z)$ near $s_0$. Write the densities there as $\\mu_K=\\rho_K(\\xi)|d\\xi|$, $\\mu_S=\\rho_S(z)|dz|$, and $\\mu_X=\\rho_X(y,z)|dy\\,dz|$. The positive Jacobian $J_\\Psi$ defined by $\\Psi^*\\mu_X=J_\\Psi(\\mu_K\\otimes\\mu_S)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nJ_\\Psi(e,s_0)=\\frac{\\rho_X(0,z_0)}{\\rho_S(z_0)}\\,\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(s_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}$ uses the density $(\\mu_K)_e$ and the standard density on $\\mathbb R^k$. Thus, when the target and slice densities are normalized so that $\\rho_X(0,z_0)=\\rho_S(z_0)$, the ordinary finite-dimensional orbit-to-slice Jacobian is exactly $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(s_0)$. For every compactly supported Riemann-integrable function $f:O\\to\\mathbb R$, the local change-of-variables identity is\n$$\n\\int_O f\\,d\\mu_X=\\int_{V\\times W}(f\\circ\\Psi)J_\\Psi\\,d\\mu_K\\,d\\mu_S.\n$$\n\nFor the exact multiplicity statement, fix $x\\in X$ and a relatively compact open set $\\Gamma\\subset K$ such that $x\\cdot\\overline{\\Gamma}\\subset U$. Let $F_x(g)=\\chi(x\\cdot g)$. Let $r\\ge 0$ be finite, and suppose $F_x$ has exactly $r$ zeros $g_1,\\ldots,g_r$ in the support of a smooth function $\\varphi$ compactly supported in $\\Gamma$, each $M_{x\\cdot g_i}$ is invertible, and the slice points $s_i=x\\cdot g_i$ are distinct. Choose a nonnegative $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^k)$, supported in a ball $B_C(0)$ of finite radius $C>0$, with integral $1$, and set $\\eta_\\epsilon(y)=\\epsilon^{-k}\\eta(y/\\epsilon)$. Then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\int_\\Gamma \\varphi(g)\\,\\eta_\\epsilon(F_x(g))\\,\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(x\\cdot g)\\,d\\mu_K(g)=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\varphi(g_i).\n$$\n\nIn particular, if $\\varphi=1$ near the zeros, this finite-regulator expression counts the $r$ distinct transverse intersections exactly. The theorem asserts only a local chart and this finite-dimensional multiplicity formula; it gives no global gauge section, functional determinant, or continuum path-integral measure.' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; item readiness is recorded separately. The planned Singer theorem has no carrier and remains explicitly escalated in batch evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.pages.json ex-qftx-translation-gauge-slice-jacobian statement= 'Take $M=\\{\\star\\}$, $G=SU(2)$ as the unit quaternions, $P=SU(2)\\to M$ with right multiplication, $H=0$, and the connection form $\\omega=q^{-1}dq$. This is an admissible non-Abelian bundle and connection datum. Choose the independent regulator $X=\\mathbb R^2$ with coordinates $(u,v)$ and density $du\\,dv$, the additive Lie group $K=(\\mathbb R,+)$ with left-invariant density $dt$, and the smooth right action\n$$\n(u,v)\\cdot t=(u+t,v).\n$$\nLet $S=\\{(0,v):v\\in\\mathbb R\\}$, let $\\chi(u,v)=u$, and give $S$ density $dv$. For every $s=(0,v)\\in S$, the Faddeev–Popov operator is $M_s(t)=t$, so $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(s)=1$. The local orbit-to-slice map is $\\Psi(t,(0,v))=(t,v)$, with density Jacobian $1$. For $x=(a,b)$, the gauge condition along its orbit is $F_x(t)=a+t$; it has one transverse zero $t_0=-a$, and the slice meets this orbit once. If $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb R$ is relatively compact and open with $t_0\\in\\Gamma$, and $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Gamma)$ has $t_0\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi$, then for every nonnegative compactly supported smooth $\\eta$ of integral $1$, the regularized intersection expression tends to $\\varphi(t_0)$.' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; item readiness is recorded separately. The planned Singer theorem has no carrier and remains explicitly escalated in batch evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.pages.json ex-qftx-so2-gauge-slice-multiplicity statement= "Take $M=\\{\\star\\}$, $G=SU(2)$ as the noncommutative group of unit quaternions, $P=SU(2)\\to M$ with right multiplication, $H=0$, and connection form $\\omega=q^{-1}dq$. This is an admissible non-Abelian bundle and connection datum. Choose the independent regulator $X=\\mathbb R^2$ with Lebesgue density. Write $R_\\theta\\in SO(2)$ for rotation through angle $\\theta$ modulo $2\\pi$. Let $K=SO(2)$ with left-invariant density $|d\\theta|$, and define the smooth right action\n$$\n(x,y)\\cdot R_\\theta=(x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta,\\,-x\\sin\\theta+y\\cos\\theta).\n$$\nTake $S=\\{(x,0):x\\in\\mathbb R\\}$, $\\chi(x,y)=y$, and density $dx$ on $S$. At $s=(r,0)$, the orbit differential in the angle direction is $(0,-r)$, so $M_s(1)=-r$ and $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}(s)=|r|$. For $r>0$, the orbit-to-slice map is $\\Psi(R_\\theta,(r,0))=(r\\cos\\theta,-r\\sin\\theta)$, whose coordinate Jacobian at the identity is $r$, agreeing with the Faddeev–Popov density ratio. For a fixed orbit point $x_0=(r,0)$, $r>0$, the gauge function is $F_{x_0}(R_\\theta)=-r\\sin\\theta$. Its two zeros are $\\theta=0,\\pi$, with distinct slice points $(r,0)$ and $(-r,0)$, and both are transverse. Taking $\\Gamma=SO(2)$ and the test function $\\varphi=1$, the regularized intersection expression tends to $2$. At the origin the full group stabilizes the point, $M_{(0,0)}=0$, and $F_{(0,0)}$ vanishes on all of $SO(2)$; the theorem's isolated transverse-zero hypotheses fail there." design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; item readiness is recorded separately. The planned Singer theorem has no carrier and remains explicitly escalated in batch evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json def-qftx-n1-kahler-target-data statement= 'Let $n$ be a positive integer. Give $\\mathbb C^n$ the Euclidean topology transported from $\\mathbb R^{2n}$ by the real coordinates $z^i=x^i+i y^i$, where $x^i,y^i\\in\\mathbb R$. Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$ be open in this topology and contain $0$. Each complex number has its unique real decomposition $a+ib$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$; write $\\overline{a+ib}=a-ib$. A function on $U$ is smooth if all of its partial derivatives of every finite order in the real coordinates $(x^1,y^1,\\ldots,x^n,y^n)$ exist and are continuous. Define the Wirtinger derivatives by $\\partial_i=\\tfrac12(\\partial_{x^i}-i\\partial_{y^i})$ and $\\partial_{\\bar i}=\\tfrac12(\\partial_{x^i}+i\\partial_{y^i})$.\n\nA **Kähler coordinate-patch datum** is such a pair $(U,K)$ with a smooth real-valued function $K:U\\to\\mathbb R$ for which the matrix $g_{i\\bar j}(z,\\bar z):=\\partial_i\\partial_{\\bar j}K(z,\\bar z)$ satisfies $g_{i\\bar j}=\\overline{g_{j\\bar i}}$ and is positive definite: for every $z\\in U$ and every nonzero $v=(v^1,\\ldots,v^n)\\in\\mathbb C^n$, $\\bar v^{\\bar j}g_{i\\bar j}(z,\\bar z)v^i>0$. This smooth Hermitian matrix is the Kähler metric specified by the potential $K$. In natural units $\\hbar=c=1$, mass dimensions are powers of mass; assign $[z^i]=1$, $[K]=2$, and $[g_{i\\bar j}]=0$.' design_state= 'Step 1 local definition added to supply an explicit prerequisite; see batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json post-qftx-n1-wess-zumino-model statement= 'Use oriented and time-oriented Minkowski spacetime $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3$, metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and natural units $\\hbar=c=1$. Let $n\\geq1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$ be an open set containing $0$, let $K:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth, and require the Hermitian matrix $g_{i\\bar j}(z,\\bar z)=\\partial_i\\partial_{\\bar j}K(z,\\bar z)$ to be positive definite at every point of $U$. Let $W:U\\to\\mathbb C$ be holomorphic. These data specify the Kähler coordinate patch and holomorphic superpotential of the model.\n\nFor each $i=1,\\ldots,n$, take an even smooth chiral superfield $\\Phi^i$ on the four-dimensional superspace, with unique scalar, Weyl-fermion, and complex auxiliary components $(\\phi^i,\\psi^i_\\alpha,F^i)$ as fixed by the chiral-superfield convention. The scalar map $\\phi:M\\to U$ is smooth with compact support, and the coefficient functions of $\\psi^i$ and $F^i$ are smooth and compactly supported. The supersymmetry parameters $\\epsilon^\\alpha$ and $\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}$ are constant Grassmann-odd Weyl spinors, and the adopted variation is $\\delta\\Phi^i=(\\epsilon^\\alpha Q_\\alpha+\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha})\\Phi^i$ with the chiral-superfield supercharges. The engineering dimensions are $[x^\\mu]=-1$, $[\\theta]=[\\bar\\theta]=-1/2$, $[\\phi^i]=1$, $[\\psi^i]=3/2$, $[F^i]=2$, $[K]=2$, and $[W]=3$ in mass units.\n\nAdopt the classical action\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{WZ}}=\\int_M d^4x\\left(\\int d^4\\theta\\,K(\\bar\\Phi,\\Phi)+\\left[\\int d^2\\theta\\,W(\\Phi)+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right]\\right),$$\n\nwhere the ordered Berezin measures are those fixed for four-dimensional N=1 superspace. In the bosonic zero-derivative sector, the algebraic auxiliary-field terms are\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{F}=g_{i\\bar j}(\\phi,\\bar\\phi)F^i\\bar F^{\\bar j}+F^iW_i(\\phi)+\\bar F^{\\bar j}\\overline{W_j(\\phi)}.$$\n\nThe canonical Wess–Zumino model is the special case $U=\\mathbb C^n$, $K=\\sum_i\\bar\\Phi^{\\bar i}\\Phi^i$, and hence $g_{i\\bar j}=\\delta_{i\\bar j}$. This is an adopted classical formulation and normalization, not a deduction that nature realizes the model.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored from the exact planned contract; current inputs and open page-level readiness edges are recorded in batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield statement= 'Use the selected smooth right-principal $SU(2)$ bundle $P\\to M$ and its connection $A$ from the non-Abelian Yang–Mills model, with $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, and positive dimensionless coupling $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}$. For each matter representation, let $V_R$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space and $R:SU(2)\\to U(V_R)$ a smooth unitary representation; write $\\rho_{R*}$ for its Lie-algebra differential. In the fundamental representation set $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and $t_a=iT_a=\\sigma_a/2$, so $\\operatorname{Tr}_2(t_at_b)=\\tfrac12\\delta_{ab}$. In representation $R$, set $t_{a,R}=i\\rho_{R*}(T_a)$. In a local trivialization write $V=V^at_a$ and $V_R=V^at_{a,R}$, and set $A_\\mu=-i g_{\\mathrm{YM}}B_\\mu$ when $B_\\mu=B_\\mu^at_a$ is its vector component. Thus the matter covariant derivative is $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+\\rho_{R*}(A_\\mu)=\\partial_\\mu-i g_{\\mathrm{YM}}B_\\mu^at_{a,R}$.\n\nA **vector superfield prepotential** is a dimensionless smooth even real matrix-valued superfield $V$ in the finite Grassmann-valued superspace of the N=1 chiral-superfield convention. Under a global rigid supersymmetry variation it transforms as $\\delta V=(\\epsilon^\\alpha Q_\\alpha+\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha})V$, with the supercharges defined in the chiral-superfield convention. Its local gauge transformations are $\\Phi_R\\mapsto e^{i\\Lambda_R}\\Phi_R$ and $e^{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}V_R}\\mapsto e^{i\\Lambda_R^\\dagger}e^{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}V_R}e^{-i\\Lambda_R}$, where $\\Phi_R$ is chiral, $V_R=V^at_{a,R}$, and $\\Lambda_R=\\Lambda^at_{a,R}$ is a dimensionless chiral gauge-parameter superfield in the represented complexified Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(2)_{\\mathbb C}=\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb C$. The associated chiral spinor field strength is defined by\n\n$$\\mathcal W_{\\alpha,R}=-\\tfrac14\\bar D^{\\dot\\beta}\\bar D_{\\dot\\beta}\\!\\left(e^{-2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}V_R}D_\\alpha e^{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}V_R}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $D_\\alpha=\\partial^L_{\\theta^\\alpha}+i\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\beta}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\beta}\\partial_\\mu$ and $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}-i\\theta^\\beta\\sigma^\\mu_{\\beta\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu$. Admissible vector-superfield data require $\\bar D_{\\dot\\gamma}\\mathcal W_{\\alpha,R}=0$ and the adjoint transition law $\\mathcal W_{\\alpha,j}=\\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}\\mathcal W_{\\alpha,i}$ on each overlap with principal transition function $g_{ij}$.\n\nIn a local Wess–Zumino-gauge representative, the canonical component fields in the prepotential are a Hermitian gauge potential $B_\\mu$, an adjoint Grassmann-odd Weyl gaugino $\\lambda_{B,\\alpha}$, and a real adjoint auxiliary field $D_B$, with expansion $V_{\\mathrm{WZ}}=\\theta\\sigma^\\mu\\bar\\theta B_\\mu+i\\theta^2\\bar\\theta\\bar\\lambda_B-i\\bar\\theta^2\\theta\\lambda_B+\\tfrac12\\theta^2\\bar\\theta^2D_B$. Here $\\theta^2=\\theta^\\alpha\\theta_\\alpha$ and $\\bar\\theta^2=\\bar\\theta_{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}$ use the fixed antisymmetric spinor tensors. Their dimensions are $[B_\\mu]=1$, $[\\lambda_B]=3/2$, and $[D_B]=2$. The connection-normalized fields are $A_\\mu=-ig_{\\mathrm{YM}}B_\\mu$, $\\lambda=g_{\\mathrm{YM}}\\lambda_B$, and $D=g_{\\mathrm{YM}}D_B$; hence $[A_\\mu]=1$, $[\\lambda]=3/2$, and $[D]=2$. The full prepotential is used for supersymmetry transformations; Wess–Zumino gauge is only a component representative and need not be preserved by an uncompensated supersymmetry variation.' design_state= 'Step 1 local addition authored to supply the specified scope or its missing explicit prerequisite; see batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json pthm-qftx-n1-wess-zumino-off-shell-invariance statement= 'Use the rigid N=1 Wess–Zumino sigma-model postulate on $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, Kähler coordinate patch $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$, smooth positive metric $g_{i\\bar j}=\\partial_i\\partial_{\\bar j}K$, holomorphic superpotential $W$, and smooth compactly supported component fields. Under the global supersymmetry variation $\\delta\\Phi^i=(\\epsilon^\\alpha Q_\\alpha+\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha})\\Phi^i$ with constant Grassmann-odd parameters and the fixed chiral-superfield supercharges, the variation of the adopted superspace Lagrangian is a spacetime divergence; consequently $\\delta S_{\\mathrm{WZ}}=0$. The supercharges satisfy $\\{Q_\\alpha,Q_\\beta\\}=0$, $\\{\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha},\\bar Q_{\\dot\\beta}\\}=0$, and $\\{Q_\\alpha,\\bar Q_{\\dot\\beta}\\}=2i\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\beta}\\partial_\\mu$. Their induced transformations on the scalar, Weyl-fermion, and auxiliary components close on translations without using equations of motion, with the auxiliary fields retained.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored from the exact planned contract; current inputs and open page-level readiness edges are recorded in batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json pthm-qftx-n1-f-term-potential statement= 'Use the rigid N=1 Kähler sigma-model action with scalar coordinates $z=(z^1,\\ldots,z^n)\\in U\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$, smooth real Kähler potential $K$, positive-definite Hermitian metric $g_{i\\bar j}=\\partial_i\\partial_{\\bar j}K$, holomorphic superpotential $W$, and complex algebraic auxiliary fields $F^i$. In the bosonic sector with fermions and spacetime derivatives set to zero, write $W_i=\\partial_iW(z)$ and $\\bar W_{\\bar j}=\\partial_{\\bar j}\\overline{W(z)}$. The auxiliary Lagrangian is\n\n$$\\mathcal L_F=g_{i\\bar j}F^i\\bar F^{\\bar j}+F^iW_i+\\bar F^{\\bar j}\\bar W_{\\bar j}.$$\n\nLet $g^{i\\bar j}$ be the inverse Hermitian matrix. The algebraic field equation is $F^i=-g^{i\\bar j}\\bar W_{\\bar j}$, and eliminating $F$ gives the nonnegative scalar-potential contribution\n\n$$V_F=g^{i\\bar j}W_i\\bar W_{\\bar j}\\geq0.$$\n\nFor the canonical Kähler potential $K=\\sum_i\\bar z^{\\bar i}z^i$, this becomes $V_F=\\sum_i|W_i|^2$. The simple sum is specific to that metric; for a noncanonical Kähler metric the inverse matrix in the displayed formula is required. This statement concerns the classical F-term sector only and does not include gauge D-terms or quantum corrections.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored from the exact planned contract; current inputs and open page-level readiness edges are recorded in batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json post-qftx-n1-gauge-matter-model statement= 'Use the selected classical pure $SU(2)$ gauge model on oriented and time-oriented Minkowski $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, one selected smooth right-principal bundle $P\\to M$, connection $A$, curvature $F_A=dA+\\tfrac12[A\\wedge A]$, invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, and positive dimensionless $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}$ from the non-Abelian Yang–Mills model. Use natural units $\\hbar=c=1$, so $[x^\\mu]=-1$, $[g_{\\mathrm{YM}}]=0$, $[A_\\mu]=1$, and $[F_{A,\\mu\\nu}]=2$. Let $g=g_{\\mathrm{YM}}$.\n\nChoose a finite index set $I$ with $|I|\\geq1$. For each $a\\in I$, choose a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space $V_a$, a smooth unitary representation $R_a:SU(2)\\to U(V_a)$, and its associated vector bundle $E_a=P\\times_{R_a}V_a$. The matter field $\\Phi^a$ is a smooth chiral superfield with local components in $E_a$ and unique scalar, left Weyl, and complex auxiliary components $(\\phi^a,\\psi^a,F^a)$; their engineering dimensions are $1$, $3/2$, and $2$. Let $V$ be the full real vector prepotential of the local vector-superfield definition, with connection-normalized component $A_\\mu=-ig B_\\mu$ in a local unitary frame, and let $\\mathcal W_\\alpha$ be its adjoint chiral field strength. The matter covariant derivative is $D_\\mu\\phi^a=\\partial_\\mu\\phi^a+\\rho_{a*}(A_\\mu)\\phi^a=\\partial_\\mu\\phi^a-ig B_\\mu^b t_{b,a}\\phi^a$, where $t_{b,a}=i\\rho_{a*}(T_b)$ and $T_b=-i\\sigma_b/2$. Take smooth field-dependent component fluctuations with compact support in the local trivializing regions used for the action; noncompact backgrounds are treated only through compactly supported relative action differences.\n\nChoose a holomorphic gauge-invariant superpotential $W_G(\\Phi)$ of engineering dimension three, so $dW_G(\\phi)(\\rho_{a*}(X)\\phi^a)=0$ for every $X\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2)$, with the represented infinitesimal action summed over $a$. For a superspace superfield $X$, write $[X]_D=\\int d^4\\theta\\,X$ and, for chiral $Y$, $[Y]_F=\\int d^2\\theta\\,Y$, using the fixed ordered Berezin measures. Let $\\kappa_\\mathbb C$ denote the complex-bilinear extension of $\\kappa$. Adopt the action\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{GM}}=\\int_M d^4x\\left\\{\\sum_{a\\in I}[\\bar\\Phi^a e^{2gV_{R_a}}\\Phi^a]_D+\\left(\\left[\\frac{1}{4g^2}\\kappa_\\mathbb C(\\mathcal W^\\alpha,\\mathcal W_\\alpha)+W_G(\\Phi)\\right]_F+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right)\\right\\},$$\n\nwhere $V_{R_a}$ is the represented vector prepotential from the local definition. Its coupling convention is fixed by requiring the bosonic gauge-curvature component to be exactly $-\\kappa(F_A\\wedge *_\\eta F_A)/(2g^2)$; the same $g$ occurs in $e^{2gV_{R_a}}$ and in $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+\\rho_{a*}(A_\\mu)$, with no second gauge-coupling rescaling. The matter fields have $[\\Phi^a]=1$, $[\\psi^a]=3/2$, and $[F^a]=2$; $W_G$ has engineering dimension three. The model is defined locally in bundle trivializations with the transition laws induced by $P$; the D/F densities are required to obey the displayed supergauge law, with $\\kappa$ invariant and $W_G$ gauge invariant, so the local action densities patch. Take configurations for which the action is integrable, or use compactly supported relative action differences with the same boundary convention as the supplied pure-gauge model. This is an adopted classical action, not a derivation that nature realizes it or that a quantum continuum theory exists. The gauge auxiliary field contributes its separate $D$-term sector; the $F$-term theorem on this page concerns only the chiral auxiliary contribution.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored from the exact planned contract; current inputs and open page-level readiness edges are recorded in batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json pthm-qftx-n1-gauge-matter-off-shell-invariance statement= 'For the adopted rigid N=1 SU(2) gauge-matter superspace action, use the full real vector prepotential $V$ and the chiral matter superfields $\\Phi^a$ without fixing Wess–Zumino gauge. Assume smooth compactly supported vector and matter component fluctuations in the local trivializing regions used for the variation. Under the global supertranslation $\\delta=(\\epsilon^\\alpha Q_\\alpha+\\bar\\epsilon^{\\dot\\alpha}\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha})$ with constant Grassmann-odd parameters, the variation of every full-superspace D-term is a sum of odd-coordinate derivatives and a spacetime divergence. The same is true of the chiral F-terms built from the gauge-invariant holomorphic superpotential and the chiral vector field strength. Compact support removes the spacetime boundary contributions, so the complete action is invariant. This is a conditional classical invariance statement for the full superspace fields; it does not assert that Wess–Zumino gauge is preserved by an uncompensated supersymmetry transformation.' design_state= 'Step 1 local addition authored to supply the specified scope or its missing explicit prerequisite; see batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json ex-qftx-n1-wess-zumino-cubic-f-potential statement= 'Take one chiral field with canonical Kähler potential $K(\\bar\\phi,\\phi)=\\bar\\phi\\phi$ and holomorphic superpotential $W(\\phi)=\\tfrac12m\\phi^2+\\tfrac16y\\phi^3$, where $m\\in\\mathbb C$ has mass dimension one and $y\\in\\mathbb C$ is dimensionless. For a smooth compactly supported scalar component $\\phi:M\\to\\mathbb C$, the auxiliary equation and F-term potential are\n\n$$F=-\\overline{m\\phi+\\tfrac12y\\phi^2},\\qquad V_F=\\left|m\\phi+\\tfrac12y\\phi^2\\right|^2.$$\n\nThis is a classical model calculation, not an empirical claim or a quantum effective potential.' design_state= 'Step 1 local addition authored to supply the specified scope or its missing explicit prerequisite; see batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-30.pages.json ex-qftx-n1-su2-fundamental-matter-covariant-derivative statement= "Choose the fundamental representation $R_{\\mathrm f}:SU(2)\\to U(\\mathbb C^2)$ with Hermitian generators $t_a=\\sigma_a/2$. In a local trivialization write a scalar matter component as $\\phi=(u,v)^T$ and the Hermitian vector potential as $B_\\mu=B_\\mu^at_a$, so the supplied connection is $A_\\mu=-ig_{\\mathrm{YM}}B_\\mu$. The fundamental covariant derivative $D_\\mu\\phi=\\partial_\\mu\\phi+\\rho_{\\mathrm f*}(A_\\mu)\\phi$ has components\n\n$$D_\\mu u=\\partial_\\mu u-\\frac{i g_{\\mathrm{YM}}}{2}\\left(B_\\mu^3u+(B_\\mu^1-iB_\\mu^2)v\\right),\\qquad D_\\mu v=\\partial_\\mu v-\\frac{i g_{\\mathrm{YM}}}{2}\\left((B_\\mu^1+iB_\\mu^2)u-B_\\mu^3v\\right).$$\n\nOn an overlap with transition $h(x)\\in SU(2)$, the associated-bundle component is $\\phi'=h^{-1}\\phi$ and the right-connection local form is $A_\\mu'=h^{-1}A_\\mu h+h^{-1}\\partial_\\mu h$. Consequently $D_\\mu'\\phi'=h^{-1}D_\\mu\\phi$, so the scalar kinetic density $-(D_\\mu\\phi)^\\dagger D^\\mu\\phi$ agrees in both local frames." design_state= 'Step 1 local addition authored to supply the specified scope or its missing explicit prerequisite; see batch-30 notes.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-31.pages.json post-qftx-n2-vector-hypermultiplet-model statement= 'Adopt a classical rigid four-dimensional model on the oriented, time-oriented Minkowski spacetime $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^\\mu$ of length, orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, time orientation of increasing $x^0$, and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Use $c=\\hbar=1$. Fix the global gauge group $G=SU(2)$, not its quotient $SO(3)$. The adjoint action is $\\operatorname{Ad}_uX=uXu^{-1}$; choose one smooth right principal bundle $\\pi:P\\to M$ in the single sector $\\Sigma=\\{[P]\\}$, where $[P]$ means smooth bundle isomorphism over $\\operatorname{id}_M$. In the anti-Hermitian basis $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, use $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, so $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$, and a fixed dimensionless coupling $g=g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$. Use $\\sigma^0=I_2$, the Pauli matrices $\\sigma^j$, $\\bar\\sigma^0=I_2$, $\\bar\\sigma^j=-\\sigma^j$, and $\\epsilon_{12}=1$; there is no theta term in this selected model.\n\nUse the N=1 superspace algebra with even coordinates $x^\\mu$ and odd coordinates $\\theta^\\alpha,\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}$, the left derivatives and Berezin measures specified in the superspace definitions, and $D_\\alpha=\\partial^L_{\\theta^\\alpha}+i\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\beta}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\beta}\\partial_\\mu$, $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}-i\\theta^\\beta\\sigma^\\mu_{\\beta\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu$. A chiral field obeys $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi=0$. In each representation $R$, the dimensionless real vector prepotential $V_R$ transforms by $e^{2gV_R}\\mapsto e^{i\\Lambda_R^\\dagger}e^{2gV_R}e^{-i\\Lambda_R}$ and a chiral matter field transforms by $Q_R\\mapsto e^{i\\Lambda_R}Q_R$, where $\\Lambda_R$ is a chiral gauge-parameter superfield. Its chiral field strength is $\\mathcal W_{\\alpha,R}=-\\tfrac14\\bar D^{\\dot\\beta}\\bar D_{\\dot\\beta}(e^{-2gV_R}D_\\alpha e^{2gV_R})$. The connection-normalized vector multiplet consists on shell of the connection $A_\\mu$, one complex adjoint scalar $\\phi$, and two adjoint Weyl fermions; its N=1 decomposition is one vector superfield $V_{\\mathrm{ad}}$ and one adjoint chiral superfield $\\Phi$. The canonical scalar is $\\phi_{\\mathrm{can}}=\\phi/g$. Here “on shell” means after eliminating algebraic auxiliary fields and imposing the field equations; the full vector superfield also contains the real auxiliary adjoint field $D$.\n\nChoose one finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space $V_R$ and smooth unitary representation $\\rho_R:SU(2)\\to U(V_R)$. The hypermultiplet consists of chiral superfields $Q$ and $\\widetilde Q$ whose scalar components are smooth sections of $P\\times_{\\rho_R}V_R$ and its dual; the dual action is $\\rho_{R^*}(u)\\ell=\\ell\\circ\\rho_R(u^{-1})$. Each chiral N=1 hypermultiplet field has one complex scalar, one Weyl fermion, and one auxiliary field; hence the pair has two complex scalars and two Weyl fermions, which form one Dirac fermion on shell. Write $\\mathfrak{su}(2)_{\\mathbb C}=\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb C$ and let $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}$ be the complex-bilinear extension of $\\kappa$. This form is nondegenerate and invariant under the complexified adjoint action, so for $R=\\mathrm{ad}$ it identifies the dual with the adjoint. Use the Hermitian form $h_R(X,Y)=\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\overline X,Y)$ on the complexified adjoint, where conjugation is with respect to the real Lie algebra. The local invariant pairing is $\\widetilde Q Q$, and $\\widetilde Q\\Phi Q$ means $\\widetilde Q\\,\\rho_{R*}(\\Phi)Q$. The hypermultiplet is canonically normalized, while $\\Phi$ is in the connection normalization above. “Holomorphic” means complex analytic in the adjoint coordinates $\\Phi^a$ on $\\mathfrak{su}(2)_{\\mathbb C}$. The microscopic vector prepotential is the holomorphic, gauge-invariant quadratic function\n\n$$\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{UV}}(\\Phi)=\\frac{i}{2g^2}\\,\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi,\\Phi),\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}(\\mathcal F_{ab})=\\frac{1}{g^2}\\kappa_{ab}>0.$$\n\nHere $\\mathcal F_{ab}=\\partial_a\\partial_b\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{UV}}$ in the basis $T_a$. In the local Berezin normalization, its N=1 vector action is\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{vec}}=\\frac{1}{4g^2}\\left([\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathcal W^\\alpha,\\mathcal W_\\alpha)]_F+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right)+\\frac{1}{g^2}[\\kappa_H(\\bar\\Phi,e^{2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}}\\Phi)]_D,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal W_{\\alpha}$ is the chiral field strength and $\\kappa_H(\\bar X,Y)=\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\bar X,Y)$. For a superspace expression $X$, $[X]_F=\\int d^2\\theta\\,X$ and $[X]_D=\\int d^4\\theta\\,X$, using the coefficient-extraction measures and generator order in the superspace Grassmann algebra. Thus the vector kinetic term has exactly the connection-normalized Yang-Mills coefficient $-\\kappa(F\\wedge *_\\eta F)/(2g^2)$.\n\nThe adopted renormalizable one-hypermultiplet action is\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{N=2}=\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{vec}}+[Q^\\dagger e^{2gV_R}Q+\\widetilde Q^\\dagger e^{2gV_{R^*}}\\widetilde Q]_D+\\left([\\sqrt2\\,\\widetilde Q\\Phi Q+m\\widetilde Q Q]_F+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right),$$\n\nwith complex mass parameter $m$ of dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. In canonical normalization $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{can}}=\\Phi/g$, the cubic coefficient is $\\sqrt2g$, the same gauge coupling that appears in the covariant derivative. The ultraviolet prepotential above is quadratic; a low-energy effective prepotential may instead be non-polynomial and valid only on a specified electric/magnetic patch. This postulate adopts no such low-energy solution or duality claim.\n\nThe model includes rigid four-dimensional $\\mathcal N=2$ supersymmetry: “rigid” means its spinor parameters are constant in spacetime, and $\\mathcal N=2$ means two independent left-Weyl supercharges, hence eight real components. Its invariance is an adopted model premise, not a deduction in this item. The N=1 superspace expression is a field/action packaging; the full hypermultiplet transformations close only after imposing the physical field equations, and the finite N=1 auxiliary fields are eliminated in this on-shell description. Fields are smooth in local trivializations and admissible variations are smooth and compactly supported; actions are taken over relatively compact regions or when the spacetime integral converges. No path-integral measure, regulator, quantum theory, or continuum existence claim is included. In mass-dimension notation $[x]=-1$, $[\\theta]=-\\frac12$, $[\\Phi]=[Q]=[\\widetilde Q]=1$, $[\\mathcal W_\\alpha]=3/2$, $[m]=1$, and $[g]=0$; equivalently scalar fields and mass parameters have units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; N=1 supplier and current vector-superfield readiness remain escalated.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-31.pages.json post-qftx-n4-super-yang-mills-model statement= "Use the oriented, time-oriented Minkowski spacetime $M=\\mathbb{R}^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^\\mu$ of length, orientation $dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, time orientation of increasing $x^0$, and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Let $P\\to M$ be one smooth right-principal $SU(2)$ bundle in the selected sector $\\{[P]\\}$; $[P]$ is its smooth bundle-isomorphism class and the global group is $SU(2)$, not $SO(3)$. The adjoint action is $\\operatorname{Ad}_uX=uXu^{-1}$. Use $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ with $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$, $c=\\hbar=1$, and dimensionless coupling $g=g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$. The Pauli matrices are $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. Use $\\sigma^0=I_2$, $\\sigma^j$ for these spatial matrices, $\\bar\\sigma^0=I_2$, $\\bar\\sigma^j=-\\sigma^j$, and $\\epsilon_{12}=1$, so $\\sigma^\\mu\\bar\\sigma^\\nu+\\sigma^\\nu\\bar\\sigma^\\mu=-2\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}I_2$. In the N=1 decomposition, $V_{\\mathrm{ad}}$ is the dimensionless real adjoint vector prepotential with $e^{2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}}\\mapsto e^{i\\Lambda^\\dagger}e^{2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}}e^{-i\\Lambda}$, where $\\Lambda$ is a chiral gauge-parameter superfield, and $\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2,\\Phi_3$ are adjoint chiral superfields in connection normalization. Each $\\Phi_i$ obeys $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi_i=0$ and has one complex adjoint scalar, one adjoint Weyl fermion, and an algebraic auxiliary field. The canonical chiral fields are $\\Phi_{i,\\mathrm{can}}=\\Phi_i/g$.\n\n\nWrite $\\mathfrak{su}(2)_{\\mathbb C}=\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb C$, let $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}$ be the complex-bilinear extension of $\\kappa$, set $\\kappa_H(\\bar X,Y)=\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\bar X,Y)$, and write $[X,Y]$ for the complexified Lie bracket. The complexified form is nondegenerate and adjoint-invariant. Use $D_\\alpha=\\partial^L_{\\theta^\\alpha}+i\\sigma^\\mu_{\\alpha\\dot\\beta}\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\beta}\\partial_\\mu$ and $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}=-\\partial^L_{\\bar\\theta^{\\dot\\alpha}}-i\\theta^\\beta\\sigma^\\mu_{\\beta\\dot\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu$. Each $\\Phi_i$ is an even chiral superfield satisfying $\\bar D_{\\dot\\alpha}\\Phi_i=0$, and the adjoint chiral field strength is $\\mathcal W_{\\alpha,\\mathrm{ad}}=-\\tfrac14\\bar D^{\\dot\\beta}\\bar D_{\\dot\\beta}(e^{-2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}}D_\\alpha e^{2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}})$. The adopted connection-normalized N=1 action is\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{N=4}=\\frac{1}{g^2}\\left\\{\\frac14\\left([\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathcal W^\\alpha,\\mathcal W_\\alpha)]_F+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right)+\\sum_{i=1}^3[\\kappa_H(\\bar\\Phi_i,e^{2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}}\\Phi_i)]_D+\\left([\\sqrt2\\,\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi_1,[\\Phi_2,\\Phi_3])]_F+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right)\\right\\}.$$\n\nHere $[Y]_F=\\int d^2\\theta\\,Y$ and $[Z]_D=\\int d^4\\theta\\,Z$ use the coefficient-extraction measures specified in the superspace Grassmann algebra. In canonical fields, the cubic superpotential coefficient is $\\sqrt2g$, so its Yukawa coupling equals the gauge coupling. On shell, meaning after eliminating algebraic auxiliary fields and imposing the field equations, the physical content is one gauge connection, six real adjoint scalars $X^I$ ($I=1,\\ldots,6$), and four adjoint Weyl fermions $\\lambda^A$ ($A=1,\\ldots,4$). The scalar potential in connection normalization is\n\n$$V_{\\mathrm{scalar}}=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\sum_{1\\le I<J\\le6}\\kappa([X^I,X^J],[X^I,X^J]);$$\n\nfor canonical fields $X^I_{\\mathrm{can}}=X^I/g$ this is $\\frac{g^2}{2}\\sum_{I<J}\\kappa([X^I_{\\mathrm{can}},X^J_{\\mathrm{can}}],[X^I_{\\mathrm{can}},X^J_{\\mathrm{can}}])$. With the N=2 model's N=1 decomposition, this is the $R=\\mathrm{ad}$, $m=0$ hypermultiplet specialization after rescaling its two canonical hypermultiplet chiral fields by $g$ to the connection normalization of $\\Phi_2$ and $\\Phi_3$.\n\nAdopt rigid four-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ supersymmetry, with four independent left-Weyl supercharges and sixteen real components, as part of this physical model postulate. The action's extended invariance and on-shell closure are not proved in this item. This is a classical action with smooth fields in local bundle trivializations and compactly supported admissible variations; no path-integral measure, regulator, quantum continuum theory, or empirical result is asserted. Use $c=\\hbar=1$: $g$ is dimensionless, connection-normalized scalar fields have units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and canonical fields have mass dimension one." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; N=1 and N=2 inputs are explicit, with upstream interface checks open.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-31.pages.json ex-qftx-n2-hypermultiplet-gauge-invariants statement= "Choose the fundamental representation $R=\\mathbb C^2$ of $SU(2)$. In each superspace fiber let $Q$ be a column in $R$, let $\\widetilde Q$ be a row in $R^*$, and let $\\Phi$ act by the represented adjoint action. Under any chiral supergauge transformation $U=e^{i\\Lambda_R}$, set $Q'=UQ$, $\\widetilde Q'=\\widetilde Q U^{-1}$, and $\\Phi'=U\\Phi U^{-1}$. Then the mass bilinear $\\widetilde Q Q$ and cubic bilinear $\\widetilde Q\\Phi Q$ in the adopted N=2 superpotential are gauge invariant. This checks gauge invariance of these two terms only; it does not prove the extended supersymmetry variation." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; proof is conditional on the N=2 model postulate.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-31.pages.json ex-qftx-n2-quadratic-prepotential statement= 'For the adopted microscopic prepotential $\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{UV}}(\\Phi)=\\frac{i}{2g^2}\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi,\\Phi)$ with $g>0$ and $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$, the Hessian $\\mathcal F_{ab}=\\partial_a\\partial_b\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{UV}}$ is the constant matrix $i\\delta_{ab}/g^2$. Its imaginary part is positive definite and independent of the scalar field $\\Phi$. This is a property of the adopted quadratic ultraviolet model; it does not assert a non-polynomial low-energy prepotential.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; proof is conditional on the N=2 model postulate.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-31.pages.json ex-qftx-n4-adjoint-hyper-decomposition statement= 'Set the N=2 hypermultiplet representation to the adjoint, use the Hermitian form $h_R(X,Y)=\\kappa(\\overline X,Y)$ on the complexified adjoint and its dual, and set its mass to $m=0$. Use the nondegenerate invariant form $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}$ to identify the dual adjoint with the adjoint. If $Q$ and $\\widetilde Q$ are the canonically normalized chiral fields, write $\\widetilde Q$ as an adjoint field under this identification; with $\\Phi_1$ the connection-normalized N=2 vector chiral field, define connection-normalized adjoint chiral fields $\\Phi_2=gQ$ and $\\Phi_3=g\\widetilde Q$. Then the N=2 hypermultiplet kinetic terms and cubic superpotential become exactly the two additional adjoint-chiral terms in the adopted N=4 N=1 action, and the on-shell component counts agree: one vector, three complex adjoint scalars, and four adjoint Weyl fermions. The calculation does not prove the N=4 supersymmetry variation.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; proof is conditional on the N=2/N=4 postulates.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-31.pages.json ex-qftx-n4-su2-noncommuting-scalar-potential statement= 'For real $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, take canonical adjoint scalar values $X^1_{\\mathrm{can}}=aT_1$, $X^2_{\\mathrm{can}}=bT_2$, and $X^I_{\\mathrm{can}}=0$ for $I=3,4,5,6$, where $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$ and\n$$\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nIf $a,b\\ne0$, the adopted N=4 scalar potential is $g^2a^2b^2/2>0$; if either coefficient is zero, it is zero.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; proof is conditional on the N=4 model postulate.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-32.pages.json def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data statement= '\nUse the flat four-dimensional N=1 superspace conventions in natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, with $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$, metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and the ordered Grassmann generators, Berezin measures, chiral constraint, and covariant derivatives fixed by the earlier superspace definitions. Fix the global gauge group as a compact closed matrix subgroup $G\\subset U(N)$; this chosen subgroup, rather than only its Lie algebra, is part of the data. Let $\\mathfrak g$ be its finite-dimensional real Lie algebra, let $\\mathcal H_i$ be finite-dimensional Hermitian spaces, and let $\\rho_i:G\\to U(\\mathcal H_i)$ be smooth unitary representations for a finite family of chiral superfields $\\Phi_i$. Work on the trivial bundle $M\\times G$ and expand in the topologically trivial sector. Let $A_\\mu$ be the connection component of the vector prepotential in a local trivialization. For the odd ghost $c$, use the non-Abelian BRST transformations $sA_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$, $sc=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, and $s\\Phi_i=-\\rho_{i*}(c)\\Phi_i$ on component fields and jets.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-32.pages.json pthm-qftx-n1-wilsonian-f-term-nonrenormalization statement= '\nLet a local rigid four-dimensional N=1 superspace action datum have the form fixed above, including its canonical matter kinetic term, with finite-dimensional unitary matter representations of a fixed compact gauge group, an additional real D-term integrand $G$, a holomorphic gauge-invariant F-term integrand $F(\\Phi,W)$, and a formal Wilsonian cutoff prescription at a nonzero scale $0<\\lambda<\\Lambda_0$. Assume the prescription gives a local derivative expansion coefficientwise in formal perturbation theory around the topologically trivial background. Let $\\langle\\cdot\\rangle_\\lambda$ be the normalized coefficientwise expectation of the selected regulated formal expansion. Assume its rigid N=1 and BRST changes of variables obey $\\langle\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}\\mathcal O\\rangle_\\lambda=\\langle\\mathcal O\\,\\delta_{\\epsilon,\\bar\\epsilon}S_\\lambda\\rangle_\\lambda$ and $\\langle s\\mathcal O\\rangle_\\lambda=0$ for every local insertion $\\mathcal O$, with counterterms satisfying the same identities and no local gauge anomaly. Then the Wilsonian chiral integrand is unchanged coefficientwise, $F_\\lambda(\\Phi,W)=F(\\Phi,W)$; the holomorphic gauge-coupling parameters may receive one-loop corrections, but no higher-loop corrections. If an unbroken U(1) factor is present, a Fayet–Iliopoulos parameter can receive a correction only at one loop, and that correction is proportional to the sum of the U(1) charges of all left-chiral multiplets, including multiplicities; it vanishes when this charge sum is zero. The theorem imposes no nonrenormalization condition on the additional real D-term integrand $G_\\lambda$, the Kähler data, or coefficients expressed after a scale-dependent chiral-field rescaling that normalizes a positive real quadratic kinetic form. It is only a formal perturbative Wilsonian statement at nonzero cutoff: it does not assert nonperturbative exactness, a result for an arbitrary one-particle-irreducible functional, or existence of a continuum interacting quantum field theory.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-32.pages.json ex-qftx-n1-wilsonian-wavefunction-vs-holomorphic statement= '\nTake one chiral superfield $\\Phi$ with mass dimension one and tree superpotential $W_0(\\Phi)=\\tfrac12m\\Phi^2+\\tfrac{y}{3!}\\Phi^3$, where $m$ has mass dimension one and $y$ is dimensionless. Use a formal perturbative Wilsonian prescription satisfying the N=1 theorem at a nonzero scale $\\lambda$, and suppose the full real quadratic kinetic coefficient at that scale is a positive function $Z(\\lambda)$. Then the holomorphic coefficients $m$ and $y$ in the unrescaled field $\\Phi$ receive no perturbative Wilsonian corrections, while the canonically normalized field $\\Phi_c=Z(\\lambda)^{1/2}\\Phi$ has coefficients $m_c(\\lambda)=m/Z(\\lambda)$ and $y_c(\\lambda)=y/Z(\\lambda)^{3/2}$. Thus the canonically normalized parameters can run if $Z(\\lambda)$ varies, even though the holomorphic Wilsonian coefficients do not.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-32.pages.json ex-qftx-n1-u1-fi-charge-trace statement= '\nTake one unbroken compact U(1) gauge factor with two left-chiral superfields of charges $+1$ and $-1$, one of each and no additional multiplicities. Use the canonical matter kinetic term with no superpotential ($F=0$) or additional real interactions ($G=0$), and a formal perturbative Wilsonian regulator satisfying the theorem at scale $\\lambda>0$. Let the bare field-independent Fayet–Iliopoulos coefficient be $\\xi$ in the term $\\xi\\int d^4x\\,d^4\\theta\\,V_1$. Then the perturbative Wilsonian coefficient satisfies $\\xi_\\lambda=\\xi$ at every loop order: the one-loop correction cancels by the charge sum, and the theorem excludes higher-loop corrections. Other real D-term couplings, if added, are not protected by this conclusion.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json def-qftx-susy-vacuum-moduli-data statement= 'Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ in four-dimensional rigid, global\n$\\mathcal N=1$ gauge-model data. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional complex\nHermitian vector space of scalar field values, let a compact Lie group $G$ act\nunitarily on $V$, and fix an orthonormal complex basis\n$(\\phi^1,\\ldots,\\phi^n)$ of $V$. Choose Hermitian matrices\n$T_a$ for the infinitesimal action of a basis of the Lie algebra of $G$, a\ngauge coupling $g>0$, and a gauge-invariant holomorphic polynomial\n$W:V\\to\\mathbb C$. Assume canonical scalar kinetic energy and no\nFayet--Iliopoulos term. Define the auxiliary-field maps\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{kin}}=-\\sum_i\\partial_\\mu\\overline{\\phi^i}\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi^i,\\qquad [\\phi^i]=\\mathrm{mass},\\quad [W]=\\mathrm{mass}^3,\\quad [g]=1.$$\n\n$$F_i(\\phi)=\\frac{\\partial W}{\\partial\\phi^i}(\\phi),\\qquad D_a(\\phi)=-g\\,\\phi^\\dagger T_a\\phi.$$\n\nThe tree-level scalar potential is\n\n$$V_{\\mathrm{tree}}(\\phi)=\\sum_{i=1}^n|F_i(\\phi)|^2+\\frac12\\sum_a D_a(\\phi)^2.$$\n\nThus $F_i$ and $D_a$ have mass dimension two and $V_{\\mathrm{tree}}$ has\nmass dimension four.\n\nA **classical supersymmetric vacuum configuration** is a constant scalar\nconfiguration $\\phi\\in V$ for which every $F_i(\\phi)$ and every\n$D_a(\\phi)$ is zero. Its **classical vacuum-moduli set** is the topological\norbit space\n\n$$\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{cl}}=\\{\\phi\\in V:F_i(\\phi)=0\\text{ for all }i,\\ D_a(\\phi)=0\\text{ for all }a\\}/G.$$\n\nwhere two configurations are identified exactly when they lie in the same\ncompact-$G$ orbit, and the set has the quotient topology. The stability\nconvention here retains every such compact-group orbit, including singular\norbits. No quotient by the complexified group, geometric-invariant-theory\nstability criterion, or smooth-manifold structure is included in this\ndefinition.\n\nFor a **specified, already constructed quantum theory** $\\mathcal T$, suppose\nthere is a Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$, a unitary translation representation\n$U(a)$, and densely defined supercharges $Q^I$ and their Hilbert adjoints on a\ncommon domain $\\mathcal D$. A **quantum supersymmetric vacuum ray** is the\nphase-equivalence class $[\\Omega]$ of a normalized vector $\\Omega\\in\\mathcal D$\nsatisfying\n$U(a)\\Omega=\\Omega$ for every spacetime translation $a$ and\n$Q^I\\Omega=(Q^I)^*\\Omega=0$ for every supercharge $I$. The **quantum\nvacuum-moduli set** $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm q}(\\mathcal T)$ is the set of these\nrays, with the topology, if any, supplied by the constructed theory. This\ndefinition is conditional on normalizable invariant vacuum vectors and a\nspecified quantum Hilbert-space model. It asserts no path-integral measure,\ncontinuum limit, nonperturbative existence, or equality between\n$\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{cl}}$ and $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm q}(\\mathcal T)$.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json def-qftx-n2-coulomb-branch-data statement= "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, spacetime $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and a selected pure four-dimensional\nrigid $\\mathcal N=2$ Yang--Mills model whose global gauge group is $SU(2)$.\nThe matter-hypermultiplet representation set is empty. In $\\mathcal N=1$\nlanguage the gauge sector is one vector multiplet and one adjoint chiral\nmultiplet with scalar $\\phi\\in\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$; the selected\naction convention uses the $\\sqrt2 g$ vector--chiral coupling, a positive\ndimensionless gauge coupling $g$, and no mass parameter. The earlier model\npostulate is the input that fixes its complete action and component\nnormalizations.\n\nA **classical Coulomb-branch configuration** is a constant adjoint scalar\nconfiguration satisfying the model's zero auxiliary-field conditions, taken\nmodulo the compact $SU(2)$ gauge action. This uses the compact-group orbit\nquotient in the preceding vacuum-moduli definition; it does not replace that\nquotient by a complexified-group stability construction. In the\nweak-coupling semiclassical frame, let $a$ be the eigenvalue coordinate fixed\nby $\\phi=\\operatorname{diag}(a,-a)$, of mass dimension one. When a quantum\ntheory with a vacuum-moduli set has actually been constructed, the **quantum\nCoulomb-branch coordinate** $u$ denotes the chosen gauge-invariant chiral\nquadratic-operator expectation value in the source trace normalization for\nwhich the weak-coupling relation is $u=a^2/2$. Its mass dimension is two.\nThis definition does not assert that $u$ globally parameterizes the quantum\nvacua.\n\nOn an open patch $U$ of the $u$-plane where the period functions are\nholomorphic and the local gauge-coupling matrix has positive imaginary part,\nan **electric/magnetic frame** means a chosen ordered basis of the formal\ncharge lattice that fixes local cycle representatives. Their period\nfunctions are the complex electric coordinate $a:U\\to\\mathbb C$ and complex\ndual coordinate $a_D:U\\to\\mathbb C$, both of mass dimension one. The local\ntwo-derivative abelian gauge action is encoded by a holomorphic\n**prepotential** $\\mathcal F_U(a)$ of mass dimension two, defined on that\npatch by\n\n$$a_D=\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal F_U}{\\partial a},\\qquad \\tau=\\frac{\\partial a_D}{\\partial a}=\\frac{\\partial^2\\mathcal F_U}{\\partial a^2},\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}\\tau>0.$$\n\nThe dimensionless $\\tau$ is the local complexified gauge coupling; changing\nthe electric/magnetic frame changes the pair $(a_D,a)$ by the corresponding\nintegral linear transformation and may require a different local\nprepotential. Thus neither period is asserted to be a single-valued global\nfunction on a punctured branch.\n\nFor this source-normalized data, define the **formal electric/magnetic charge\nlabel lattice** to be $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{SW}}=\\mathbb Z^2$, with a label\n$(n_e,n_m)$ and period pairing\n$\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}(u;n_e,n_m)=n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)$. The pair labels\nthe central-charge formula; the lattice definition does not assert that every\nlabel is an allowed line operator, a dynamical particle, a stable state, or a\nstate in any constructed Hilbert space. Those are separate model data.\n\nThe word **exact** in this page refers only to the adopted source-proposed\nWilsonian two-derivative low-energy data on a regular patch of the selected\npure SU(2) model. It does not mean a proof of a nonperturbative continuum\npath integral, a global single-valued prepotential, or an exact statement\nabout arbitrary $\\mathcal N=2$ theories." design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model statement= "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and the local duality frame near one\nmonopole singularity of the selected pure $SU(2)$, four-dimensional rigid\n$\\mathcal N=2$ model. Let $A_D$ be the complex scalar coordinate in the\nlocal dual $U(1)_D$ vector multiplet, and let $M,\\widetilde M$ be complex\nscalar fields of dual electric charges $+1$ and $-1$ in two $\\mathcal N=1$\nchiral multiplets. The dual gauge group acts by\n\n$$A_D\\mapsto A_D,\\qquad M\\mapsto e^{i\\alpha}M,\\qquad \\widetilde M\\mapsto e^{-i\\alpha}\\widetilde M,\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z).$$\n\nLet $u(A_D)$ be the supplied holomorphic coordinate function on an open\npatch $|A_D|<r$ about $A_D=0$, with $u(0)=u_m$ and\n$c:=u'(0)\\ne0$. Let $\\mu\\ne0$ be the coefficient of the small adjoint\nmass perturbation, let $g_D>0$ be the dimensionless dual gauge coupling,\nand use canonical scalar kinetic terms and zero Fayet--Iliopoulos parameter.\nDefine\n\n$$W_\\mu(A_D,M,\\widetilde M)=\\sqrt2 A_D M\\widetilde M+\\mu u(A_D),\\qquad D=g_D\\bigl(|M|^2-|\\widetilde M|^2\\bigr).$$\n\n$$V_\\mu=|\\partial_{A_D}W_\\mu|^2+|\\partial_M W_\\mu|^2+|\\partial_{\\widetilde M}W_\\mu|^2+\\frac12D^2.$$\n\nLet $\\widetilde F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu\\widetilde A_\\nu-\n\\partial_\\nu\\widetilde A_\\mu$ and\n$\\widetilde D_\\mu A_D=\\partial_\\mu A_D$,\n$\\widetilde D_\\mu M=(\\partial_\\mu-i\\widetilde A_\\mu)M$, and\n$\\widetilde D_\\mu\\widetilde M=(\\partial_\\mu+i\\widetilde A_\\mu)\n\\widetilde M$. In metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, the\ncanonical bosonic kinetic Lagrangian is\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{kin}}=-\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\left(\\partial_\\mu\\overline{A_D}\\,\\partial_\\nu A_D+\\overline{\\widetilde D_\\mu M}\\,\\widetilde D_\\nu M+\\overline{\\widetilde D_\\mu\\widetilde M}\\,\\widetilde D_\\nu\\widetilde M\\right)-\\frac{1}{4g_D^2}\\widetilde F_{\\mu\\nu}\\widetilde F^{\\mu\\nu}.$$\n\nThe mass dimensions are\n$[A_D]=[M]=[\\widetilde M]=[\\mu]=\\mathrm{mass}$,\n$[u]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$, $[W_\\mu]=\\mathrm{mass}^3$,\n$[F_{A_D}]=[F_M]=[F_{\\widetilde M}]=[D]=[\\widetilde F_{\\mu\\nu}]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$,\n$[\\widetilde A_\\mu]=\\mathrm{mass}$,\n$[c]=\\mathrm{mass}$, and $[g_D]=1$. The allowed regime is restricted to\nfield values within this patch and energies below its stated effective\ncutoff; “small $\\mu$” means that the induced scale\n$(|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2)^{1/2}$ lies well below that cutoff.\n\nThis is a one-singularity effective data package. It includes neither a\nsimultaneously local electric description of mutually nonlocal dyons nor a\nclaim that the local action extends over the full moduli space. A finite-energy\nvortex is not part of this Definition; its existence, when needed, is a\nseparate hypothesis." design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json def-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-flux-tube-sector statement= "Use the dual $U(1)_D$ fields and potential of the preceding local\nmonopole-Higgs model. Let $x^1,x^2$ be coordinates on the transverse plane\n$\\mathbb R^2$ and let $x^3$ be the coordinate along a straight static tube.\nWrite $\\widetilde A_i$ for the spatial dual gauge potential,\n$\\widetilde F_{12}=\\partial_1\\widetilde A_2-\\partial_2\\widetilde A_1$,\n$D_iM=(\\partial_i-i\\widetilde A_i)M$, and\n$D_i\\widetilde M=(\\partial_i+i\\widetilde A_i)\\widetilde M$.\n\nA **finite-energy dual vortex profile of winding $n$** is a smooth static\nconfiguration $(A_D,M,\\widetilde M,\\widetilde A_1,\\widetilde A_2)$ on\n$\\mathbb R^2$ for which the transverse energy\n\n$$\\sigma=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{\\widetilde F_{12}^2}{2g_D^2}+|D_iM|^2+|D_i\\widetilde M|^2+V_\\mu-V_{\\mathrm{vac}}\\right)\\,d^2x$$\n\nis finite. The fields approach a supersymmetric vacuum at infinity satisfying\n$A_D=0$, $|M|=|\\widetilde M|=v>0$,\n$\\sqrt2 M\\widetilde M+\\mu u'(0)=0$, and\n$V_{\\mathrm{vac}}=0$. The phase map $M/|M|$ restricted to a sufficiently\nlarge circle has degree $n\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ as a map to the unit\ncircle; the phase map $\\widetilde M/|\\widetilde M|$ has degree $-n$.\nRequire also that $D_iM$ and $D_i\\widetilde M$ tend to zero on that circle,\nthat the tangential component of $\\widetilde A-d\\arg M$ tends to zero, and\nthat the limiting flux exists. The profile is a translation-invariant\ntube along $x^3$; its tension is the transverse integral $\\sigma$. This\nDefinition includes no assertion that such a configuration exists or is a\nstationary solution of the field equations.\n\nIn the adopted electric/magnetic frame, the **original electric flux** of\nthis sector means the dual magnetic flux\n$\\Phi_D=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{|x|\\le R}\\widetilde F_{12}\\,d^2x$.\nThe flux dictionary is part of the selected duality model; this Definition\ndoes not state that every electric line charge is represented by a vortex.\nIn natural units, $[\\widetilde A_i]=\\mathrm{mass}$,\n$[\\widetilde F_{12}]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$, and\n$[\\sigma]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$." design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json post-qftx-n2-seiberg-witten-effective-solution statement= "Adopt the source-proposed low-energy solution data for pure four-dimensional\nrigid $\\mathcal N=2$ Yang--Mills with global gauge group $SU(2)$, one\nadjoint vector multiplet, rank one, no matter hypermultiplets, and the coupling,\n$\\sqrt2$ vector--chiral interaction, units, and prepotential convention of\nthe selected N=2 vector-multiplet model. Use natural units\n$c=\\hbar=1$, metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and a positive\ndynamical scale $\\Lambda$ of mass dimension one. The gauge-invariant\ncoordinate $u$ has mass dimension two; set $w=u/\\Lambda^2$.\n\nOn the regular $w$-plane with singular values $w=1,-1,\\infty$ removed,\nadopt the family of genus-one curves, where $x,y,w$ are dimensionless:\n\n$$\\Sigma_w:\\quad y^2=(x-1)(x+1)(x-w).$$\n\nand the meromorphic period differential\n\n$$\\lambda_{\\mathrm{SW}}=\\frac{\\sqrt2\\Lambda}{2\\pi}\\frac{y\\,dx}{x^2-1}.$$\n\nWithin this proposed solution, take the quantum vacuum-moduli data to be the\nentire $u$-plane, with a $\\mathbb Z_2$ symmetry $u\\mapsto-u$ and singular\nvalues $u=\\Lambda^2,-\\Lambda^2,\\infty$. This is part of the adopted\nsolution ansatz, not a consequence of a constructed continuum theory.\n\nFor a chosen local cycle basis $\\gamma_m,\\gamma_e$ on $\\Sigma_w$ with\nintersection number $\\gamma_m\\cdot\\gamma_e=1$, define\n$a_D=\\oint_{\\gamma_m}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{SW}}$ and\n$a=\\oint_{\\gamma_e}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{SW}}$. These periods have mass\ndimension one, are multivalued under continuation around the punctures, and\nare used in the local prepotential convention\n$a_D=\\partial\\mathcal F/\\partial a$ and\n$\\tau=\\partial^2\\mathcal F/\\partial a^2$ with\n$\\operatorname{Im}\\tau>0$. In the source's counterclockwise-loop\nconvention, the period column $(a_D,a)^T$ has monodromy matrices\n\n$$M_\\infty=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad M_+=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\-2&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad M_-=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\\\-2&3\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nAdopt the source's formal charge-label lattice $\\mathbb Z^2$ and central-charge pairing\n$\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}=n_ea+n_ma_D$ for labels\n$(n_e,n_m)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$. In the supersymmetry convention of this page,\nthe scalar central charge is normalized as\n$z=\\sqrt2\\,\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}$. The mass inequality and its\nsaturation statement are separate theorem deductions. No claim is made that\nevery lattice label is an allowed line operator or is represented by a stable\nparticle.\n\nAdopt the singularity interpretation that the $w=1$ point has a light\nmonopole with $a_D=0$, while the $w=-1$ point has a light dyon with\n$a-a_D=0$ in the chosen frame. These are source-proposed physical inputs\ntested by their monodromies; they are not deduced here from a constructed\ncontinuum theory.\nIn the local monopole patch, use the dual $U(1)_D$ data from the preceding\neffective-model definition. A small N=1-breaking mass parameter $\\mu$ of\nmass dimension one adds the local superpotential term $\\mu u(A_D)$ to\n$\\sqrt2 A_D M\\widetilde M$. The local description applies only near this\nsingularity, below its effective cutoff, and does not simultaneously describe\nthe mutually nonlocal dyon at the other singularity.\n\nHere **exact low-energy solution** means the adopted two-derivative\nWilsonian data of this source-defined model on its regular patches. It does\nnot mean a proof of a nonperturbative UV path integral, a global\nsingle-valued prepotential, or an identity for arbitrary gauge groups,\nmatter contents, or N=2 theories." design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json pthm-qftx-n2-bps-central-charge-mass statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Use the four-dimensional rigid\n$\\mathcal N=2$ super-Poincare convention\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\epsilon_{12}=1$, and\n$c=\\hbar=1$. Let $U$ be a regular patch of the selected pure-SU(2)\nSeiberg--Witten period data. Fix $u\\in U$ and a formal charge label\n$(n_e,n_m)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$. Suppose the selected physical model has a\nnonzero massive unitary rest-fiber representation $\\mathcal K$ with mass\n$M>0$, scalar central-charge matrix $Z^{12}=zI$, $Z^{21}=-zI$, a common\ndense invariant product domain $\\mathcal D$, and actual Hilbert adjoints for\nall supercharges. Require its charge normalization to be\n\n$$z=\\sqrt2\\,\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}(u;n_e,n_m)=\\sqrt2\\bigl(n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)\\bigr).$$\n\nThen\n\n$$M\\ge\\sqrt2\\,|n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)|.$$\n\nIf equality holds, choose $\\varphi\\in\\mathbb R$ with\n$e^{-i\\varphi}z=|z|$ and define\n$A=Q^1_1-e^{i\\varphi}(Q^2_2)^*$ and\n$A^\\sharp=(Q^1_1)^*-e^{-i\\varphi}Q^2_2$ on $\\mathcal D$. Both\n$A\\psi$ and $A^\\sharp\\psi$ vanish for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$.\nEquality defines algebraic BPS saturation only; the statement asserts no\nstate exists for any chosen charge label and no BPS state is stable.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json pthm-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-vortex-flux-and-tension statement= 'Use the local dual $U(1)_D$ model with natural units, positive coupling\n$g_D>0$, smooth gauge potential on $\\mathbb R^2$, and the vortex boundary\nconditions of the preceding definition. If a finite-energy dual vortex\nprofile of winding $n\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ exists, then its dual\nmagnetic flux, which is the original electric flux in the adopted duality\nframe, is\n\n$$\\Phi_D=2\\pi n,$$\n\nand its transverse tension satisfies $0<\\sigma<\\infty$. This is a\nconditional statement about an admitted profile; it asserts neither\nexistence nor stability of any vortex or a linearly confining minimum-energy\npotential between external charges.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json pthm-qftx-n2-monopole-condensation-after-n1-perturbation statement= "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and the local monopole patch of the\nselected pure-SU(2) N=2 source-proposed low-energy model. Let $u(A_D)$,\n$c=u'(0)\\ne0$, $M$, $\\widetilde M$, $g_D$, and $\\mu$ be the data in the\nlocal dual-Higgs definition, with $\\mu\\ne0$ and\n$(|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2)^{1/2}$ well below the effective cutoff. Restrict to a\nneighborhood of $A_D=0$ on which $u'(A_D)\\ne0$. The supersymmetric vacuum\nequations in this patch have exactly one $U(1)_D$ gauge orbit, represented\nby\n\n$$A_D=0,\\qquad M\\widetilde M=-\\frac{\\mu c}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad |M|=|\\widetilde M|=v:=\\left(\\frac{|\\mu c|}{\\sqrt2}\\right)^{1/2}>0.$$\n\nThus the local monopole fields have nonzero gauge-fixed amplitudes and\n$U(1)_D$ is fully Higgsed. With the canonical kinetic normalization of the\nlocal model, the dual vector mass is\n\n$$m_D^2=2g_D^2\\bigl(|M|^2+|\\widetilde M|^2\\bigr)=4g_D^2v^2>0.$$\n\nFor the confinement conclusion, make the following additional explicit\neffective-model assumption: for a fixed nonzero original electric test-charge\nsector and every sufficiently large separation $L$, the minimum-energy\nconfiguration in that sector is a straight, translation-invariant dual\nvortex of nonzero winding as defined here, and its endcap energy is bounded\nuniformly in $L$. If that flux-tube sector exists, its original electric\nflux is quantized and its tension is positive; the corresponding potential\nhas $V(L)=\\sigma L+O(1)$, where $O(1)$ denotes a term bounded uniformly as\n$L\\to\\infty$. This is linear confinement only in the stated local effective\nmodel and under the stated vortex/minimization assumption. The theorem does\nnot prove that a vortex exists or minimizes the energy." design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json ex-qftx-susy-u1-pair-classical-vacuum-moduli statement= 'Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. Take the finite-dimensional scalar field\nspace $V=\\mathbb C^2$ with coordinates $(q,\\widetilde q)$, standard\nHermitian norm, compact gauge group $U(1)$ acting by\n$(q,\\widetilde q)\\mapsto(e^{i\\alpha}q,e^{-i\\alpha}\\widetilde q)$, gauge\ncoupling $g>0$, zero superpotential $W=0$, canonical scalar kinetic terms,\nand zero Fayet--Iliopoulos parameter. In the tree-level model the auxiliary\nmaps are $F_q=F_{\\widetilde q}=0$ and\n$D=-g(|q|^2-|\\widetilde q|^2)$. The classical supersymmetric vacuum set is\ntherefore\n$\\{(q,\\widetilde q):|q|=|\\widetilde q|\\}/U(1)$. The gauge-invariant\ncoordinate $m=q\\widetilde q$ gives a bijection of this orbit set with\n$\\mathbb C$. This is a classical tree-level result and is not a claim about\nthe quantum vacuum space.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json ex-qftx-n1-sqcd-su-nf-cubic-anomaly-check statement= 'Let integers $N_c\\ge2$ and $N_f$ satisfy\n$\\frac32N_c<N_f<3N_c$, and put $\\widetilde N_c=N_f-N_c$. Consider the\nfollowing proposed four-dimensional $\\mathcal N=1$ SQCD field data, in\nnatural units. In the electric $SU(N_c)$ theory, there are $N_f$ quark\nchiral multiplets $Q$ in the fundamental of the flavor group\n$SU(N_f)_L$, with $N_c$ color copies; the antiquark multiplets are singlets\nunder $SU(N_f)_L$. In the proposed magnetic $SU(\\widetilde N_c)$ theory,\nthe quark multiplets $q$ are in the antifundamental of $SU(N_f)_L$, with\n$\\widetilde N_c$ color copies, and the meson multiplet $M$ transforms in\n$(\\mathbf N_f,\\overline{\\mathbf N_f})$ under\n$SU(N_f)_L\\times SU(N_f)_R$, giving $N_f$ copies of the fundamental of\n$SU(N_f)_L$. The magnetic antiquarks and both gauginos are\n$SU(N_f)_L$ singlets. Normalize the cubic-anomaly coefficient of a\nleft-handed Weyl fermion to $+1$ in $\\mathbf N_f$ and $-1$ in\n$\\overline{\\mathbf N_f}$, and add coefficients over independent copies.\nThen the electric and proposed magnetic $SU(N_f)_L^3$ coefficients are\n\n$$\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{electric}}=N_c,\\qquad \\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{magnetic}}=-(N_f-N_c)+N_f=N_c.$$\n\nThis equality is one anomaly-matching consistency check of the proposed\nfield data; it is not a proof of Seiberg duality or of equality of the\ninfrared continuum theories.' design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-33.pages.json ex-qftx-n2-monopole-perturbed-vacuum statement= "In the local dual $U(1)_D$ model, let $u(A_D)$ be holomorphic near\n$A_D=0$, let $c=u'(0)\\ne0$, and choose a neighborhood small enough that\n$u'(A_D)\\ne0$ throughout. Choose a nonzero small perturbation\n$\\mu$ for which $(|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2)^{1/2}$ is below the effective cutoff.\nThe supersymmetric vacuum equations have the gauge orbit represented by\n\n$$A_D=0,\\qquad M=v>0,\\qquad \\widetilde M=-\\frac{\\mu c}{\\sqrt2v},\\qquad v=\\left(\\frac{|\\mu c|}{\\sqrt2}\\right)^{1/2}.$$\n\nAt this representative $|M|=|\\widetilde M|=v$, so the D equation vanishes.\nThe dual $U(1)_D$ is fully Higgsed and the canonical dual vector mass is\n$m_D^2=4g_D^2v^2>0$. This is a symbolic local effective-model calculation.\nThe example does not assert existence of a finite-energy vortex or linear\nconfinement; the zero-perturbation case $\\mu=0$ is outside its scope." design_state= 'Step 1 carrier authored; item-specific proof/source contract and scoped checks are being completed.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-36.pages.json def-aqft-topological-defect statement= 'Fix an integer $d\\ge1$ and a smooth $d$-manifold $M$, possibly with boundary. Fix the metric and signature, orientation and any time orientation, the geometric background bundles or lifts required by the model, and the boundary conditions. A **selected model** $\\mathcal T$ on $M$ consists of a specified configuration space $\\mathcal C_{\\mathcal T}(M)$ of fields, including each field bundle, regularity class, and admissible boundary behavior; a specified collection of local observables and their supports; a set $\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal T}$ of admissible finite observable lists with boundary data; a declared value space $\\mathcal V_{\\mathcal T}$ (for example, $\\mathbb C$ or a specified distribution space); and a normalized correlation functional $C_{\\mathcal T}:\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal T}\\to\\mathcal V_{\\mathcal T}$. The model also fixes its unit system, action and charge normalizations, and any regulator, measure, or renormalization prescription used to define $C_{\\mathcal T}$. These are model inputs: this definition does not construct them or assume a general interacting continuum theory exists.\n\nA **defect insertion** in $\\mathcal T$ is data $(j,\\lambda)$ where $j:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M^\\circ$ is a smooth embedding of a manifold $\\Sigma$ without boundary whose image is closed in the interior $M^\\circ=M\\setminus\\partial M$, and $\\lambda$ is the model-specified local field, operator, or boundary-condition data supported on $j(\\Sigma)$. Its codimension is $d-\\dim\\Sigma$. The model must define the modified correlator $C_{\\mathcal T}(D_{j,\\lambda};O_1,\\ldots,O_n)$ for every allowed list of other insertions $O_j$. An orientation, coorientation, or framing is included in $\\lambda$ whenever the model needs it. A defect network is a finite list of such strata with a specified incidence relation; each allowed meeting stratum has a stated order of incident labels and a local junction operator inserted by the model. A list of strata or a picture without this field and junction data is not a defect insertion.\n\nAn **isotopy** of this insertion is a smooth family of embeddings $j_t:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M^\\circ$, $t\\in[0,1]$, that transports all included orientation, coorientation, framing, and junction data, leaves the boundary conditions fixed, and keeps every support disjoint from every other insertion throughout the deformation. The insertion is **topological in $\\mathcal T$** if, for every admissible list of other insertions and boundary data and every such isotopy, the model-defined correlators satisfy\n\n$$C_{\\mathcal T}(D_{j_0,\\lambda_0};O_1,\\ldots,O_n)=C_{\\mathcal T}(D_{j_1,\\lambda_1};O_1,\\ldots,O_n).$$\n\nThis is a property to verify for the specified model and insertion, not an automatic consequence of assigning a defect label or drawing a crossing. No physical quantity with units is introduced here; all dimensional quantities and normalizations remain those declared by $\\mathcal T$.' design_state= 'Assigned A-page definition authored from the exact future item contract.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-36.pages.json def-aqft-topological-interface statement= "Use the selected-model and defect-insertion data from the preceding defect definition: a model specifies its manifold, background and boundary data, fields with regularity conditions, unit conventions, and correlator functional; an insertion is a model-defined local modification supported on an embedded submanifold. Let $\\mathcal T_-$ and $\\mathcal T_+$ be two such models in the same spacetime dimension $d$ on the same smooth manifold $M$. Their metric or signature, background bundles, boundary conditions, field normalizations, and units must either agree or be related by an explicitly supplied identification. No such identification is inferred from names or equal dimensions.\n\nA **codimension-one interface** from $\\mathcal T_-$ to $\\mathcal T_+$ is data $(\\mathcal W,\\mathcal I)$ where $\\mathcal W\\hookrightarrow M^\\circ$ is a closed smooth embedded hypersurface with a chosen coorientation and a specified collar $\\mathcal W\\times(-\\epsilon,\\epsilon)$, $\\epsilon>0$. The negative side uses $\\mathcal T_-$ and the positive side uses $\\mathcal T_+$. The interface data $\\mathcal I$ is a specified local transmission condition, operator map, or boundary condition relating the one-sided field data in that collar, together with any orientation, framing, or junction labels required by the models. The combined model must assign a correlator $C_{\\mathcal T_-,\\mathcal T_+}(\\mathcal I(\\mathcal W);O_1,\\ldots,O_n)$ to every stated admissible list of insertions. The interface is **topological** when this correlator obeys the isotopy-invariance criterion of the preceding defect definition, with the two side labels and all interface data transported and no other insertion crossed.\n\nFor interfaces $\\mathcal I_{01}:\\mathcal T_0\\to\\mathcal T_1$ and $\\mathcal I_{12}:\\mathcal T_1\\to\\mathcal T_2$, define their **fusion** only when the model supplies a product collar containing two parallel interfaces at separation $\\delta>0$ and, as $\\delta\\downarrow0$, every declared correlator with the pair converges to the correlator of a single interface. The limiting insertion, its normalization, and its junction data are the fused interface $\\mathcal I_{12}\\star\\mathcal I_{01}:\\mathcal T_0\\to\\mathcal T_2$. If this limit is not part of the model or does not exist, no single fused interface is asserted.\n\nAn **endodefect** has $\\mathcal T_- = \\mathcal T_+$ as the same complete model data, not merely models with similarly named fields. The **transparent identity interface** $\\mathbf 1_{\\mathcal T}$ is the model-supplied transmission condition that identifies the two sides without changing any correlator. An endodefect is an **invertible symmetry interface** only if a two-sided inverse exists under the stated fusion operation, both composites equal $\\mathbf 1_{\\mathcal T}$ in all declared correlators, and the induced map is an automorphism of the model's observable and background data preserving its correlators. A topological interface between distinct source and target models is an interface, not an internal symmetry of either model. No supersymmetry or BPS condition is implicit; claiming one requires additional model data and a separate check of the specified supercharges and their domains.\n\nNo dimensional quantity is added by this definition. Each model and interface must retain its declared unit system and normalization, and this definition does not assert that a continuum fusion limit exists." design_state= 'Assigned A-page definition authored from the exact future item contract.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-36.pages.json ex-aqft-z2-topological-defect-finite-quantum-mechanics statement= 'Let $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C^2$ with its standard positive inner product, and let the Hamiltonian be the zero matrix $H=0$. This defines a finite-dimensional $0+1$-dimensional quantum-mechanical transfer model: propagation through an interval of length $s\\ge0$ is $e^{-sH}=I$, where $s$ is Euclidean time in units with $\\hbar=1$. The local observables are all matrices in $\\operatorname{End}(\\mathcal H)$, and a correlator with fixed boundary vectors is the matrix element of their time-ordered product with the propagation matrices between insertions.\n\nLet $G=\\mathbb Z_2=\\{0,1\\}$ with addition modulo $2$, and define $U_0=I$ and $U_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. Insert $U_g$ at an interior point $\\tau\\in(0,T)$ of the time interval $[0,T]$. For any finite ordered list of other fixed observable insertions, moving $\\tau$ within a gap that contains no other insertion leaves their order relative to $U_g$ unchanged. The insertion is a nontrivial invertible $\\mathbb Z_2$ symmetry defect of this model.' design_state= 'Owner-directed B-page finite-model example or counterexample, proved locally; no empirical or continuum claim.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-36.pages.json ex-aqft-invertible-interface-between-finite-quantum-mechanics statement= 'Use natural units $\\hbar=1$. Let the source model $\\mathcal T_-$ and target model $\\mathcal T_+$ each have Hilbert space $\\mathbb C^2$ with its standard positive inner product and all complex $2\\times2$ matrices as local observables. Their Hamiltonian matrices are\n\n$$H_- = \\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&\\Delta\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad H_+ = \\begin{pmatrix}\\Delta&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\Delta>0.$$\n\nHere $\\Delta$ has units of energy and a Euclidean time interval $s$ has units of inverse energy; propagation is $e^{-sH_\\pm}$. Define the interface map\n\n$$V=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}:\\mathcal H_-\\longrightarrow\\mathcal H_+.$$\n\nThe target observable corresponding to $O_-\\in\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^2)$ is $O_+=VO_-V^\\dagger$. For a single interface at time $\\tau\\in(0,T)$, its transfer amplitude between boundary vectors $\\psi_-\\in\\mathcal H_-$ and $\\psi_+\\in\\mathcal H_+$ is\n\n$$C_\\tau=\\langle\\psi_+,e^{-(T-\\tau)H_+}V e^{-\\tau H_-}\\psi_-\\rangle.$$\n\nThis is an invertible topological interface between two specified models. It is an internal symmetry only if the source and target models are subsequently identified as one complete model.' design_state= 'Owner-directed B-page finite-model example or counterexample, proved locally; no empirical or continuum claim.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-36.pages.json cex-aqft-topological-interface-not-symmetry statement= 'Every topological codimension-one interface between two selected quantum-mechanical models is an invertible internal symmetry of its source model.' design_state= 'Owner-directed B-page finite-model example or counterexample, proved locally; no empirical or continuum claim.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-37.pages.json def-aqft-p-form-global-symmetry statement= "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$, an integer $p$ with $0\\le p<d$, and a smooth $d$-dimensional spacetime manifold $M$, possibly with boundary. A selected model $\\mathcal T$ specifies its spacetime structure and boundary conditions; field configuration space, field bundles, regularity and admitted sectors; observables and their supports; normalized correlation functionals; and, when present, its action, regulator, measure, unit system and charge normalization. No such data are inferred from a familiar theory name.\n\nUse the preceding topological-defect definition with its exact content: a defect is model-specified field, operator or boundary-condition data on a closed embedded support, and it is topological when every declared correlator is invariant under each admissible isotopy that transports its data, avoids other insertions and preserves boundary data. For this symmetry, each defect is an endodefect insertion in the same selected model; the model supplies parallel fusion and the induced action on its admitted observables and backgrounds. A support $\\Sigma$ is the image of a smooth embedding $\\iota:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M^\\circ$ from a manifold without boundary, with closed image in the interior $M^\\circ$.\n\nA **group-like $p$-form global symmetry** of $\\mathcal T$ consists of a locally compact Hausdorff topological group $G$ and an assignment $g\\mapsto U_g(\\Sigma)$ of invertible topological symmetry defects to the allowed closed supports $\\Sigma$ of dimension $d-p-1$ and codimension $p+1$. The model must define the parallel fusion operation and a transparent identity defect, with $U_e(\\Sigma)=\\mathbf 1_{\\mathcal T}$ and $U_g(\\Sigma)\\star U_h(\\Sigma)=U_{gh}(\\Sigma)$ whenever that fusion is defined. Each $U_g$ has a two-sided inverse $U_{g^{-1}}$ under the supplied fusion law, and crossing it induces an automorphism preserving the declared observables, backgrounds and correlators. The group $G$ is **effective**: elements inducing identical actions in every admitted correlator and on every admitted observable and background are identified. A group element labels a transformation, not the charge of an observable.\n\nA **genuine $p$-dimensional observable** is an observable supported on a closed embedded $p$-dimensional submanifold whose definition is independent of an attached higher-dimensional support. Its symmetry action is defined by moving a symmetry defect across it, with the model specifying the resulting junction or operator action. For $p>0$, if crossing by every $U_g$ preserves the one-dimensional space $\\mathbb C O$ for a nonzero genuine observable $O$, write the scalar action as $\\chi_O(g)O$. If the resulting map $\\chi_O:G\\to U(1)$ is a continuous homomorphism in a model where $G$ is Abelian, call $\\chi_O$ the character charge of $O$; it is an element of $\\widehat G$. This definition does not assert scalarity, continuity or multiplicativity for every observable. For $p=0$, local observables may carry higher-dimensional representations of a non-Abelian group; the representation label is distinct from the group element implementing a transformation. For a locally compact Hausdorff Abelian group $A$, write its character group as $\\widehat A=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{cts}}(A,U(1))$, with pointwise multiplication. Here $U(1)=\\{e^{i\\alpha}:\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\}$, so every character value and its argument are dimensionless.\n\nA **background** for a symmetry is nondynamical model data, not summed over. For a finite Abelian group $A$, fix a triangulation $K$ of $M$ and let $C^k(K;A)$ be the group of functions from oriented $k$-simplices to $A$, with orientation reversal acting by negation. Its coboundary is the alternating sum over faces,\n\n$$\n(\\delta c)([v_0,\\ldots,v_{k+1}])=\\sum_{j=0}^{k+1}(-1)^j c([v_0,\\ldots,\\widehat v_j,\\ldots,v_{k+1}]).\n$$\n\nSince $\\delta^2=0$ by pairwise cancellation of twice-deleted faces, a discrete $(p+1)$-form background with no higher-group mixing is a cocycle $B\\in C^{p+1}(K;A)$ modulo $B\\mapsto B+\\delta\\Lambda$ for $\\Lambda\\in C^p(K;A)$. Relative cochains or boundary-fixed gauge transformations are used when required by the model's boundary conditions. If other backgrounds mix with $B$, the model must give the modified cocycle and gauge-transformation laws.\n\nFor a continuous $U(1)^r$ $p$-form symmetry, a background consists of model-supplied local $(p+1)$-form potentials, their overlap and lower-overlap transition data, a globally specified curvature locally given by $H=dB$, and the small and large gauge transformations with their holonomy and integral-flux normalization. Small transformations have the local form $B\\mapsto B+d\\Lambda$ for a $p$-form $\\Lambda$. The unit system and all coefficients in background couplings remain model inputs. The model must define its correlators in these backgrounds; this definition asserts neither that every symmetry admits such a coupling nor that the symmetry can be gauged." design_state= 'Codimension-general topological-defect prerequisite is current and ready. Direct dependency corrected from the supplier codimension-one interface definition; owner escalation is recorded for this cross-batch edge change.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-37.pages.json pthm-aqft-p-form-charge-characters statement= 'Let $d\\ge2$, $1\\le p<d$, and let $\\mathcal T$ have a group-like $p$-form global symmetry $G$ as defined above. Assume the selected model has a unitary Hilbert-space realization and, for every $g,h\\in G$, admits two parallel copies of their codimension-$(p+1)$ symmetry supports inside a contractible neighborhood with a disk spanned by two independent normal directions, together with an isotopy exchanging their order without intersecting any other insertion or changing boundary data. Then $G$ is Abelian.\n\nLet $O(C)$ be a nonzero genuine $p$-dimensional operator bounded in the operator norm of the selected Hilbert-space representation. Call it **simple for this result** when each topological local junction endomorphism on $O$ produced by passing a symmetry defect across it is a scalar multiple of $O$. Assume the induced action on the one-dimensional space spanned by $O$ is continuous and unitary. There is a continuous character $\\chi_O:G\\to U(1)$ such that passing $U_g$ across $O$ multiplies it by $\\chi_O(g)$. Thus the charge of $O$ belongs to the Pontryagin dual $\\widehat G=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G,U(1))$. This theorem makes no claim for $p=0$ or for operators with nonscalar crossing endomorphisms.' design_state= 'Complete conditional proof authored; its p-form definition prerequisite is current and ready.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-37.pages.json ex-aqft-maxwell-magnetic-one-form-charge statement= 'Use natural units $\\hbar=c=1$ and $M=\\mathbb R^4$ with its standard orientation and Euclidean metric. The selected field model is pure compact Maxwell theory with actual gauge group $U(1)=\\{e^{i\\alpha}:\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\}$, no dynamical electric or magnetic matter, and a smooth right principal $U(1)$-bundle with connection on the complement of admitted external line insertions. A principal connection is a smooth $\\mathfrak u(1)$-valued one-form reproducing each fundamental vertical vector and equivariant under the right group action. Identify $\\mathfrak u(1)=i\\mathbb R$ with $\\mathbb R$ so its local potentials $A_i$ have holonomy $\\exp(i\\int A_i)$ and the real curvature $F$ obeys $F|_{U_i}=dA_i$, since the Abelian Lie bracket vanishes. The model fixes the action convention $S[A]=(2e^2)^{-1}\\int F\\wedge *F$ with dimensionless $e>0$, integrating outside any prescribed line-core tube with its declared boundary condition. It also fixes the allowed bundle sectors, boundary behavior at infinity, and any regulator or measure; no continuum measure is constructed here. With this normalization $[A_i]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $[F]=\\mathrm{length}^{-2}$, and $\\int_\\Sigma F$ is dimensionless.\n\nLet $C$ be a closed smooth oriented curve carrying an admitted genuine external magnetic line $T_m(C)$, where genuine means that no auxiliary surface is attached to define the insertion. Its integer charge $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ is specified by $\\int_{S^2_{\\mathrm{link}}}F=2\\pi m$ on every sufficiently small oriented normal two-sphere linking $C$. The model has no other magnetic sources, and its normalized correlation functional is assumed to be defined for these insertions.\n\nFor a closed smooth oriented surface $\\Sigma$ disjoint from the line, and $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, define the magnetic surface operator\n\n$$\nU_\\alpha(\\Sigma)=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{i\\alpha}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma F\\right).\n$$\n\nFor this example, a smooth singular three-chain is a finite real linear combination of smooth maps from the standard 3-simplex, with boundary the alternating sum of its face maps. For a source-free move, the model supplies a compact smooth singular three-chain $V$ in $M\\setminus C$ with $\\partial V=\\Sigma_1-\\Sigma_0$. For a move across the line, the model supplies a compact oriented smooth three-dimensional track $W$ in $M$, represented as a finite smooth singular chain, with endpoint boundary $\\partial W=\\Sigma_1-\\Sigma_0$; its support is a smooth embedded three-manifold near each of its finitely many intersections with $C$, and the intersections are transverse interior points. Transverse means that the tangent space of this three-manifold and the tangent line of $C$ span $T_xM$. Excise a small open three-ball in each such regular neighborhood. The model supplies a source-free collar in the punctured tubular neighborhood identifying each resulting boundary sphere with a small oriented normal two-sphere $S_j^2$ linking $C$. The resulting punctured chain $V$ lies in $M\\setminus C$ and has $\\partial V=\\Sigma_1-\\Sigma_0-\\sum_j\\epsilon_jS_j^2$, where $\\epsilon_j$ is the oriented intersection sign of $W$ with $C$. Define the algebraic intersection number $I(W,C)=\\sum_j\\epsilon_j$. The claimed action is that a source-free move leaves $U_\\alpha$ unchanged, while a move across the line multiplies it by $e^{i\\alpha m I(W,C)}$. In particular, the line carries the character $\\chi_m(e^{i\\alpha})=e^{i\\alpha m}$ of the magnetic one-form $U(1)$ symmetry.' design_state= 'The field-configuration proof derives the U(1) character directly from the integer flux data, so it does not import the abstract theorem or assume that the line satisfies its Hilbert-space simplicity hypotheses.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-37.pages.json ex-aqft-s3-zero-form-representation-labels statement= "Let $M=S^1_\\beta$ be Euclidean time of circumference $\\beta>0$, let $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C^3$ with orthonormal basis $e_1,e_2,e_3$, and let the Hamiltonian be $H_0=0$. Use natural units $\\hbar=1$. The finite model's normalized correlation functional for time-ordered operator insertions is $C(O_1,\\ldots,O_n)=\\frac13\\operatorname{Tr}(O_1\\cdots O_n)$. Let $S_3$ be the group of permutations of $\\{1,2,3\\}$, with composition $(\\sigma\\tau)(j)=\\sigma(\\tau(j))$, and define the unitary action $\\rho(\\sigma)e_j=e_{\\sigma(j)}$. A symmetry defect at time $t$ is $U_\\sigma(t)=\\rho(\\sigma)$.\n\nThe state space decomposes as $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C(e_1+e_2+e_3)\\oplus V_2$, where $V_2=\\{(z_1,z_2,z_3)\\in\\mathbb C^3:z_1+z_2+z_3=0\\}$. The first summand is the trivial representation and $V_2$ is the two-dimensional standard irreducible representation. The representation label of a state sector is therefore either the trivial representation or the standard two-dimensional representation; a permutation $\\sigma\\in S_3$ is instead a group element implementing a symmetry transformation. This is an ordinary $p=0$ example and does not conflict with the exchange theorem for $p\\ge1$." design_state= 'Complete finite-model verification authored; the p-form and character-theorem suppliers are current and ready.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-38.pages.json def-aqft-two-group-symmetry-realization statement= "Fix an integer $d\\geq2$, a closed compact oriented smooth $d$-manifold $M$, and a finite ordered triangulation $K$ of $M$, meaning a finite vertex-ordered simplicial complex whose geometric realization is $M$. The model fixes an orientation and any additional spin or related lifting data required by its fields; these choices are its tangential structure. A **selected finite-regulator Euclidean model** on $K$ specifies its exact symmetry groups, field representations, finite field-value sets $X_\\sigma$ for cells $\\sigma\\in K$, constraints, and configuration spaces $\\Phi_x\\subseteq\\prod_{\\sigma\\in K}X_\\sigma$ for each background $x$. It also specifies a dimensionless Euclidean action $S_x:\\Phi_x\\to\\mathbb R$, finite measure weights $\\mu_x:\\Phi_x\\to\\mathbb C$, and a unital algebra $\\mathcal O_x$ of model-defined observables, represented as functions on $\\Phi_x$. An observable is **local** when its function depends only on field variables on a specified subcomplex of $K$; a line observable has support on a one-dimensional subcomplex. Write $W_x(\\phi):=\\mu_x(\\phi)e^{-S_x(\\phi)}$. For local insertion functions $o_{j,x}:\\Phi_x\\to\\mathbb C$, define the unnormalized correlator by $C_x(O_1,\\ldots,O_n):=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\Phi_x}W_x(\\phi)\\prod_{j=1}^n o_{j,x}(\\phi)$; the empty tuple has value $Z_x=C_x()$. Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$; cochain labels, action phases, and the partition weight are dimensionless. The model separately assigns dimensions to any continuous field quantities or observables it includes. No continuum limit or Lorentzian continuation is part of this definition.\n\nLet $G$ be the selected finite internal $0$-form symmetry group. Its action on the model's local field and observable data is specified by group homomorphisms into the corresponding automorphism groups. Let $A$ be the selected finite abelian $1$-form symmetry group, written additively, with the exact support, charge, and background conventions of the selected p-form symmetry model. Each generator $U_a(\\Sigma)$ is a defect insertion with support $j(\\Sigma)$, where $\\Sigma$ is a smooth manifold without boundary and $j:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M$ is a smooth embedding with closed codimension-two image represented by a subcomplex of $K$; the insertion includes the model-specified local field or operator data and any required orientation, coorientation, framing, or junction data on that image. It is topological when the model's correlators are invariant under permitted isotopies of that support avoiding other insertions; an allowed isotopy is a piecewise-smooth family of such embeddings in $M$ with disjoint supports and transported defect data. The generators fuse by $U_aU_{a'}=U_{a+a'}$. A genuine line observable is a line-supported insertion defined without an attached surface or other defect. Its $A$-charge is a character $\\chi_\\gamma\\in\\widehat A:=\\operatorname{Hom}(A,U(1))$ such that sweeping $U_a(\\Sigma)$ once across that line in the chosen positive orientation multiplies the correlator by $\\chi_\\gamma(a)$. The selected $0$-form symmetry acts on these defect labels through a specified left action $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(A)$.\n\nChoose a normalized inhomogeneous $3$-cocycle $\\beta:G^3\\to A$ for this left action; normalized means it vanishes if any argument is the identity, and the cocycle equation is\n\n$$\n\\rho_g\\beta(h,k,l)-\\beta(gh,k,l)+\\beta(g,hk,l)-\\beta(g,h,kl)+\\beta(g,h,k)=0.\n$$\n\nFor a normalized $2$-cochain $c:G^2\\to A$, set\n\n$$\n(\\delta_\\rho c)(g,h,k):=\\rho_g c(h,k)-c(gh,k)+c(g,hk)-c(g,h).\n$$\n\nTwo normalized $3$-cocycles represent the same Postnikov class when their difference is $\\delta_\\rho c$ for such a normalized $c$; $[\\beta]$ denotes this equivalence class. The skeletal associator convention is directed from $(gh)k$ to $g(hk)$. A discrete background is an object $x=(a,B)$ of the finite background gauge $2$-groupoid on $K$: $a_{ij}\\in G$ is a flat edge cochain, $a_{ij}a_{jk}=a_{ik}$ on every ordered triangle; $B_{ijk}\\in A$ is a face cochain at its first vertex; and on every ordered tetrahedron\n\n$$\n(\\delta_a B)_{ijkl}:=\\rho_{a_{ij}}B_{jkl}-B_{ikl}+B_{ijl}-B_{ijk}=\\beta(a_{ij},a_{jk},a_{kl})=(a^*\\beta)_{ijkl}.\n$$\n\nThus the two background fields are constrained and are not independent. A $1$-gauge arrow is given by vertex labels $g_i\\in G$ and edge labels $\\lambda_{ij}\\in A$, with\n\n$$\na'_{ij}=g_i a_{ij}g_j^{-1},\\qquad \\zeta^g_{ijk}=-\\beta(g_i,a_{ij},a_{jk})+\\beta(a'_{ij},g_j,a_{jk})-\\beta(a'_{ij},a'_{jk},g_k),\n$$\n\n$$\nB'_{ijk}=\\rho_{g_i}B_{ijk}+\\zeta^g_{ijk}+\\rho_{a'_{ij}}\\lambda_{jk}-\\lambda_{ik}+\\lambda_{ij}.\n$$\n\nA $2$-arrow between $1$-arrows with the same vertex labels $g$ and edge labels $\\lambda,\\lambda'$ is a vertex $A$-cochain $t$ satisfying $\\lambda'-\\lambda=\\delta_{a'}t$, where $(\\delta_{a'}t)_{ij}=\\rho_{a'_{ij}}t_j-t_i$; vertical composition adds these cochains. For consecutive $1$-arrows $(g,\\lambda)$ then $(h,\\mu)$, their composite has vertex labels $hg$ and edge labels $\\nu_{ij}=\\rho_{h_i}\\lambda_{ij}+\\mu_{ij}+\\tau_{ij}$, where $\\tau_{ij}=\\beta(h_i,g_i,a_{ij})-\\beta(h_i,a'_{ij},g_j)+\\beta(a''_{ij},h_j,g_j)$ and $a''_{ij}=h_i a'_{ij}h_j^{-1}$. Horizontal composition sends $2$-arrow cochains $t,s$ to $s+\\rho_h t$. For three consecutive arrows with vertex labels $g,h,k$, the associator is the vertex cochain $\\sigma_i=\\beta(k_i,h_i,g_i)$; identity arrows have vertex labels $e$ and edge labels $0$, and unit constraints are identity $2$-arrows. The pentagon and triangle coherence equations are those of this finite background gauge $2$-groupoid.\n\nA **finite 2-group background coupling** in the selected model assigns to each finite 2-group background its configuration space, local observable algebra, action, measure, and partition function $Z_x=C_x()$, and assigns to every $1$-gauge arrow $f:x\\to y$ a specified bijection $T_f:\\Phi_x\\to\\Phi_y$ and observable-algebra isomorphism $\\alpha_f:\\mathcal O_x\\to\\mathcal O_y$. Every $2$-arrow is assigned its specified intertwiner, and all assignments must obey the identity, composition, associator, and unit coherence laws of the background gauge $2$-groupoid. The action and measure are model inputs; the finite 2-group data alone do not choose them or determine their transformation phase.\n\nA permitted background counterterm is a model-declared dimensionless functional $L_x\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ that is a finite sum of functions, each depending only on cochain labels in one simplex or a specified finite cluster of simplices; it reweights the model by $e^{iL_x}$. If under a gauge arrow $f:x\\to y$ all unnormalized correlators transform by a common phase $\\chi_f(x)\\in U(1)$, coherence requires $\\chi_{\\mathrm{id}_x}(x)=1$, $\\chi_{g\\circ f}(x)=\\chi_g(y)\\chi_f(x)$ for composable arrows, and equal phases for $2$-isomorphic arrows. The residual background anomaly is the class of these phases under $\\chi_f(x)\\mapsto e^{iL_y}\\chi_f(x)e^{-iL_x}$ for the permitted counterterms. The anomaly is absent in the selected scheme exactly when an allowed counterterm makes every such phase $1$. The Postnikov class specifies the background constraint and gauge transformations; it is not itself the model's transformation phase or anomaly. It may enter an anomaly calculation for a specified action and measure." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; the statement, complete argument, placement, proof contract and exact dependencies are recorded. All direct suppliers are current-ready and their interfaces have been checked.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-38.pages.json pthm-aqft-two-group-background-coupling statement= "Let $K$ be a finite ordered triangulation of a closed compact oriented smooth $d$-manifold, $d\\geq2$, with its chosen tangential structure and finite 2-group background gauge $2$-groupoid as specified in the finite realization definition. For each background object $x$, let the selected Euclidean finite-regulator model have a finite configuration set $\\Phi_x$, dimensionless action $S_x:\\Phi_x\\to\\mathbb R$, measure weight $\\mu_x:\\Phi_x\\to\\mathbb C$, and total weight $W_x(\\phi):=\\mu_x(\\phi)e^{-S_x(\\phi)}$, in natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. For an ordered tuple of $n\\geq0$ local observable insertions $O_1,\\ldots,O_n$, let $o_{j,x}:\\Phi_x\\to\\mathbb C$ be the model's insertion function and define the unnormalized correlator\n\n$$\nC_x(O_1,\\ldots,O_n):=\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\Phi_x}W_x(\\phi)\\prod_{j=1}^n o_{j,x}(\\phi),\n$$\n\nwhere the empty product gives the partition function $Z_x=C_x()$. Suppose every $1$-gauge arrow $f:x\\to y$ has a model-specified bijection $T_f:\\Phi_x\\to\\Phi_y$, the observable transport satisfies $o_{j,y}(T_f\\phi)=o_{j,x}(\\phi)$, and the adopted action-and-measure law is\n\n$$\nW_y(T_f\\phi)=\\chi_f(x)W_x(\\phi),\n$$\n\nwith a model-specified phase $\\chi_f(x)\\in U(1)$ independent of $\\phi$. Assume these phases are $1$ on identity arrows, multiply under composition, and agree on $2$-isomorphic arrows. Then for every such arrow and observable tuple,\n\n$$\nC_y(\\alpha_fO_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_fO_n)=\\chi_f(x)C_x(O_1,\\ldots,O_n),\\qquad Z_y=\\chi_f(x)Z_x.\n$$\n\nIf an allowed local background counterterm $L_x$ reweights the model by $e^{iL_x}$, the phase changes to $\\chi'_f(x)=e^{iL_y}\\chi_f(x)e^{-iL_x}$. By the stated anomaly convention, the residual anomaly is absent exactly when one permitted counterterm makes every phase $1$. The Postnikov cocycle $\\beta$ fixes the allowed background cochains and gauge arrows but does not, by itself, specify $\\chi_f$; that phase is determined by the selected model's action and measure. The result is confined to the stated finite regulator and does not assert a continuum QFT or a universal anomaly classification." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; the statement, complete argument, placement, proof contract and exact dependencies are recorded. All direct suppliers are current-ready and their interfaces have been checked.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-38.pages.json ex-aqft-two-group-split-background-coupling statement= "Let $K=\\partial\\Delta^4$ be the ordered boundary complex of a $4$-simplex with vertices $0<1<2<3<4$, a finite triangulation of the closed oriented $3$-sphere. Take $G=A=\\mathbb Z_2$ with trivial action and $\\beta=0$, and set every edge label $a_{ij}=0$ and every face label $B_{ijk}=0$. In the selected skeletal model the tensor product is $(g,u)\\otimes(h,v)=(g+h,u+v)$ and the associator is zero. In the finite unit-response model, let the unique field configuration have weight $1$. For the $1$-gauge arrow with all vertex labels $g_i=0$, edge label $\\lambda_{01}=1$, and every other edge label zero, the transformed edge cochain is still zero; the transformed face cochain has $B'_{012}=B'_{013}=B'_{014}=1$ and every other face label zero. Since $B'=\\delta\\lambda$, its tetrahedron coboundary vanishes on every tetrahedron, so $\\delta_aB'=0=a'^*\\beta$. The partition weight remains $1$, so this gauge transformation has phase $1$. This is a finite background-response example, not a claim of a faithful continuum QFT realization." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; the statement, complete argument, placement, proof contract and exact dependencies are recorded. All direct suppliers are current-ready and their interfaces have been checked.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-38.pages.json ex-aqft-two-group-nonzero-postnikov-unit-response statement= "Let $K=\\partial\\Delta^4$ be the ordered boundary complex of a $4$-simplex with vertices $0<1<2<3<4$, a finite triangulation of the closed oriented $3$-sphere. Let $G=A=\\mathbb Z_2$ under addition with trivial action, and define $\\beta(g,h,k)=ghk$ modulo $2$. On vertices set $q=(0,1,0,1,0)$, put $a_{ij}=q_j-q_i$, and define $B_{ijk}=q_i a_{ij}a_{jk}+c_{jk}-c_{ik}+c_{ij}$ modulo $2$, where the edge cochain $c$ has $c_{01}=c_{03}=c_{23}=1$ and all other edge labels zero. Then $\\beta$ is a normalized $3$-cocycle and is not the coboundary of any normalized $2$-cochain, so its Postnikov class is nonzero under the cohomology convention of the background definition. The edge cochain is flat, and $\\delta_aB=a^*\\beta$ on every tetrahedron. On tetrahedron $0123$, $B_{012}=1$ and $B_{013}=B_{023}=B_{123}=0$, so $\\delta_aB=1$. For the $0$-form gauge labels $g_0=1$, $g_1=g_2=g_3=g_4=0$ and all $1$-form labels zero, the transformed edges on $0123$ are $a'_{01}=0$, $a'_{12}=a'_{23}=a'_{02}=1$, and $a'_{13}=a'_{03}=0$; all four transformed face labels on that tetrahedron vanish. In the finite unit-response model, the partition weight remains $1$ and the gauge phase is trivial despite the nonzero Postnikov class. This is a finite response example, not a continuum QFT realization." design_state= 'Step 1 authored; the statement, complete argument, placement, proof contract and exact dependencies are recorded. All direct suppliers are current-ready and their interfaces have been checked.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-39.pages.json def-aqft-ising-fusion-data statement= 'Let the scalar field be $\\mathbb C$ and let the simple labels be $\\mathbf 1$, $\\psi$, and $\\sigma$. Define a $\\mathbb C$-linear semisimple category candidate whose objects are finite direct sums of these labels, whose simple-label Hom space is $\\mathbb C$ when the labels agree and $0$ otherwise, and whose Hom spaces between direct sums are the corresponding finite matrices. Give the simple-label fusion product by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf 1\\otimes a=a\\otimes\\mathbf 1=a,\\qquad \\psi\\otimes\\psi=\\mathbf 1,\\qquad \\psi\\otimes\\sigma=\\sigma\\otimes\\psi=\\sigma,\\qquad \\sigma\\otimes\\sigma=\\mathbf 1\\oplus\\psi.\n$$\n\nExtend the product bilinearly to direct sums and on morphisms by the induced tensor product of scalar maps. The unit is $\\mathbf 1$ and the unitors are strict. Fusion spaces are $V_{ab}^c=\\operatorname{Hom}(a\\otimes b,c)$; each is one-dimensional when the displayed fusion rule allows $c$ and is zero otherwise. In every allowed one-dimensional fusion space choose its displayed basis vector. On three simple labels, define the associator in these bases by the matrix $[F^{abc}_d]_{xy}$, where $x$ is the intermediate label in $(a\\otimes b)\\otimes c\\to d$ and $y$ is the intermediate label in $a\\otimes(b\\otimes c)\\to d$. Set\n\n$$\n\\left[F^{\\sigma\\sigma\\sigma}_{\\sigma}\\right]_{xy}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}_{xy},\\qquad x,y\\in\\{\\mathbf 1,\\psi\\},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[F^{\\psi\\sigma\\psi}_{\\sigma}\\right]_{\\sigma\\sigma}=-1,\\qquad \\left[F^{\\sigma\\psi\\sigma}_{\\psi}\\right]_{\\sigma\\sigma}=-1.\n$$\n\nEvery other coefficient is $1$ when all four fusion vertices are allowed and $0$ when any required fusion vertex is forbidden. Extend each associator linearly over direct sums. The candidate dual of each simple label is itself. For $\\mathbf 1$ and $\\psi$, take evaluation and coevaluation to be the unit-channel basis maps with coefficient $1$. For $\\sigma$, take evaluation $\\sigma\\otimes\\sigma\\to\\mathbf 1$ and coevaluation $\\mathbf 1\\to\\sigma\\otimes\\sigma$ to have coefficient $2^{1/4}$ in the $\\mathbf 1$ channel and zero in the $\\psi$ channel. This item specifies the data; the separate coherence result checks that they satisfy the fusion-category axioms. No braiding or physical realization is included in this definition.' design_state= 'A-page local mathematical definition of exact Ising fusion channels, F-symbols and duality maps needed for the physical realization.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-39.pages.json lem-aqft-ising-fusion-data-coherence statement= 'For the complex-linear Ising fusion-data package with simple labels $\\mathbf 1,\\psi,\\sigma$, fusion rules $\\psi\\otimes\\psi\\cong\\mathbf 1$, $\\psi\\otimes\\sigma\\cong\\sigma\\otimes\\psi\\cong\\sigma$, and $\\sigma\\otimes\\sigma\\cong\\mathbf 1\\oplus\\psi$, with the associator and dual maps specified below, the associator satisfies the pentagon, the strict unitors satisfy the triangle, and the evaluation and coevaluation maps satisfy both snake identities. Consequently the package is a finite semisimple rigid monoidal category with simple unit, hence a finite semisimple fusion category. With the chosen fusion-space basis orthonormal and the standard Hermitian inner products, its associators are unitary.' design_state= 'A-page local mathematical coherence result with an exact exhaustive pentagon check; no physical premises.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-39.pages.json pthm-aqft-noninvertible-defect-realization statement= "Fix an integer $L\\ge2$ and the periodic site set $\\mathbb Z/L\\mathbb Z$. Let $\\mathcal H_L=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z_2^L)$ with orthonormal basis $|h_1,\\ldots,h_L\\rangle$, where every $h_j\\in\\mathbb Z_2$ and $h_0=h_L$. Define Pauli operators by $Z_j|\\mathbf h\\rangle=(-1)^{h_j}|\\mathbf h\\rangle$ and $X_j|\\mathbf h\\rangle=|\\mathbf h+\\mathbf e_j\\rangle$. Adopt the finite quantum Ising model with Hamiltonian\n\n$$\nH_L=-J\\sum_{j=1}^{L}(Z_jZ_{j+1}+X_j),\\qquad J>0,\n$$\n\nwhere $Z_{L+1}=Z_1$, $J$ has units of energy, and $\\hbar=1$. Euclidean propagation through a time interval $s\\ge0$ is the exact finite matrix $e^{-sH_L}$, with $s$ in inverse-energy units. Correlators are matrix elements between arbitrary boundary vectors of finite ordered lists of observable matrices and these propagation factors.\n\nLet $\\widehat{\\mathcal H}_L=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z_2^L)$ have basis $|\\widehat h_{1/2},\\ldots,\\widehat h_{L-1/2}\\rangle$, with dual Pauli operators $\\widehat Z$ and $\\widehat X$ defined by the same formulas on half-integer sites. Define the Kramers–Wannier defect map $D_L:\\mathcal H_L\\to\\widehat{\\mathcal H}_L$ by\n\n$$\n\\langle\\widehat{\\mathbf h}|D_L|\\mathbf h\\rangle=2^{-L/2}(-1)^{\\sum_{j=1}^{L}\\widehat h_{j-1/2}(h_{j-1}+h_j)}.\n$$\n\nIdentify the dual chain with the original chain by the half-site shift $j+1/2\\mapsto j$; under this identification $D_L$ is an endodefect. A defect line is a wrapping insertion of $D_L$ on a Euclidean time slice. Its allowed position changes are slides within gaps between fixed observable insertions. In this finite lattice model, topologicality means invariance under the declared local line slides and re-association moves; no smooth continuum isotopy claim is made. A finite defect network has labels $\\mathbf 1,\\psi,\\sigma$, represented on the chain by $I,P_L,D_L$, where $P_L=\\prod_jX_j$. Its fusion spaces are one-dimensional precisely for\n\n$$\n\\mathbf 1\\otimes a=a\\otimes\\mathbf 1=a,\\qquad \\psi\\otimes\\psi=\\mathbf 1,\\qquad \\psi\\otimes\\sigma=\\sigma\\otimes\\psi=\\sigma,\\qquad \\sigma\\otimes\\sigma=\\mathbf 1\\oplus\\psi,\n$$\n\nand zero otherwise. Fix the Ising junction bases and associator as follows. Write $\\epsilon(\\mathbf 1)=0$ and $\\epsilon(\\psi)=1$; then\n\n$$\n[F^{\\sigma\\sigma\\sigma}_{\\sigma}]_{ab}=2^{-1/2}(-1)^{\\epsilon(a)\\epsilon(b)},\\qquad a,b\\in\\{\\mathbf 1,\\psi\\},\n$$\n\n$$\n[F^{\\psi\\sigma\\psi}_{\\sigma}]_{\\sigma\\sigma}=-1,\\qquad [F^{\\sigma\\psi\\sigma}_{\\psi}]_{\\sigma\\sigma}=-1,\n$$\n\nand every other allowed associator coefficient is $1$. In the local height basis, the nontrivial trivalent tensors with cyclic labels $(\\sigma,\\sigma,\\psi)$ and $(\\sigma,\\psi,\\sigma)$ are $2^{-1/4}(-1)^h$ and $(X)_{hh'}=\\delta_{h',h+1}$, respectively; reversed orientations use adjoint tensors. Together with the unit-channel maps specified above, these are the adopted local junction and re-association data of this finite network model; they are not inferred from fusion multiplicities alone.\n\nFor this selected finite model, $D_L$ is topological under the declared defect slides, its exact operator fusion is $D_L^2=I+P_L$, $P_L^2=I$, and $D_LP_L=P_LD_L=D_L$, and the junction spaces and associators are the finite semisimple unitary Ising fusion data above. In particular $D_L$ is non-invertible. The claim is confined to this finite lattice model and asserts no continuum limit or empirical fact." design_state= 'Assigned conditional finite-lattice physical theorem with explicit Hamiltonian, defect operator, fusion and junction data.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-39.pages.json ex-aqft-ising-fusion-object-noninvertible statement= 'Let $\\mathcal I$ be the complex finite semisimple fusion category with simple objects $\\mathbf 1,\\psi,\\sigma$, fusion rules $\\psi\\otimes\\psi\\cong\\mathbf 1$, $\\psi\\otimes\\sigma\\cong\\sigma\\otimes\\psi\\cong\\sigma$, and $\\sigma\\otimes\\sigma\\cong\\mathbf 1\\oplus\\psi$. Encode the intermediate labels by $\\epsilon(\\mathbf 1)=0$ and $\\epsilon(\\psi)=1$. Its coherent Ising associator has non-identity coefficients $[F^{\\sigma\\sigma\\sigma}_{\\sigma}]_{ab}=2^{-1/2}(-1)^{\\epsilon(a)\\epsilon(b)}$ for $a,b\\in\\{\\mathbf 1,\\psi\\}$, $[F^{\\psi\\sigma\\psi}_{\\sigma}]_{\\sigma\\sigma}=-1$, and $[F^{\\sigma\\psi\\sigma}_{\\psi}]_{\\sigma\\sigma}=-1$; every other allowed associator coefficient is $1$. The simple object $\\sigma$ has no right tensor inverse and is therefore a simple non-invertible object. This is an abstract algebraic example and makes no physical-realization claim.' design_state= 'B-page abstract finite algebraic example using the exact local mathematical suppliers; no physical-realization claim.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-39.pages.json ex-aqft-ising-chain-three-site-noninvertible-defect statement= 'Let $\\mathcal H_3=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z_2^3)$ with orthonormal basis $|h_1,h_2,h_3\\rangle$, and define $Z_j|\\mathbf h\\rangle=(-1)^{h_j}|\\mathbf h\\rangle$ and $X_j|\\mathbf h\\rangle=|\\mathbf h+\\mathbf e_j\\rangle$. Use periodic indices and the finite Hamiltonian $H_3=-J\\sum_{j=1}^3(Z_jZ_{j+1}+X_j)$, where $J>0$ has energy units and $\\hbar=1$. Euclidean propagation through $s\\ge0$ is $e^{-sH_3}$. Define $D_3$ from this ring to its half-shifted dual by\n\n$$\n\\langle\\widehat{\\mathbf h}|D_3|\\mathbf h\\rangle=2^{-3/2}(-1)^{\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\widehat h_{j-1/2}(h_{j-1}+h_j)},\\qquad h_0=h_3,\n$$\n\nand identify dual sites with original sites by the half-shift. Let $P_3=X_1X_2X_3$ and $v=|000\\rangle-|111\\rangle$. The defect inserted on a Euclidean time slice is topological under the declared slides within gaps of fixed insertions, satisfies $D_3^2=I+P_3$, and annihilates $v$ after two fusions. Therefore this exact finite-model defect is non-invertible. No continuum or empirical claim is made.' design_state= 'B-page exact three-site finite-Hamiltonian example of a topological non-invertible defect; no continuum or empirical claim.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json def-qftx-nonabelian-brst-complex statement= '\nLet $n\\geq 1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty open set with coordinates $x^1,\\ldots,x^n$, and let $\\mathfrak g$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra with bilinear alternating bracket $[\\ ,\\ ]_{\\mathfrak g}$ satisfying the Jacobi identity. Let $V=V_{\\bar 0}\\oplus V_{\\bar 1}$ be a finite-dimensional $\\mathbb Z_2$-graded complex vector space, and let $\\rho:\\mathfrak g\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$ be an even representation, meaning $\\rho([X,Y]_{\\mathfrak g})=\\rho(X)\\rho(Y)-\\rho(Y)\\rho(X)$ for all $X,Y\\in\\mathfrak g$ and each $\\rho(X)$ preserves the grading of $V$. This is local Lie-algebra data; no global group, principal bundle, action functional, or quantum theory is selected here.\n\nChoose a basis $T_a$ of $\\mathfrak g$ and write $[T_b,T_c]_{\\mathfrak g}=f^a{}_{bc}T_a$. A local gauge potential is a $\\mathfrak g$-valued one-form $A=A_\\mu^aT_a\\,dx^\\mu$ whose component jet generators $A_{\\mu,I}^a$ are even and have ghost number zero. A matter field is a $V$-valued local field $\\psi$ with each component jet generator $\\psi_I^\\alpha$ assigned the parity of its graded component and ghost number zero. The ghost is $c=c^aT_a$, with odd component jet generators $c_I^a$ of ghost number one. The coefficient bracket of homogeneous $\\mathfrak g$-valued fields is $[X,Y]^a=f^a{}_{bc}X^bY^c$, using multiplication in the graded-commutative coefficient algebra; in particular $[c,c]^a=f^a{}_{bc}c^bc^c$ has no factor of $1/2$.\n\nFor a multi-index $I=(I_1,\\ldots,I_n)\\in\\mathbb N^n$, let $\\Phi_I$ denote the corresponding formal jet generator of a field component $\\Phi$. The finite-jet local algebra $\\mathcal F_U$ is $C^\\infty(U)$ tensored with the polynomial algebra in its even jet generators and the exterior algebra in its odd jet generators, where each element uses only finitely many jet generators. The total derivatives $D_\\mu$ act by $D_\\mu f=\\partial_\\mu f$ on $f\\in C^\\infty(U)$ and $D_\\mu\\Phi_I=\\Phi_{I+e_\\mu}$ on every jet generator; the multi-index derivatives commute.\n\nDefine the degree-one BRST operator candidate $s$ on the fields by\n\n$$sA_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c],\\qquad sc=-\\frac12[c,c],\\qquad s\\psi=-\\rho(c)\\psi.$$\n\nOn coefficient functions set $sf=0$, and on every jet generator set $s(\\Phi_I)=D_I(s\\Phi)$; extend $s$ to $\\mathcal F_U$ by the odd left-derivation rule $s(FG)=(sF)G+(-1)^{|F|}F(sG)$ for homogeneous $F$, where $|F|$ is total parity. The minimal sector here contains only $A,\\psi,c$ and their jets. Antighosts and Nakanishi–Lautrup variables are not included; adjoining such a nonminimal pair requires separate definitions and a proof before any cohomological triviality claim. The specified data are a BRST complex once $s^2=0$, which is established for these formulas in the following nilpotence lemma.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json lem-qftx-nonabelian-brst-nilpotence statement= '\nLet $n\\geq 1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and open, let $\\mathfrak g$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra, and let $V=V_{\\bar 0}\\oplus V_{\\bar 1}$ be a finite-dimensional graded complex vector space with an even Lie-algebra representation $\\rho:\\mathfrak g\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$. Form the graded-commutative finite-jet algebra over $C^\\infty(U)$ from an even $\\mathfrak g$-valued local one-form $A=A_\\mu^aT_a\\,dx^\\mu$, a ghost $c=c^aT_a$ with odd components of ghost number one, and matter fields $\\psi^\\alpha$ of fixed component parity and ghost number zero. Let $D_\\mu$ be the total derivatives on coefficient functions and formal field jets, and let $s$ be the left odd derivation of ghost number one specified by $sA_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$, $sc=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, $s\\psi=-\\rho(c)\\psi$, and $s(D_I\\Phi)=D_I(s\\Phi)$ on every multi-index jet. Then $s^2=0$ on the entire finite-jet algebra and $sD_\\mu=D_\\mu s$ for each $\\mu$.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json def-qftx-local-brst-cohomology statement= '\nLet $n\\geq1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and open, let $\\mathcal F_U$ be the finite-jet local algebra with nilpotent BRST derivation $s$ and commuting total derivatives $D_\\mu$, and let $dx^1,\\ldots,dx^n$ be anticommuting coordinate one-forms. For $g\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $0\\leq p\\leq n$, let $\\mathcal A_U^{g,p}$ be the space of local horizontal $p$-forms of ghost number $g$, namely finite sums of $dx^{\\mu_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dx^{\\mu_p}$ with coefficients in the ghost-number-$g$ part of $\\mathcal F_U$. Define the horizontal differential $d_H:=dx^\\mu D_\\mu$; its square is $d_H^2a=dx^\\mu\\wedge dx^\\nu D_\\mu D_\\nu a=\\tfrac12dx^\\mu\\wedge dx^\\nu[D_\\mu,D_\\nu]a=0$. Extend $s$ to horizontal forms by $s(dx^\\mu\\wedge a)=-dx^\\mu\\wedge sa$. Since $sD_\\mu=D_\\mu s$, one has $s(d_Ha)=-dx^\\mu\\wedge D_\\mu(sa)=-d_H(sa)$, hence $sd_H+d_Hs=0$.\n\nFor $p=0$, set $\\mathcal A_U^{g,-1}=\\{0\\}$. The local BRST cohomology modulo total derivatives in bidegree $(g,p)$ is\n\n$$H^{g,p}(s\\mid d_H):=\\frac{\\{a\\in\\mathcal A_U^{g,p}:\\text{there is }b\\in\\mathcal A_U^{g+1,p-1}\\text{ with }sa+d_Hb=0\\}}{\\{sm+d_Hn:m\\in\\mathcal A_U^{g-1,p},\\ n\\in\\mathcal A_U^{g,p-1}\\}}.$$\n\nThe denominator is a subspace of the numerator: for $a=sm+d_Hn$, choose $b=sn$ and compute $sa+d_Hb=s^2m+sd_Hn+d_Hsn=0$. For $p=0$, $n=b=0$ and the same verification reduces to $s^2m=0$. This is cohomology of the specified local jet algebra and horizontal differential; it is not cohomology of a physical state space or Hilbert space. Interpreting $d_H$-exact top forms as equal local functionals additionally assumes compact support or boundary conditions that make the corresponding surface term vanish.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json def-qftx-bv-antibracket-master-action statement= '\nUse the local field data and BRST convention on an open set $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ given by a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra $\\mathfrak g$, a graded representation space $V$, an even $\\mathfrak g$-valued gauge potential $A_\\mu$, an odd ghost $c$ of ghost number $1$, and matter components $\\psi^\\alpha$ of parity $\\epsilon_\\alpha$ and ghost number $0$. For every field or ghost coordinate $\\Phi^A$ of parity $\\epsilon_A$ and ghost number $g_A$, introduce an antifield $\\Phi_A^*$ of parity $\\epsilon_A+1\\pmod 2$ and ghost number $-1-g_A$. Thus $A^*$ and the antifields of even matter are odd of ghost number $-1$, while $c^*$ is even of ghost number $-2$. The minimal sector consists of $A,\\psi,c$ and these antifields.\n\nFor a finite-dimensional canonical coordinate algebra, take the polynomial algebra in the even coordinates tensor the exterior algebra in the odd coordinates. In the local-field notation, use finite-jet local functionals with variational derivatives only when the chosen domain makes those derivatives and integrations by parts well-defined; compact support or boundary conditions that kill surface terms are part of that domain. For homogeneous functionals $F,G$, define the canonical antibracket by\n\n$$\n(F,G):=\\sum_A\\left(\\frac{\\partial_r F}{\\partial\\Phi^A}\\frac{\\partial_lG}{\\partial\\Phi_A^*}-\\frac{\\partial_rF}{\\partial\\Phi_A^*}\\frac{\\partial_lG}{\\partial\\Phi^A}\\right),\n$$\n\nwith an integral over $U$ replacing the sum in a local-field expression. Left and right derivatives are the graded derivatives acting from the indicated side. This convention has odd parity, ghost number $+1$, the shifted graded-skew identity, the shifted Jacobi identity, and the graded Leibniz identity. A regular finite-dimensional canonical domain has no nonzero central element of ghost number $1$: an element central against every coordinate has all coordinate derivatives zero and is a numerical constant. A formal local-functional domain used below must separately satisfy this same no-ghost-number-one-center condition.\n\nLet a local gauge parameter be a smooth map $\\lambda:U\\to\\mathfrak g$, and define its infinitesimal action on the fields by $\\delta_\\lambda A_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu\\lambda+[A_\\mu,\\lambda]$ and $\\delta_\\lambda\\psi=-\\rho(\\lambda)\\psi$. Write these variations as $\\delta_\\lambda\\Phi^i=R^i_\\alpha[\\Phi]\\lambda^\\alpha$, where $R$ is the resulting linear differential operator in $\\lambda$. Assume this declared gauge action is irreducible: if $\\delta_\\lambda\\Phi^i\\equiv0$ for every field configuration, then $\\lambda\\equiv0$. Assume also that its Lie-algebra action closes off shell, meaning its commutator closes without using equations of motion, and that no additional gauge symmetry is included. Let $S_0[A,\\psi]$ be a specified local classical action invariant under these transformations. Write $s_0$ for the BRST operator on the minimal fields given by $s_0A_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu c+[A_\\mu,c]$, $s_0c=-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, and $s_0\\psi=-\\rho(c)\\psi$. The minimal BV functional candidate is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{BV}}:=S_0+\\int_U d^nx\\,\\sum_A(-1)^{\\epsilon_A}\\Phi_A^*\\,s_0\\Phi^A,$$\n\nwhere each product is in the displayed order and the same left/right derivative convention is used in the antibracket. It is a **classical master action** exactly when it satisfies the classical master equation $(S_{\\mathrm{BV}},S_{\\mathrm{BV}})=0$; the displayed formula alone does not assert that a particular $S_0$ satisfies that equation.\n\nThe optional nonminimal sector adds an odd antighost $\\bar c^a$ of ghost number $-1$, an even auxiliary field $b^a$ of ghost number $0$, and their antifields, with the BV functional term $-\\bar c_a^*b^a$ giving $s\\bar c=b$ and $sb=0$. This definition makes no cohomological contractibility claim for that pair. The formal local-field notation does not define an infinite-dimensional measure, a BV Laplacian $\\Delta$, a path integral, or the quantum master equation.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json lem-qftx-bv-canonical-action-generates-brst-differential statement= '\nLet $\\mathcal B$ be the finite-dimensional algebra that is polynomial in even canonical coordinates and exterior in odd canonical coordinates $\\Phi^A,\\Phi_A^*$. Give each homogeneous minimal coordinate $\\Phi^A$ parity $\\epsilon_A\\in\\{0,1\\}$ and ghost number $g_A$, and give its antifield parity $\\epsilon_A+1\\pmod 2$ and ghost number $-1-g_A$. Use the canonical antibracket\n\n$$ (F,G)=\\sum_A\\left(\\frac{\\partial_rF}{\\partial\\Phi^A}\\frac{\\partial_lG}{\\partial\\Phi_A^*}-\\frac{\\partial_rF}{\\partial\\Phi_A^*}\\frac{\\partial_lG}{\\partial\\Phi^A}\\right). $$\n\nSuppose each $s_0\\Phi^A$ is independent of all antifields, has parity $\\epsilon_A+1\\pmod 2$ and ghost number $g_A+1$, and let $S_0$ be even of ghost number zero and independent of antifields. Define $S:=S_0+\\sum_A(-1)^{\\epsilon_A}\\Phi_A^*s_0\\Phi^A$, with the displayed product order. Then $(S,\\Phi^A)=s_0\\Phi^A$ for each minimal coordinate. For a local-field version on a nonempty open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, use fields $\\Phi^A(x)$ and antifields $\\Phi_A^*(x)$ with the same parity and ghost-number assignments, replace coordinate derivatives in the bracket by variational derivatives and the sum by $\\int_U d^nx$, and set $S_U:=S_{0,U}+\\int_U d^nx\\,\\sum_A(-1)^{\\epsilon_A}\\Phi_A^*(x)s_0\\Phi^A(x)$. If this domain makes the variational derivatives and integrations by parts well-defined and its boundary term vanishes, then $(S_U,\\Phi^A(x))=s_0\\Phi^A(x)$ pointwise.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json lem-qftx-bv-master-equation-brst-nilpotence statement= '\nLet $\\mathcal B$ be a graded-commutative canonical BV algebra with an odd antibracket $(\\ ,\\ )$ of ghost number $+1$ satisfying shifted graded skew-symmetry and the shifted Jacobi identity. Assume the center of $\\mathcal B$ contains no nonzero element of ghost number one. The finite-dimensional canonical coordinate algebra in the preceding definition has this property because a central element has zero derivative with respect to every field and antifield coordinate and is therefore a numerical constant; for a formal local-functional domain the no-ghost-number-one-center condition is an explicit hypothesis. For any even $S_{\\mathrm{BV}}\\in\\mathcal B$ of ghost number zero, define $sF:=(S_{\\mathrm{BV}},F)$. Then $s^2=0$ on $\\mathcal B$ if and only if the classical master equation $(S_{\\mathrm{BV}},S_{\\mathrm{BV}})=0$ holds.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-brst-nilpotence statement= '\nLet $\\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ with basis $T_a=-\\tfrac{i}{2}\\sigma_a$, where\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen $[T_a,T_b]=\\epsilon_{ab}{}^cT_c$. Use the defining representation on $V=\\mathbb C^2$, so $\\rho(T_a)=T_a$, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty open set. On the finite-jet algebra with even gauge potential $A_\\mu=A_\\mu^aT_a$, odd ghosts $c^a$, and a homogeneous matter field $\\psi:U\\to\\mathbb C^2$, define\n\n$$sA_\\mu^a=\\partial_\\mu c^a+\\epsilon^a{}_{bc}A_\\mu^bc^c,\\qquad sc^a=-\\frac12\\epsilon^a{}_{bc}c^bc^c,\\qquad s\\psi=-c^aT_a\\psi.$$\n\nThe operator $s$ squares to zero on these generators and their formal spacetime jets.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-4.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-bv-ghost-master-equation statement= '\nLet $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ have basis $T_a$ with $[T_b,T_c]=\\epsilon^a{}_{bc}T_a$. Form the finite graded-commutative coordinate algebra $\\mathcal B=\\mathbb C[c_1^*,c_2^*,c_3^*]\\otimes\\Lambda(c^1,c^2,c^3)$, where each $c^a$ is odd of ghost number $1$ and each $c_a^*$ is even of ghost number $-2$. Use the canonical antibracket\n\n$$\n(F,G)=\\sum_{a=1}^3\\left(\\frac{\\partial_rF}{\\partial c^a}\\frac{\\partial_lG}{\\partial c_a^*}-\\frac{\\partial_rF}{\\partial c_a^*}\\frac{\\partial_lG}{\\partial c^a}\\right).\n$$\n\nFor $S_{\\mathrm{CE}}=\\tfrac12c_a^*\\epsilon^a{}_{bc}c^bc^c$, the element $S_{\\mathrm{CE}}$ is even with ghost number zero and satisfies $(S_{\\mathrm{CE}},S_{\\mathrm{CE}})=0$. Hence the Hamiltonian differential $sF=(S_{\\mathrm{CE}},F)$ is nilpotent. This is only the finite-dimensional non-Abelian ghost sector; it is not a Yang–Mills action or a quantum field theory.' design_state= None
