research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-47.pages.json thm-qftx-complex-associated-bundle-realification statement= 'Let $H$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie group, let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth\nright principal $H$-bundle, let $W$ be a finite-dimensional complex vector\nspace, and let $\\rho:H\\to\\mathrm{GL}_{\\mathbb C}(W)$ be a smooth complex\nrepresentation. Regard $W$ as the underlying real vector space $W_{\\mathbb R}$\nand $\\rho$ as the real representation $\\rho_{\\mathbb R}$. The associated\nquotient $P\\times_{H,\\rho_{\\mathbb R}}W_{\\mathbb R}$ has the smooth real\nvector-bundle structure supplied by the associated-vector-bundle theorem. The\nmap $J[p,w]=[p,iw]$ is well-defined and smooth and satisfies $J^2=-\\mathrm{id}$.\nIt therefore gives this real bundle a complex vector-bundle structure of rank\n$\\dim_{\\mathbb C}W$, with transition maps $\\rho(g_{ji})$ in any local principal\ntrivializations $s_i=s_jg_{ji}$. This assertion specifies the complex structure\ninduced by $J$ and makes no uniqueness claim about other possible complex\nstructures on the same real bundle.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-47.pages.json def-qftx-susy-background-data statement= 'Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. Let $M$ be a nonempty Hausdorff second-countable smooth four-manifold without boundary, let $g$ be a smooth positive-definite metric on $TM$, and choose an orientation $o$. Coordinates $x^\\mu$ have units of length, the metric components in length coordinates are dimensionless, and the scalar curvature has units of inverse length squared. A concrete background may instead have a boundary, but then its boundary conditions and the vanishing or treatment of every boundary term are additional data.\n\nWrite $SO(4)=\\{R\\in M_4(\\mathbb R):R^{\\mathsf T}R=I,\\ \\det R=1\\}$. For $x\\in M$, an oriented $g$-orthonormal frame is an orientation-preserving linear isometry $e:\\mathbb R^4\\to T_xM$. The oriented orthonormal frame bundle $F_g^+(M)$ is the set of these frames, with projection to $M$ and right action $e\\cdot R=e\\circ R$. Its smooth principal $SO(4)$-bundle structure is part of the supplied geometry. A Euclidean spin structure is a smooth right principal $\\operatorname{Spin}(4)$-bundle $\\widetilde F\\to M$ and a smooth equivariant map $\\lambda:\\widetilde F\\to F_g^+(M)$ whose restriction to every fibre is a two-sheeted cover. Use $\\operatorname{Spin}(4)=SU(2)_+\\times SU(2)_-$, where $SU(2)=\\left\\{\\begin{psmallmatrix}z&w\\\\-\\bar w&\\bar z\\end{psmallmatrix}:z,w\\in\\mathbb C,\\ |z|^2+|w|^2=1\\right\\}$ and the two factors act by their defining matrix representations on $\\mathbb C^2$. Identify each factor with the unit quaternions by $\\begin{psmallmatrix}z&w\\\\-\\bar w&\\bar z\\end{psmallmatrix}\\leftrightarrow z+w\\mathbf j$, with $\\mathbf j^2=-1$ and $\\mathbf j z=\\bar z\\mathbf j$; write $\\mathbb H=\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,i,\\mathbf j,i\\mathbf j\\}\\cong\\mathbb R^4$. The fixed standard double-covering homomorphism $\\rho:\\operatorname{Spin}(4)\\to SO(4)$ is $\\rho(u_+,u_-)q=u_+qu_-^{-1}$; it is surjective with kernel $\\{(1,1),(-1,-1)\\}$. The chiral spinor bundles are the associated bundles $S_\\pm=\\widetilde F\\times_{\\pi_\\pm}\\mathbb C^2$, where $\\pi_\\pm$ are the defining representations of the two $SU(2)$ factors and the quotient convention is $(ps,z)\\sim(p,\\pi_\\pm(s)z)$. Equip each real associated bundle with the complex structure $[p,z]\\mapsto[p,iz]$, so $S_\\pm$ has complex rank two. A spin connection is a principal connection on $\\widetilde F$ projecting to the Levi-Civita connection of $g$; it induces covariant derivatives on $S_\\pm$. A spin structure and its connection are supplied data, not conclusions that follow from the metric.\n\nAn R-symmetry background consists of a specified finite-dimensional Lie group $K_R$, a smooth right principal $K_R$-bundle $P_R\\to M$, a principal connection $A_R$, and a declared smooth representation of $K_R$ on each field bundle. Gauge data consist of a specified compact Lie group $G$, a smooth right principal $G$-bundle $P_G\\to M$, its gauge group of smooth bundle automorphisms over $\\operatorname{id}_M$, and a principal connection $A$. Matter and auxiliary fields are smooth sections of explicitly declared vector bundles associated to $\\widetilde F$, $P_R$, and $P_G$; bosonic and fermionic fields are assigned even and odd parity, respectively. The curvature, global form of $G$, representations, bundle sectors, and reality contours are part of the model data.\n\nA rigid supersymmetric background datum is a fixed Riemannian metric, spin structure and spin connection, R-symmetry background, supergravity auxiliary background fields, and a nonzero smooth spinor section $\\epsilon$ in a declared spinor/R-symmetry representation. It also supplies the exact first-order linear system $\\mathscr K(\\nabla^S,A_R,\\mathcal B)(\\epsilon)=0$ imposed on $\\epsilon$, where $\\mathcal B$ denotes the named auxiliary background fields and $\\mathscr K$ is the operator obtained by setting the chosen supergravity fermion variation to zero. The equation, its signature, gamma-matrix convention, spinor reality conditions, and boundary conditions must be specified separately for each model; no universal Killing-spinor equation or existence statement is built into this definition. “Rigid” means the metric and supergravity fields are fixed external data and are not integrated over.\n\nAn off-shell multiplet is a specified $\\mathbb Z_2$-graded collection of bosonic, fermionic, and auxiliary fields together with an odd transformation $Q$ on all of them. An isometry is a diffeomorphism preserving $g$ and $o$; its action on associated bundles must also be supplied. A flavor transformation is the action of a separately named global internal group on the declared field representations. The multiplet closes off shell when $Q^2$ equals an explicitly declared combination of lifted spacetime isometries, gauge transformations, R-symmetry transformations, and flavor transformations on every field, without using Euler–Lagrange equations. A localization package additionally specifies its field configuration space $\\mathcal F$, action $S$, gauge group $\\mathcal G$, a gauge-fixing slice (a locally transverse condition on gauge orbits), the associated Faddeev–Popov operator and determinant, and a ghost complex (graded ghost, antighost, and multiplier fields with an odd BRST differential). A Faddeev–Popov zero mode is a vector in the kernel of the specified gauge-fixing operator; stabilizer modes are infinitesimal gauge transformations that fix the chosen connection. The package also specifies its integration contour $\\mathcal C$, boundary conditions, and either an actual measure or a regulator map from field configurations to regulated spaces $\\mathcal F_\\Lambda$ with measures $\\mu_\\Lambda$. If a measure is only written formally as $[D\\Phi]$, this notation is not a constructed measure. Applying a $Q$-exact deformation $S_t=S+tQV$ further requires an explicit assumption or proof of $Q$-invariance of the regulated measure and contour, absence or treatment of anomalies, justified differentiation under the integral, and vanishing or controlled boundary terms. This definition supplies no such proof.\n\nFor a four-dimensional gauge model in these units, $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}$ is dimensionless, $[A_\\mu]=[\\Phi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $[F_{\\mu\\nu}]=\\mathrm{length}^{-2}$, and a fermion field has mass dimension $3/2$; the supercharge has mass dimension $1/2$ and its spinor parameter has mass dimension $-1/2$. Every additional mass, radius, equivariant parameter, and suppressed constant must be assigned dimensions in the concrete item. No metric is asserted to admit this data, and finite-dimensional equivariant identities alone do not construct a continuum field-space measure.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-47.pages.json post-qftx-rigid-supersymmetric-background statement= "Adopt the following Euclidean source model in the conventions stated here. Let $r>0$ have units of length and let\n$$S^4_r=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^5:\\sum_{j=1}^5 y_j^2=r^2\\}$$\nhave its round metric, standard orientation, and scalar curvature $12/r^2$. In stereographic coordinates $x^\\mu$ of length, $\\mu=1,\\dots,4$, the metric is\n$$ds^2=\\frac{\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(dx^\\mu)^2}{(1+x^2/(4r^2))^2},\\qquad x^2=\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(x^\\mu)^2.$$\nUse the standard spin structure, a trivial $SU(2)_R$ background bundle with zero connection, and the $SU(2)_R$ generator selected below. The global gauge group is $G=SU(2)$, not $SO(3)$; its Lie algebra consists of anti-Hermitian $2\\times2$ traceless matrices and has positive invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, normalized by $\\kappa(-i\\sigma_a/2,-i\\sigma_b/2)=\\delta_{ab}$. Let $\\Sigma$ be the declared set of isomorphism classes of smooth right principal $SU(2)$-bundles over $S^4_r$; each $P\\in\\Sigma$ is a separate gauge sector. The sector set and any sum over it are formal model inputs, not a classification or measure construction.\n\nThe matter content is the massless four-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ vector multiplet: one gauge connection $A_\\mu$, six real adjoint scalar fields $\\Phi_A$ with $A\\in\\{0,5,6,7,8,9\\}$, and four adjoint Weyl fermions. Equivalently, its flat-space component content is one $\\mathcal N=1$ vector multiplet and three adjoint $\\mathcal N=1$ chiral multiplets. Use the ordinary trace $\\operatorname{Tr}_2$ in the defining representation of $SU(2)$ and a positive dimensionless coupling $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}$; this fixes a concrete trace normalization, whose match to the planned N=4 supplier remains conditional. For the source's dimensional-reduction notation set $D_\\mu=\\nabla_\\mu+[A_\\mu,\\cdot]$, $D_A X=[\\Phi_A,X]$, $F_{MN}=[D_M,D_N]$, and $\\eta_{MN}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,\\dots,1)$ for $M,N=0,\\dots,9$. With the seven localization auxiliaries included, the adopted classical action input is\n$$S_{\\mathrm{off}}=-\\frac{1}{g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}\\int_{S^4_r}d^4x\\,\\sqrt g\\,\\operatorname{Tr}_2\\left(\\frac12\\sum_{M,N}F_{MN}F^{MN}-\\Psi\\Gamma^M D_M\\Psi+\\frac{2}{r^2}\\sum_A\\Phi_A\\Phi^A-\\sum_{i=1}^7K_iK_i\\right).$$\nHere $\\Psi$ is the adjoint ten-dimensional Majorana–Weyl fermion used to package the four-dimensional fields; after Euclidean continuation it is complex and has no independent conjugate field. The chiral gamma matrices obey $\\Gamma_M\\widetilde\\Gamma_N+\\Gamma_N\\widetilde\\Gamma_M=2\\eta_{MN}I_{16}$, with $\\widetilde\\Gamma_0=-\\Gamma_0$ and $\\widetilde\\Gamma_M=\\Gamma_M$ for $M=1,\\dots,9$; $\\Gamma_{MN}:=\\widetilde\\Gamma_{[M}\\Gamma_{N]}$. The four sphere directions have positive metric. The term with $2/r^2$ is the conformal curvature coupling of the six scalars. The Yang–Mills coupling is dimensionless, $[A_\\mu]=[\\Phi_A]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $[K_i]=[F_{MN}]=\\mathrm{length}^{-2}$, and $[\\Psi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-3/2}$.\n\nLet $\\widehat\\epsilon_s=(0_8,1,0_7)^{\\mathsf T}$ be the normalized sixteen-component chiral spinor in the source gamma convention, with $\\Gamma_{5678}\\widehat\\epsilon_s=\\widehat\\epsilon_s$ and $\\Gamma_{1234}\\widehat\\epsilon_s=-\\widehat\\epsilon_s$, and set $\\widehat\\epsilon_c=(2r)^{-1}\\Gamma_{012}\\widehat\\epsilon_s$. With $\\Omega(x)=1+x^2/(4r^2)$, adopt\n$$\\epsilon(x)=\\Omega(x)^{-1/2}\\bigl(\\widehat\\epsilon_s+x^i\\widetilde\\Gamma_i\\widehat\\epsilon_c\\bigr),\\qquad\n\\widetilde\\epsilon(x)=\\Omega(x)^{-1/2}\\left(\\widehat\\epsilon_c-\\frac{x^i}{4r^2}\\Gamma_i\\widehat\\epsilon_s\\right),\\quad i=1,\\dots,4.$$\nThey solve the conformal Killing-spinor system\n$$\\nabla_\\mu\\epsilon=\\widetilde\\Gamma_\\mu\\widetilde\\epsilon,\\qquad\n\\nabla_\\mu\\widetilde\\epsilon=-\\frac{1}{4r^2}\\Gamma_\\mu\\epsilon.$$\nThe selected spinors obey the source's same-chirality conditions, in particular $\\widetilde\\epsilon\\epsilon=0$, so the dilation term in the supercharge square vanishes. Their bilinear $v^\\mu=\\epsilon\\Gamma^\\mu\\epsilon$ generates the $U(1)$ isometry rotating the $(x^1,x^2)$ and $(x^3,x^4)$ planes equally. The corresponding internal R-symmetry generator is $R_{AB}=2\\widetilde\\epsilon\\Gamma_{AB}\\epsilon$ on scalar fields and its induced spin representation on fermions.\n\nFor this single supercharge, introduce seven adjoint auxiliary scalars $K_i$, $i=1,\\dots,7$, and seven spinors $\\nu_i$ satisfying\n$$\\epsilon\\Gamma_M\\nu_i=0,\\qquad\n\\frac12(\\epsilon\\Gamma^N\\epsilon)\\widetilde\\Gamma_{N,\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{i=1}^7\\nu_{i,\\alpha}\\nu_{i,\\beta}+\\epsilon_\\alpha\\epsilon_\\beta,\\qquad\n\\nu_i\\Gamma_M\\nu_j=\\delta_{ij}\\epsilon\\Gamma_M\\epsilon.$$\nAdopt the off-shell transformations\n$$Q_\\epsilon A_M=\\Psi\\Gamma_M\\epsilon,\\qquad\nQ_\\epsilon\\Psi=\\frac12\\Gamma^{MN}F_{MN}\\epsilon+\\frac12\\Gamma^\\mu\\Gamma^A\\Phi_A\\nabla_\\mu\\epsilon+\\sum_iK_i\\nu_i,\\qquad\nQ_\\epsilon K_i=-\\nu_i\\Gamma^M D_M\\Psi.$$\nWith $v^M=\\epsilon\\Gamma^M\\epsilon$, their square is the source's off-shell symmetry\n$$Q_\\epsilon^2=-\\mathcal L_v-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(v^M A_M)-\\delta_R-\\delta_\\Omega,$$\nwhere $\\mathcal L_v$ is the lifted Lie derivative along the spacetime part $v^\\mu$, $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)$ acts on an adjoint field $X$ by $[u,X]$ and on a connection by $-D_\\mu u$, $\\delta_R$ is the internal transformation generated by $R_{AB}$, and $\\delta_\\Omega$ is the dilation with parameter $2\\widetilde\\epsilon\\epsilon=0$. This off-shell closure is for the selected generator; it is not a claim that the full hypermultiplet algebra closes with finitely many auxiliary fields.\n\nIn a trivialization chart use the Faddeev–Popov gauge slice $d^*A=0$, where $d^*$ is the metric adjoint of the exterior derivative. In a nontrivial bundle sector $P$, choose a background connection $A_*$ and fluctuations $a=A-A_*$ in $\\Omega^1(S^4,\\operatorname{ad}P)$; the supplied local slice is $d_{A_*}^*a=0$ and its Faddeev–Popov operator is $d_{A_*}^*D_A$ with stabilizer modes omitted. Introduce odd ghost and antighost fields $c,\\widetilde c$, an even multiplier $b$, and the constant zero-mode fields $a_0,\\widetilde a_0,b_0,c_0,\\widetilde c_0$. In the trivialization chart the Faddeev–Popov operator is $d^*D_{A_{\\mathrm{gauge}}}$ with its zero modes omitted; its determinant is denoted $\\det'(d^*D_{A_{\\mathrm{gauge}}})$. Take dimensionless gauge-fixing parameters $\\xi_1,\\xi_2>0$ and the BRST gauge-fixing functional\n$$\\delta\\left((\\widetilde c,\\,i d^*A+\\tfrac{\\xi_1}{2}b+i b_0)-(c,\\,\\widetilde a_0-\\tfrac{\\xi_2}{2}a_0)\\right).$$\nThe brackets are the integrated invariant pairing on adjoint-valued fields, and the prime removes the stabilizer/constant zero modes treated by the displayed auxiliary fields. Let $S^\\delta_{\\mathrm{gf}}$ denote the BRST variation of this functional. If $\\psi$ is the fermionic one-form superpartner of $A$ and $\\rho_{\\mathrm{gh}}(\\omega)$ is the infinitesimal action of the selected circle generator on ghosts, the source's $Q$-closed completion is\n$$S^Q_{\\mathrm{gf}}=S^\\delta_{\\mathrm{gf}}-(\\widetilde c,d^*\\psi+\\tfrac{\\xi_1}{2}\\rho_{\\mathrm{gh}}(\\omega)\\widetilde c)-(\\phi,\\widetilde a_0-\\tfrac{\\xi_2}{2}a_0).$$\nThe gauge-fixed functional integral is the formal expression\n$$Z_{\\mathrm{formal}}=\\sum_{[P]\\in\\Sigma}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal G(P),g_{\\mathrm{YM}})}\n\\int_{\\mathcal C_P}[D\\Phi\\,Dc\\,D\\widetilde c\\,Db\\,D(\\text{zero modes})]\\,\ne^{-S_{\\mathrm{off}}[P]-S_{\\mathrm{gf}}^Q[P]}.$$\nHere $\\mathcal G(P)$ is the smooth gauge-automorphism group of $P$, $\\mathcal C_P$ is the declared contour in that sector, and $\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal G(P),g_{\\mathrm{YM}})$ is the source's formal gauge-group volume with its invariant measure scaled by the gauge coupling; it is not an actual infinite-dimensional Haar measure. In the Euclidean contour, gauge fields and $\\Phi_{5},\\dots,\\Phi_9$ lie in the real Lie algebra, $\\Phi_0=i\\Phi_0^E$ with $\\Phi_0^E$ real, the seven localization auxiliaries satisfy $K_i=iK_i^E$ with $K_i^E$ real, and $a_0=i a_0^E$ with $a_0^E$ real.\n\nLet $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)$ denote the infinitesimal gauge action with parameter $u$, and let $\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$ denote the lifted infinitesimal circle action. The supersymmetry differential acts by $sX=X'$, $sX'=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X$, $sc=\\phi$, $s\\phi=0$, $sa_0=0$, $s\\widetilde c=s\\widetilde a_0=sb_0=s\\widetilde c_0=sc_0=0$, and $sb=\\rho_{\\mathrm{gh}}(\\omega)\\widetilde c$. The Faddeev–Popov differential acts on an adjoint-valued tensor field $X$ by $\\delta X=-[c,X]$ and on its odd superpartner by $\\delta X'=-[c,X']$. On the gauge connection it acts by $\\delta A_\\mu=D_\\mu c=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(c)A_\\mu$, using the earlier convention $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)A_\\mu=-D_\\mu u$; the connection's one-form superpartner $\\psi$ transforms as $\\delta\\psi=-[c,\\psi]$. The remaining rules are $\\delta c=-a_0-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, $\\delta a_0=0$, $\\delta\\phi=-[c,\\phi]-\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)\\phi$, $\\delta\\widetilde c=b$, $\\delta b=[a_0,\\widetilde c]$, $\\delta\\widetilde a_0=\\widetilde c_0$, $\\delta\\widetilde c_0=[a_0,\\widetilde a_0]$, $\\delta b_0=c_0$, and $\\delta c_0=[a_0,b_0]$. Here $X$ ranges over adjoint-valued physical and auxiliary tensor fields other than the connection, $X'$ is its odd superpartner, $c,\\widetilde c,c_0,\\widetilde c_0$ are odd, and $a_0,\\widetilde a_0,b,b_0,\\phi$ are even. The total localization differential is $Q=s+\\delta$, with $s$ the supersymmetry differential and $\\delta$ the Faddeev–Popov differential. These rules give, on adjoint-valued tensor fields and their superpartners,\n$$s^2X'=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X'+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X',\\qquad \\delta^2X=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)X,\\qquad \\{s,\\delta\\}X=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X,$$\nand the same graded algebra on the connection and its one-form superpartner when the gauge action on the connection is interpreted as $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)A_\\mu=-D_\\mu u$. On ghosts, $\\{s,\\delta\\}=\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$ and $s^2=0$; the displayed ghost transformations give $\\delta^2=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)$. Thus $Q^2=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$ on fields and ghosts: a constant gauge transformation plus the $U(1)$ spacetime rotation. These are the finite-dimensional geometric symmetry data of the background; this assertion does not identify the infinite-dimensional field space with a smooth finite-dimensional manifold.\n\nAdopt as a formal regulator the supersymmetry-compatible noncommutative deformation used by the source for gauge-field regularization on $\\mathbb R^4$. Its $\\Omega$-background is the $U(1)^2$ action rotating the two coordinate planes in $\\mathbb R^4\\cong\\mathbb C^2$, with angular parameters $\\varepsilon_1=\\varepsilon_2=r^{-1}$, each of mass dimension one. For the four-sphere, assume an extension exists and agrees near the north and south poles with the stated $\\mathbb R^4$ regulator. This extension is not explicitly constructed in the source and is an additional unresolved model assumption. The regulated measure, contour, and gauge-fixed action are likewise formal inputs; any use of $S_t=S+tQV$ is conditional on measure and contour invariance, no anomalous Jacobian, justified differentiation under the integral, and no uncontrolled boundary contribution. This postulate is a source-qualified modeling choice, not a proof of a continuum path integral or a result about nature." design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-47.pages.json ex-qftx-susy-flat-torus-geometric-background statement= 'Let $\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_4>0$ be lengths and let $T^4_\\ell=\\mathbb R^4/(\\ell_1\\mathbb Z\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus\\ell_4\\mathbb Z)$ have its quotient flat metric and standard orientation. Use $F_g^+=T^4_\\ell\\times SO(4)$ and $\\widetilde F=T^4_\\ell\\times\\operatorname{Spin}(4)$ with covering map $(x,s)\\mapsto(x,\\rho(s))$, where $\\operatorname{Spin}(4)=SU(2)_+\\times SU(2)_-$ and $\\rho(u_+,u_-)q=u_+qu_-^{-1}$ on $\\mathbb H\\cong\\mathbb R^4$. Equip this trivial spin lift with its periodic spin structure and zero spin connection. Use the trivial $SU(2)_R$ bundle with zero connection and zero supergravity auxiliary fields. For the geometric Killing-spinor equation $\\nabla^S_\\mu\\epsilon=0$, every constant nonzero section of $S_+$ is a solution. These data supply only the geometric spin/R and Killing-spinor input; they do not specify an off-shell multiplet or $Q$, gauge fixing, an integration contour, a measure, or a regulator, and therefore are not a complete localization package.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-47.pages.json ex-qftx-susy-round-s4-localization-data statement= "Take the adopted massless $\\mathcal N=4$ model on the round $S^4_r$ with its specified conformal Killing spinors, seven auxiliary scalars, and combined gauge-fixed differential $Q=s+\\delta$. For every adjoint-valued tensor field $X$ and its odd superpartner $X'$, the source algebra gives $sX=X'$, $sX'=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X$, $\\delta X=-[c,X]$, and $\\delta X'=-[c,X']$, with $\\delta^2X=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)X$ and $\\{s,\\delta\\}X=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X$. For the connection, $sA=\\psi$, its superpartner transforms by $s\\psi=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)A+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)A$, and the Faddeev–Popov rule is $\\delta A_\\mu=D_\\mu c=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(c)A_\\mu$; the same square and mixed-square identities hold with the gauge action $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)A_\\mu=-D_\\mu u$. On the ghost fields, $s^2=0$, $\\delta^2=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)$, and $\\{s,\\delta\\}=\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$. Thus the combined square is $Q^2=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$: a constant gauge transformation and the selected $U(1)$ isometry. The $U(1)$ factor acts smoothly on the finite-dimensional base $S^4_r$; its ordinary Cartan complex is the invariant polynomial-valued differential-form complex for that circle action. This finite Cartan complex concerns forms on $S^4_r$, not the infinite-dimensional field configuration space. The measure and noncommutative regulator extension remain formal assumptions of the adopted model." design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-5.pages.json lem-qftx-hodge-star-decomposition-four-dimensions statement= 'Let $V$ be a four-dimensional real vector space with an orientation and inner product $g$, and let $W$ be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space. Define the Hodge operator $*_g$ on $\\Lambda^2V^*$ by $\\alpha\\wedge *_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g$, and extend it $W$-linearly to $\\Lambda^2V^*\\otimes W$. Give this tensor product the inner product induced by those on $\\Lambda^2V^*$ and $W$. Then $*_g^2=I$, $*_g$ is an isometry, and its $+1$ and $-1$ eigenspaces are orthogonal. Every $\\Phi\\in\\Lambda^2V^*\\otimes W$ has the unique decomposition\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\Phi^++\\Phi^-,\\qquad \\Phi^\\pm:=\\frac{\\Phi\\pm *_g\\Phi}{2},\\qquad *_g\\Phi^\\pm=\\pm\\Phi^\\pm.\n$$\n\nIf the inner product on $V$ is rescaled to $\\widehat g=\\lambda^2g$ for any $\\lambda>0$, then the Hodge operator on two-forms is unchanged: $*_{\\widehat g}=*_g$.' design_state= 'Step 1 ready; current prerequisites, placement, proof contract, and scoped checks recorded.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-5.pages.json lem-qftx-two-chart-principal-bundle-gluing statement= 'Let $X$ be a Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold covered by open sets $U$ and $V$. Let $G$ be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable real Lie group, and let $\\tau:U\\cap V\\to G$ be smooth. On the disjoint union $Q=(U\\times G)_U\\sqcup(V\\times G)_V$, impose the equivalence relation generated, for $x\\in U\\cap V$ and $h\\in G$, by\n\n$$ (x,h)_V\\sim (x,\\tau(x)h)_U. $$\n\nLet $P:=Q/{\\sim}$ with the quotient topology, write $[x,h]_U$ and $[x,h]_V$ for its classes, and define $\\pi:P\\to X$ by $\\pi([x,h]_U)=\\pi([x,h]_V)=x$. Define the right action by $[x,h]_i\\cdot k=[x,hk]_i$ for $k\\in G$ and $i\\in\\{U,V\\}$. Then $P$ is a smooth right principal $G$-bundle over $X$. Its local sections $s_U(x)=[x,e]_U$ and $s_V(x)=[x,e]_V$ satisfy $s_V(x)=s_U(x)\\tau(x)$ on $U\\cap V$.' design_state= 'Step 1 ready; current prerequisites, placement, proof contract, and scoped checks recorded.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-5.pages.json def-qftx-instanton-number-and-asd-sector statement= 'Assume full Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be a nonempty, connected, compact, oriented, finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth four-manifold without boundary, with a supplied Riemannian metric. Let $G\\subseteq U(V,h)$ be a compact non-Abelian matrix Lie group in its specified global form, let $\\pi:P\\to X$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle in a specified bundle sector (an isomorphism class of smooth principal bundles under isomorphisms over $X$), and let $\\omega$ be a supplied smooth principal connection. Let $B:\\mathfrak g\\times\\mathfrak g\\to\\mathbb R$ be a positive-definite symmetric bilinear form invariant under $\\operatorname{Ad}(G)$. For local sections $s_i:U_i\\to P$, set $A_i=s_i^*\\omega$ and $F_i=dA_i+\\tfrac12[A_i\\wedge A_i]$. If $F_i=\\sum_a e_a F_i^a$ in a finite basis of $\\mathfrak g$, define $B(F_i\\wedge F_i):=\\sum_{a,b}B(e_a,e_b)F_i^a\\wedge F_i^b$; this expression is independent of the basis. Invariance of $B$ makes the local expressions $\\beta_B|_{U_i}:=(8\\pi^2)^{-1}B(F_i\\wedge F_i)$ restrictions of a global four-form $\\beta_B$. The **trace-normalized topological charge** is\n\n$$\nq_B(P,\\omega):=\\int_X\\beta_B.\n$$\n\nThe number $q_B(P,\\omega)$ is independent of the supplied connection. For a general compact matrix group and invariant $B$, it is a real characteristic number; no integrality is asserted. In the special case $G=SU(2)$ in its defining representation on $\\mathbb C^2$, with $h(v,w)=v^*w$ and $B(X,Y)=-\\operatorname{tr}_2(XY)$, the associated Hermitian bundle $E=P\\times_{SU(2)}\\mathbb C^2$ has\n\n$$\nq_B(P,\\omega)=-\\langle c_2(E),[X]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z,\n$$\n\nwhere $c_2(E)\\in H^4(X;\\mathbb Z)$ is the integral second Chern class and $[X]\\in H_4(X;\\mathbb Z)$ is the orientation fundamental class. This sign is fixed by the library convention $c(E,\\nabla)=\\det(I-\\Omega_\\nabla/(2\\pi i))$.\n\nThe Hodge operator $*_g$ on two-forms is characterized by $\\alpha\\wedge *_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_g$ and acts componentwise on $\\mathfrak g$-valued forms. Set $F_i^+=(F_i+*_gF_i)/2$ and $F_i^-=(F_i-*_gF_i)/2$. The self-dual sector consists of connections with $F_i^-=0$, equivalently $F_i=*_gF_i$; the anti-self-dual (ASD) sector consists of connections with $F_i^+=0$, equivalently $F_i=-*_gF_i$. These conditions are independent of the local section.' design_state= 'Step 1 ready; current prerequisites, placement, proof contract, and scoped checks recorded.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-5.pages.json thm-qftx-yang-mills-selfdual-action-bound statement= 'Assume full Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be a nonempty, connected, compact, oriented, finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth four-manifold without boundary, with Riemannian metric $g_X$. Let $G\\subseteq U(V,h)$ be a compact non-Abelian matrix Lie group in its specified global form, let $P\\to X$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle, and let $\\omega$ be a supplied smooth principal connection. Let $B$ be a positive-definite, symmetric, $\\operatorname{Ad}(G)$-invariant bilinear form on $\\mathfrak g$. For any local section $s_i$, set $A_i=s_i^*\\omega$ and $F_i=dA_i+\\tfrac12[A_i\\wedge A_i]$. Invariance of $B$ makes the local four-forms $\\beta_B|_{U_i}=\\frac{1}{8\\pi^2}B(F_i\\wedge F_i)$ and $\\gamma_B|_{U_i}=B(F_i\\wedge *_{g_X}F_i)$ restrictions of global forms. Define $q_B=\\int_X\\beta_B$. For a real scalar $g>0$, define the Euclidean Yang–Mills functional by\n\n$$\nS_B(P,\\omega):=\\frac{1}{g^2}\\int_X\\gamma_B,\n$$\n\nwhere the Hodge operator is characterized by $\\alpha\\wedge *_{g_X}\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_{g_X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_X}$ and acts componentwise on $\\mathfrak g$-valued forms. Then\n\n$$\nS_B(P,\\omega)\\geq \\frac{8\\pi^2}{g^2}\\,|q_B(P,\\omega)|.\n$$\n\nEquality holds exactly in these cases: if $q_B>0$, then $F_i=*_{g_X}F_i$; if $q_B<0$, then $F_i=-*_{g_X}F_i$; if $q_B=0$, then $F_i=0$. Every self-dual or anti-self-dual supplied connection satisfies the source-free Euclidean Yang–Mills equation $D_{A_i}(*_{g_X}F_i)=0$, where $D_{A_i}\\eta=d\\eta+[A_i\\wedge\\eta]$. The statement asserts neither existence nor uniqueness of a connection in any charge sector.' design_state= 'Step 1 ready; current prerequisites, placement, proof contract, and scoped checks recorded.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-5.pages.json prop-qftx-su2-bpst-explicit-connection-on-s4 statement= 'Assume full Axiom of Choice. Let $S^4=\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^5:|z|=1\\}$ have its unit round metric $g_X$. Denote by $p_\\infty$ the point omitted by the north stereographic chart and by $p_0$ the point with north-chart coordinates $x=0$. Orient $S^4$ so that north-chart coordinates $x=(x^1,x^2,x^3,x^4)$ satisfy $dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3\\wedge dx^4>0$. Set $r^2=\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(x^\\mu)^2$. The metric in the north chart is $4(1+r^2)^{-2}\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(dx^\\mu)^2$. Let $U_N=S^4\\setminus\\{p_\\infty\\}$ carry coordinate $x$, and $U_S=S^4\\setminus\\{p_0\\}$ carry coordinate $y=x/r^2$; the metric in the south chart is $4(1+|y|^2)^{-2}\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(dy^\\mu)^2$.\n\nDefine $SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I,\\det U=1\\}$. Write $\\epsilon_{123}=1$ and let\n\n$$ \\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad T_a=-\\frac{i}{2}\\sigma_a. $$\n\nThe $T_a$ are traceless anti-Hermitian generators. Set $B(X,Y)=-\\operatorname{tr}_2(XY)$. Define antisymmetric symbols by $\\eta_{aij}=\\epsilon_{aij}$, $\\eta_{ai4}=\\delta_{ai}$, $\\bar\\eta_{aij}=-\\epsilon_{aij}$, and $\\bar\\eta_{ai4}=\\delta_{ai}$ for spatial indices $i,j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, where $\\delta_{ai}$ is the Kronecker delta; extend them antisymmetrically in the last two indices. For a scale parameter $\\rho>0$, define $u(x)=(x^4I-i x^j\\sigma_j)/r\\in SU(2)$ on $U_N\\cap U_S$. Form the quotient of the two trivial right $SU(2)$-bundles by identifying $(x,h)_S\\sim(x,u(x)^{-1}h)_N$ for each $x\\in U_N\\cap U_S$ and $h\\in SU(2)$, so its local sections satisfy $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$. Take the set of admitted bundle sectors to be the singleton containing the isomorphism class of this glued bundle, and take the representation family to be the singleton defining representation on $\\mathbb C^2$. Define the local connection potentials by\n\n$$ A_N=\\frac{2\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}x^\\nu}{r^2+\\rho^2}T_a\\,dx^\\mu,\\qquad A_S=-\\frac{2\\rho^2\\bar\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}y^\\nu}{1+\\rho^2|y|^2}T_a\\,dy^\\mu. $$\n\nOn the overlap these obey $A_S=\\operatorname{Ad}_u A_N+u\\,d(u^{-1})$, so they define a smooth principal connection on the glued bundle. Let $F$ be its global curvature, with chart representatives $F_N$ and $F_S$. In the north chart its curvature, written $F_N=\\tfrac12F^N_{\\mu\\nu}dx^\\mu\\wedge dx^\\nu$, has components\n\n$$ F^N_{\\mu\\nu}=-\\frac{4\\rho^2}{(r^2+\\rho^2)^2}\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}T_a. $$\n\nLet $*_{g_X}$ be the Hodge star characterized on two-forms by $\\alpha\\wedge *_{g_X}\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_{g_X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_X$, acting componentwise on the Lie-algebra coefficients. This curvature is self-dual, meaning $F_N=*_{g_X}F_N$. With $B$ as fixed above, define the charge by $q_B=(8\\pi^2)^{-1}\\int_{S^4}B(F\\wedge F)$ and, for any real $g>0$, define the action by $S_B(g)=g^{-2}\\int_{S^4}B(F\\wedge *_{g_X}F)$. Then $q_B=1$, $S_B(g)=8\\pi^2/g^2<\\infty$, and the action saturates the self-dual bound. This is one explicit Belavin–Polyakov–Schwartz–Tyupkin (BPST) charge-one connection; no uniqueness or moduli classification is asserted.' design_state= 'Owner supplier repair: full source-backed mathematical construction promoted to A-page proposition; proof preserved.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-5.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-bpst-one-instanton statement= 'Assume full Axiom of Choice. Let $S^4=\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^5:|z|=1\\}$ have its unit round metric $g_X$. Denote by $p_\\infty$ the point omitted by the north stereographic chart and by $p_0$ the point with north-chart coordinates $x=0$. Orient $S^4$ so that north-chart coordinates $x=(x^1,x^2,x^3,x^4)$ satisfy $dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3\\wedge dx^4>0$. Set $r^2=\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(x^\\mu)^2$. The metric in the north chart is $4(1+r^2)^{-2}\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(dx^\\mu)^2$. Let $U_N=S^4\\setminus\\{p_\\infty\\}$ carry coordinate $x$, and $U_S=S^4\\setminus\\{p_0\\}$ carry coordinate $y=x/r^2$; the metric in the south chart is $4(1+|y|^2)^{-2}\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(dy^\\mu)^2$.\n\nDefine $SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I,\\det U=1\\}$. Write $\\epsilon_{123}=1$ and let\n\n$$ \\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad T_a=-\\frac{i}{2}\\sigma_a. $$\n\nThe $T_a$ are traceless anti-Hermitian generators. Set $B(X,Y)=-\\operatorname{tr}_2(XY)$. Define antisymmetric symbols by $\\eta_{aij}=\\epsilon_{aij}$, $\\eta_{ai4}=\\delta_{ai}$, $\\bar\\eta_{aij}=-\\epsilon_{aij}$, and $\\bar\\eta_{ai4}=\\delta_{ai}$ for spatial indices $i,j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, where $\\delta_{ai}$ is the Kronecker delta; extend them antisymmetrically in the last two indices. For a scale parameter $\\rho>0$, define $u(x)=(x^4I-i x^j\\sigma_j)/r\\in SU(2)$ on $U_N\\cap U_S$. Form the quotient of the two trivial right $SU(2)$-bundles by identifying $(x,h)_S\\sim(x,u(x)^{-1}h)_N$ for each $x\\in U_N\\cap U_S$ and $h\\in SU(2)$, so its local sections satisfy $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$. Take the set of admitted bundle sectors to be the singleton containing the isomorphism class of this glued bundle, and take the representation family to be the singleton defining representation on $\\mathbb C^2$. Define the local connection potentials by\n\n$$ A_N=\\frac{2\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}x^\\nu}{r^2+\\rho^2}T_a\\,dx^\\mu,\\qquad A_S=-\\frac{2\\rho^2\\bar\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}y^\\nu}{1+\\rho^2|y|^2}T_a\\,dy^\\mu. $$\n\nOn the overlap these obey $A_S=\\operatorname{Ad}_u A_N+u\\,d(u^{-1})$, so they define a smooth principal connection on the glued bundle. Let $F$ be its global curvature, with chart representatives $F_N$ and $F_S$. In the north chart its curvature, written $F_N=\\tfrac12F^N_{\\mu\\nu}dx^\\mu\\wedge dx^\\nu$, has components\n\n$$ F^N_{\\mu\\nu}=-\\frac{4\\rho^2}{(r^2+\\rho^2)^2}\\eta_{a\\mu\\nu}T_a. $$\n\nLet $*_{g_X}$ be the Hodge star characterized on two-forms by $\\alpha\\wedge *_{g_X}\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_{g_X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_X$, acting componentwise on the Lie-algebra coefficients. This curvature is self-dual, meaning $F_N=*_{g_X}F_N$. With $B$ as fixed above, define the charge by $q_B=(8\\pi^2)^{-1}\\int_{S^4}B(F\\wedge F)$ and, for any real $g>0$, define the action by $S_B(g)=g^{-2}\\int_{S^4}B(F\\wedge *_{g_X}F)$. Then $q_B=1$, $S_B(g)=8\\pi^2/g^2<\\infty$, and the action saturates the self-dual bound. This is one explicit Belavin–Polyakov–Schwartz–Tyupkin (BPST) charge-one connection; no uniqueness or moduli classification is asserted.' design_state= 'Step 1 ready; current prerequisites, placement, proof contract, and scoped checks recorded.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-52.pages.json def-qftx-adjoint-higgs-monopole-sector statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Use natural units $\\hbar=c=1$ on Minkowski spacetime $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, orientation $dt\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, and spatial orientation $\\epsilon_{123}=1$. Fix the global gauge group $SU(2)$, not $SO(3)$, and the trivial right principal $SU(2)$-bundle over each spatial slice. Let\n\n$$T_a=-\\frac{i}{2}\\sigma_a\\quad(a=1,2,3),\\qquad \\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY),$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma_a$ are the Pauli matrices and $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ has basis $T_1,T_2,T_3$. Thus $[T_a,T_b]=\\epsilon_{abc}T_c$ and $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$. The positive coupling $g$ is dimensionless. The scalar field is one real adjoint Higgs field: a smooth $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued map $\\Phi$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, viewed as the real form in the complexified adjoint representation. A gauge connection is a smooth $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$-valued one-form $A=A_i dx^i$. In a local section change $s_j=s_i h_{ij}$, use the right-action convention $A_j=\\operatorname{Ad}_{h_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+h_{ij}^{-1}dh_{ij}$ and $\\Phi_j=\\operatorname{Ad}_{h_{ij}^{-1}}\\Phi_i$. Define\n\n$$F_{ij}=\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i+[A_i,A_j],\\qquad D_i\\Phi=\\partial_i\\Phi+[A_i,\\Phi],\\qquad B_i=\\frac12\\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}.$$\n\nThe static Prasad–Sommerfield sector has $\\partial_tA_i=\\partial_t\\Phi=0$, $A_0=0$, no other dynamical fields, and identically zero Higgs potential. Its adopted energy functional is\n\n$$E[A,\\Phi]=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_{i=1}^3\\bigl(\\kappa(B_i,B_i)+\\kappa(D_i\\Phi,D_i\\Phi)\\bigr)\\,d^3x.$$\n\nThe field dimensions are $[A_i]=[\\Phi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$ and $[F_{ij}]=[B_i]=[D_i\\Phi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-2}$, so $E$ has dimension $\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$. A configuration is finite-energy when this integral is finite.\n\nThe boundary data fix a number $v>0$ of dimension $\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$ and require $|\\Phi|_\\kappa\\to v$ and $n_\\infty(\\omega):=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\Phi(R\\omega)/v$ to exist smoothly on the unit sphere $S^2$ of spatial directions. Here $|X|_\\kappa^2:=\\kappa(X,X)$. The limiting tangential covariant derivative of $n_\\infty$ vanishes, and the limiting magnetic field lies in the pointwise stabilizer Lie algebra $\\{X\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2):[X,n_\\infty(\\omega)]=0\\}$. The stabilizer subgroup of $T_3$ is $U(1)=\\{\\exp(\\theta T_3):\\theta\\in\\mathbb R/(4\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\}$. On closed northern and southern hemispheres choose smooth local lifts $u_N,u_S$ with $\\operatorname{Ad}_{u_\\alpha}T_3=n_\\infty$; their equatorial transition is $u_S=u_N h$ with $h(\\varphi)=\\exp(\\theta(\\varphi)T_3)$, where $\\varphi\\in[0,2\\pi]$ parametrizes the equator and $\\theta:[0,2\\pi]\\to\\mathbb R$ is a smooth lift. The local adapted frames, connection forms, and curvature forms extend smoothly, respectively $C^1$, to open neighborhoods of the closed hemispheres. The surface limit below exists. The admissible external Wilson probes include the fundamental doublet, on which $T_3$ has weights $\\pm\\tfrac12$; this specifies the electric probe lattice without adding a propagating fundamental matter field. Define the classical magnetic surface charge and its energy normalization by\n\n$$m:=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R^2}\\kappa(\\Phi,B_i)\\,\\hat x_i\\,dS,\\qquad T:=g^{-2}m,$$\n\nwhere $\\hat x=x/|x|$ is the outward unit normal and $dS$ is Euclidean area. The transition increment $\\theta(2\\pi)-\\theta(0)$ is not assumed to be quantized in this definition. These are adopted classical model data; they assert no empirical law, quantum continuum construction, supersymmetry representation, or quantum BPS state.' design_state= 'Authored from the assigned model contract; exact gauge and SU(2) supplier interfaces inspected.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-52.pages.json pthm-qftx-su2-monopole-flux-quantization statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. On oriented Euclidean $\\mathbb R^3$ with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, use the zero-potential static model with global group $SU(2)$, $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, $F_{ij}=\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i+[A_i,A_j]$, $D_i\\Phi=\\partial_i\\Phi+[A_i,\\Phi]$, and $B_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}$. Let $g>0$ be dimensionless, $v>0$ of dimension $\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, and $A_i,\\Phi$ be smooth static fields. Assume $|\\Phi|_\\kappa\\to v$, $\\Phi(R\\omega)/v\\to n_\\infty(\\omega)$ smoothly, $D_{\\mathrm{tan}}n_\\infty=0$, and $B_\\infty$ takes values in the centralizer of $n_\\infty$. Assume the limiting adapted local connections and curvatures are $C^1$ on neighborhoods of the closed northern and southern hemispheres. Choose smooth lifts $u_N,u_S$ with $\\operatorname{Ad}_{u_\\alpha}T_3=n_\\infty$ and transition $u_S=u_Nh$ on their common equator, where $h(\\varphi)=\\exp(\\theta(\\varphi)T_3)$ and $\\theta:[0,2\\pi]\\to\\mathbb R$ is a smooth lift. The equator is oriented as the boundary of the northern hemisphere, and the admissible external electric probes include the fundamental doublet. If the surface flux limit\n\n$$m=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R^2}\\kappa(\\Phi,B_i)\\hat x_i\\,dS$$\n\nexists, define\n\n$$\\nu:=-\\frac{\\theta(2\\pi)-\\theta(0)}{4\\pi}.$$\n\nThen $\\nu\\in\\mathbb Z$ and the classical magnetic surface charge and energy-normalized surface term are\n\n$$m=4\\pi v\\nu,\\qquad T=\\frac{m}{g^2}=\\frac{4\\pi v}{g^2}\\nu.$$\n\nThis is flux quantization for the stated global form, boundary reduction, and probe lattice; it does not assert a universal normalization for other groups or global forms.' design_state= "Added on the assigned A page to supply the design's separate flux-quantization obligation."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-52.pages.json pthm-qftx-bogomolny-monopole-energy-bound statement= "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Use natural units on Minkowski $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and oriented Euclidean spatial slice with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$. Fix the global group $SU(2)$, basis $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, and positive $\\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ with $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$. Let $g>0$ be dimensionless and let $A_i,\\Phi$ be smooth static fields in the zero-potential adjoint-Higgs model, with $[A_i]=[\\Phi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $F_{ij}=\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i+[A_i,A_j]$, $D_i\\Phi=\\partial_i\\Phi+[A_i,\\Phi]$, and $B_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}$. Require the model's smooth finite-energy boundary reduction: $|\\Phi|_\\kappa\\to v>0$ for a fixed $v$ of dimension $\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, with smooth $n_\\infty=\\Phi/v$, the limiting tangential covariant derivative of $n_\\infty$ vanishes, and the limiting magnetic field lies in its stabilizer algebra. Suppose the energy\n\n$$E=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_i\\bigl(\\kappa(B_i,B_i)+\\kappa(D_i\\Phi,D_i\\Phi)\\bigr)\\,d^3x$$\n\nis finite and the model's asymptotic reduction and surface limit\n\n$$m=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R^2}\\kappa(\\Phi,B_i)\\hat x_i\\,dS$$\n\nexist. Put $T=m/g^2$. Then both identities hold:\n\n$$E=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_i\\kappa(B_i-D_i\\Phi,B_i-D_i\\Phi)\\,d^3x+T,$$\n\n$$E=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_i\\kappa(B_i+D_i\\Phi,B_i+D_i\\Phi)\\,d^3x-T.$$\n\nConsequently $E\\ge |T|$. If $T>0$, equality holds exactly when $B_i=D_i\\Phi$ for every $i$ and every $x$; if $T<0$, equality holds exactly when $B_i=-D_i\\Phi$ everywhere. If $T=0$, equality holds exactly when $B_i=0$ and $D_i\\Phi=0$ everywhere. These are conditional classical deductions for the adopted energy functional; the word BPS here refers only to classical energy saturation and asserts no quantum state or supersymmetry central-charge relation." design_state= 'Authored with both normalized square identities, Bianchi surface term, and separate zero-charge equality case.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-52.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-bps-monopole statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and use natural units on oriented Euclidean $\\mathbb R^3$ with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, the zero-potential static model with one real adjoint Higgs field, no other dynamical matter, and global group $SU(2)$ (not $SO(3)$). Let $A_i,\\Phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3,\\mathfrak{su}(2))$ have dimensions $[A_i]=[\\Phi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$. Let $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ with $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$, $g>0$ be dimensionless, and $v>0$ have dimension $\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$. The fields use $F_{ij}=\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i+[A_i,A_j]$, $D_i\\Phi=\\partial_i\\Phi+[A_i,\\Phi]$, $B_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}$, and $E=(2g^2)^{-1}\\int\\sum_i\\bigl(\\kappa(B_i,B_i)+\\kappa(D_i\\Phi,D_i\\Phi)\\bigr)\\,d^3x$. The allowed external electric probes include the fundamental doublet. For $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $r=|x|>0$, $n_i=x_i/r$, and $u=vr$, define\n\n$$H(u)=\\coth u-\\frac1u,\\qquad K(u)=\\frac{u}{\\sinh u},\\qquad \\Phi^a(x)=-vH(u)n_a,\\qquad A_i^a(x)=\\epsilon_{aij}n_j\\frac{1-K(u)}r.$$\n\nUse the removable analytic limits $H(0)=0$ and $K(0)=1$. At infinity $\\Phi\\to-v\\,\\hat x^aT_a$, the unbroken algebra is its pointwise stabilizer, and the equatorial transition $h(\\varphi)=\\exp(\\theta(\\varphi)T_3)$ has integer $\\nu=-(\\theta(2\\pi)-\\theta(0))/(4\\pi)$ in the fundamental-doublet-compatible SU(2) convention. In these conventions the fields extend smoothly to $r=0$, satisfy the positive-charge Bogomolny equation $B_i=D_i\\Phi$, have $m=4\\pi v$ and $\\nu=1$, and have finite energy $E=4\\pi v/g^2$. They give an explicit smooth unit-flux solution of the first-order classical Bogomolny equation that saturates the classical energy bound. No quantum state, supersymmetry representation, or empirical observation is asserted.' design_state= 'Authored as a smooth positive-unit-flux profile; local field formulas and energy are verified in the example carrier.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data statement= 'Assume the axiom of countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Fix the compact noncommutative finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, its Lie algebra $\\mathfrak g=T_eG$, and a specified smooth finite-dimensional complex unitary representation $\\rho:G\\to U(W,h)$ on a Hermitian vector space $(W,h)$ supplied by a non-Abelian gauge datum. Thus the group multiplication and inversion are smooth, $G$ is compact in its manifold topology, and $\\rho(g)$ preserves $h$ for every $g\\in G$. No connection, principal bundle sector, action functional, or continuum theory is part of the lattice data below.\n\nA **finite oriented lattice two-complex** is a finite set $\\mathcal V$ of vertices, a finite set $\\mathcal E^+$ containing one chosen orientation of each edge, an endpoint map $s,t:\\mathcal E^+\\to\\mathcal V$, and a finite set $\\mathcal F$ of faces. Each face $p\\in\\mathcal F$ is supplied with a based closed edge word $(e_1,\\ldots,e_k)$ in the oriented edges $\\mathcal E^+\\sqcup\\overline{\\mathcal E^+}$, where the endpoint of each edge is the starting point of the next and the last endpoint is the first starting point. For the reverse orientation $\\bar e$ of $e\\in\\mathcal E^+$, set $s(\\bar e)=t(e)$ and $t(\\bar e)=s(e)$.\n\nA **link configuration** is a tuple $U=(U_e)_{e\\in\\mathcal E^+}\\in G^{\\mathcal E^+}$, with $U_{\\bar e}:=U_e^{-1}$. The **holonomy of a face** $p=(e_1,\\ldots,e_k)$ is the ordered product $U_p:=U_{e_1}\\cdots U_{e_k}$ in traversal order, with the first traversed edge on the left. The vertex gauge group is $G^{\\mathcal V}$ with coordinatewise multiplication; write $\\mathbf e=(e)_{v\\in\\mathcal V}$ for its identity tuple. It acts by\n\n$$\n(h\\cdot U)_e=h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1},\\qquad h=(h_v)_{v\\in\\mathcal V}\\in G^{\\mathcal V}.\n$$\n\nChoose a normalized bi-invariant Borel probability measure $\\mu_G$ on $G$. The link measure is the finite product $\\mu_G^{\\mathcal E^+}$. For each face choose a continuous function $\\Phi_p:G\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfying $\\Phi_p(gzg^{-1})=\\Phi_p(z)$ and $\\Phi_p(z^{-1})=\\Phi_p(z)$. Define the dimensionless lattice action and its normalized expectation by\n\n$$\nS_\\Lambda(U):=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal F}\\Phi_p(U_p),\\qquad Z_\\Lambda:=\\int_{G^{\\mathcal E^+}}e^{-S_\\Lambda(U)}\\,d\\mu_G^{\\mathcal E^+}(U),\\qquad \\langle O\\rangle_\\Lambda:=Z_\\Lambda^{-1}\\int_{G^{\\mathcal E^+}}O(U)e^{-S_\\Lambda(U)}\\,d\\mu_G^{\\mathcal E^+}(U)\n$$\n\nfor bounded measurable $O:G^{\\mathcal E^+}\\to\\mathbb C$. The finite-volume **Gauss sector** is the closed subspace of $L^2(G^{\\mathcal E^+},\\mathbb P_\\Lambda)$ consisting of measurable functions with finite integral of $|f|^2$, modulo equality $\\mathbb P_\\Lambda$-almost everywhere, whose equivalence classes are invariant under each element of $G^{\\mathcal V}$. Here $\\mathbb P_\\Lambda$ is the normalized probability with density $Z_\\Lambda^{-1}e^{-S_\\Lambda}$ relative to product link measure. For $v\\in\\mathcal V$ and $X\\in\\mathfrak g$, let $X^{(v)}$ be the tangent vector at the identity of $G^{\\mathcal V}$ with component $X$ at $v$ and zero at other vertices. If $\\alpha(h,U)=h\\cdot U$, define the infinitesimal **Gauss generator** on $C^\\infty(G^{\\mathcal E^+})$ by\n\n$$\n(D_{v,X}f)(U):=df_U\\!\\left(d\\alpha_{(\\mathbf e,U)}(X^{(v)},0)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe full Gauss sector uses invariance under every vertex gauge transformation; the infinitesimal equations $D_{v,X}f=0$ alone are not asserted to impose invariance under disconnected components of $G$. Link variables and the action are dimensionless. This finite mathematical regulator makes no continuum-limit or empirical claim.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json lem-qftx-lattice-data-well-defined statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every finite oriented lattice two-complex with compact Lie group $G$, normalized bi-invariant link probability $\\mu_G$, and continuous conjugation-invariant face actions $\\Phi_p$, the vertex formulas $(h\\cdot U)_e=h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$ define a smooth group action on $G^{\\mathcal E^+}$. Each face holonomy transforms by conjugation at its base vertex. Consequently the product Haar measure and the Boltzmann weight $e^{-\\sum_p\\Phi_p(U_p)}$ are gauge invariant, and the partition function satisfies $0<Z_\\Lambda<\\infty$. Thus the normalized finite-lattice expectation is a well-defined gauge-invariant probability expectation. In $L^2$ of that probability, the subspace of classes invariant under each vertex gauge transformation is closed. The infinitesimal Gauss generators defined by differentiating this smooth action are well-defined as directional derivatives on smooth functions.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json def-qftx-wilson-loop statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and take finite oriented lattice data with compact group $G$, link variables $U_e$, and vertex action $U_e\\mapsto h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$. Fix a positive-dimensional finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space $(W,h)$ and a smooth group homomorphism $\\rho:G\\to U(W,h)$, where $U(W,h)$ is the group of invertible complex-linear maps preserving $h$; put $d=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}W\\geq1$. A **based closed lattice loop** is a finite edge word $C=(e_1,\\ldots,e_\\ell)$ of integer length $\\ell\\geq0$ with a chosen base vertex $v_0$, such that consecutive endpoints match and, when $\\ell>0$, the first and last endpoints are $v_0$; when $\\ell=0$, the loop consists only of $v_0$. Its ordered holonomy is $U_C:=U_{e_1}\\cdots U_{e_\\ell}$, in traversal order with the first edge on the left; for $\\ell=0$, set $U_C=e$. The reversed word is $C^{-1}=(\\bar e_\\ell,\\ldots,\\bar e_1)$. The **normalized Wilson loop** is\n\n$$\nW_\\rho(C;U):=\\frac{1}{d}\\operatorname{Tr}_W\\!\\big(\\rho(U_C)\\big).\n$$\n\nThe loop is dimensionless. Changing the base vertex by a cyclic rebase conjugates $U_C$ and leaves $W_\\rho$ unchanged; reversing the loop gives $W_\\rho(C^{-1};U)=\\overline{W_\\rho(C;U)}$. On a fixed finite lattice with a specified finite product Haar probability and bounded action, $\\langle W_\\rho(C)\\rangle_\\Lambda$ is the ordinary normalized finite-dimensional integral. No renormalized continuum Wilson loop or continuum limit is defined here.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json lem-qftx-wilson-loop-gauge-invariance statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $G$ be a group and let $(W,q)$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian inner-product space. Define $U(W,q):=\\{T\\in\\operatorname{GL}_{\\mathbb C}(W):q(Tu,Tv)=q(u,v)\\text{ for all }u,v\\in W\\}$, and let $\\rho:G\\to U(W,q)$ be a group homomorphism, with $d=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}W\\geq1$. Let a finite oriented graph have vertices $\\mathcal V$ and one chosen orientation $\\mathcal E^+$ for each edge; for $e\\in\\mathcal E^+$ write $s(e),t(e)$ for its source and target. A link configuration assigns $U_e\\in G$ to each $e\\in\\mathcal E^+$ and sets $U_{\\bar e}=U_e^{-1}$. A based closed edge word $C=(e_1,\\ldots,e_\\ell)$ has length $\\ell\\geq0$, consecutive endpoints match, and when $\\ell>0$ it starts and ends at a chosen vertex $v_0$; for $\\ell=0$ it consists only of $v_0$. Put $U_C:=U_{e_1}\\cdots U_{e_\\ell}$ in traversal order, with the empty product equal to the identity, and put $W_\\rho(C;U):=d^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}_W(\\rho(U_C))$. For every vertex tuple $k\\in G^{\\mathcal V}$ acting by $(k\\cdot U)_e=k_{s(e)}U_ek_{t(e)}^{-1}$, one has $U_C(k\\cdot U)=k_{v_0}U_C(U)k_{v_0}^{-1}$. Thus the normalized trace is gauge invariant and unchanged when the based word is cyclically rotated; for $\\ell>0$ reversal is $C^{-1}=(\\bar e_\\ell,\\ldots,\\bar e_1)$, while the empty word reverses to itself, and $W_\\rho(C^{-1};U)=\\overline{W_\\rho(C;U)}$.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json def-qftx-su2-wilson-plaquette-measure statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Use the matrix group $G_2=SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\\ \\det U=1\\}$ with matrix multiplication and its standard real Lie-group structure. Its defining representation is $\\rho_2(U)=U$ on $W=\\mathbb C^2$ with Hermitian form $h(v,w)=v^*w$. In real coordinates every element has the form\n\n$$\nU(x)=\\begin{pmatrix}x_0+ix_3&x_2+ix_1\\\\-x_2+ix_1&x_0-ix_3\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad x\\in S^3:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^4:\\textstyle\\sum_{j=0}^3x_j^2=1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet $\\mu_2$ be the pushforward of normalized round surface measure on $S^3$ under $x\\mapsto U(x)$. In spherical coordinates $x_0=\\cos\\theta$, $(x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\sin\\theta\\,n$, $0\\leq\\theta\\leq\\pi$, $n\\in S^2$, its coordinate density is $d\\mu_2=(2/\\pi)\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\theta\\,d\\omega_2(n)$, where $n=(\\sin\\varphi\\cos\\psi,\\sin\\varphi\\sin\\psi,\\cos\\varphi)$ and $d\\omega_2=(1/(4\\pi))\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\psi$ for $0\\leq\\varphi\\leq\\pi$, $0\\leq\\psi<2\\pi$. Define the real class function $\\tau(U):=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}_2(U)=x_0$.\n\nFor dimensionless $\\beta\\geq0$, define the pure-gauge Wilson plaquette action on any finite oriented lattice by\n\n$$\nS_\\beta(U):=\\beta\\sum_{p}\\bigl(1-\\tau(U_p)\\bigr),\\qquad \\beta=\\frac{4}{g_0^2}\n$$\n\nwhen a dimensionless bare coupling $g_0>0$ is used. The finite link measure is the product of $\\mu_2$ over positively oriented edges, and its normalized expectation is the finite product integral with density proportional to $e^{-S_\\beta}$. The one-plaquette normalizing factor and one-plaquette probability are\n\n$$\nz_\\beta:=\\int_{G_2}e^{\\beta\\tau(U)}\\,d\\mu_2(U),\\qquad d\\nu_\\beta(U):=z_\\beta^{-1}e^{\\beta\\tau(U)}d\\mu_2(U).\n$$\n\nThis defines only a finite-lattice mathematical measure; it does not define an infinite-volume or continuum expectation.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json lem-qftx-planar-plaquette-factorization statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and let $G$ be a compact group with normalized bi-invariant Borel probability $\\mu$. For positive integers $M,N$, let $\\Lambda_{M,N}$ have vertices $(i,j)$ with $0\\leq i\\leq M$ and $0\\leq j\\leq N$, east-oriented horizontal edges $h_{i,j}:(i,j)\\to(i+1,j)$ for $0\\leq i<M$, $0\\leq j\\leq N$, north-oriented vertical edges $v_{i,j}:(i,j)\\to(i,j+1)$ for $0\\leq i\\leq M$, $0\\leq j<N$, and faces $p_{i,j}$ for $0\\leq i<M$, $0\\leq j<N$. The face $p_{i,j}$ has counterclockwise boundary word $(h_{i,j},v_{i+1,j},\\bar h_{i,j+1},\\bar v_{i,j})$, started at its lower-left vertex; multiply its link matrices in traversal order, with the first factor on the left. Let $G^{\\mathcal V}$ act on positively oriented link variables by $(g\\cdot U)_e=g_{s(e)}U_eg_{t(e)}^{-1}$. Use the product Haar measure $\\mu^{\\mathcal E^+}$ on links and free boundary edges. Give each face a positive continuous conjugation-invariant weight $w_{i,j}:G\\to(0,\\infty)$ with $w_{i,j}(g^{-1})=w_{i,j}(g)$, and normalize the density proportional to $\\prod_{i,j}w_{i,j}(U_{p_{i,j}})$ relative to this link measure. For any chosen column $a\\in\\{0,\\ldots,M\\}$, fixing the gauge factor at $(a,0)$ to the identity gives a unique representative with every horizontal edge and every vertical edge in column $a$ equal to the identity. Write $V_{i,j}$ for the vertical link in this representative and set $P_{i,j}:=V_{i+1,j}V_{i,j}^{-1}$. The plaquette variables $(P_{i,j})$ are independent, and $P_{i,j}$ has probability\n\n$$\nd\\nu_{i,j}(g)=\\frac{w_{i,j}(g)}{\\int_Gw_{i,j}(z)\\,d\\mu(z)}\\,d\\mu(g).\n$$\n\nThis is a finite planar lattice statement with no periodic identifications or continuum limit.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json lem-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-average statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and use the finite $SU(2)$ Wilson plaquette measure with $\\beta>0$ defined above. Put\n\n$$\nI_0(\\beta):=\\int_{SU(2)}e^{\\beta\\tau(U)}d\\mu_2(U),\\qquad I_1(\\beta):=\\int_{SU(2)}\\tau(U)e^{\\beta\\tau(U)}d\\mu_2(U),\\qquad \\lambda(\\beta):=I_1(\\beta)/I_0(\\beta).\n$$\n\nThen $\\mu_2$ is a normalized bi-invariant Borel probability, $I_0(\\beta)>0$, $0<\\lambda(\\beta)<1$, and the defining-representation matrix expectation is\n\n$$\n\\int_{SU(2)}U\\,d\\nu_\\beta(U)=\\lambda(\\beta)I_2.\n$$\n\nAs $\\beta\\downarrow0$, $\\lambda(\\beta)=\\beta/4+O(\\beta^3)$. The parameter $\\beta$ and the one-plaquette expectation are dimensionless.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json thm-qftx-strong-coupling-wilson-area-law statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M,N\\geq1$ be integers and use the finite $M$-by-$N$ square lattice with free boundary (no periodic identifications and all boundary links retained), one $SU(2)$ link matrix per positively oriented edge, product normalized round measure, and only link variables (no matter variables), with the Wilson action $S_\\beta(U)=\\beta\\sum_p(1-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}_2 U_p)$. Use the defining two-dimensional representation and normalized Wilson loop $W(C)=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}_2 U_C$, with edge matrices multiplied in traversal order. Let $\\beta=4/g_0^2$ for dimensionless $g_0>0$, and restrict to the explicit strong-coupling window $0<\\beta\\leq1$ (equivalently $g_0^2\\geq4$). For any axis-aligned rectangle contained in the lattice with corners $(a,c)$ and $(b,d)$, where $0\\leq a<b\\leq M$ and $0\\leq c<d\\leq N$, let $C_{a,b;c,d}$ be its boundary traversed counterclockwise from $(a,c)$. Its area in plaquette units is $A=(b-a)(d-c)$. The defining representation has nontrivial $\\mathbb Z_2$ center charge because $-I_2$ acts as $-I_2$. Put\n\n$$\n\\lambda(\\beta):=\\frac{\\int_{SU(2)}\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}_2(U)e^{\\beta\\operatorname{Tr}_2(U)/2}\\,d\\mu_2(U)}{\\int_{SU(2)}e^{\\beta\\operatorname{Tr}_2(U)/2}\\,d\\mu_2(U)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu_2$ is normalized round measure on $SU(2)\\cong S^3$. Then $0<\\lambda(\\beta)<1$ and the exact finite-lattice expectation is\n\n$$\n\\langle W(C_{a,b;c,d})\\rangle_\\beta=\\lambda(\\beta)^A=\\exp\\!\\bigl(-\\sigma_{\\rm lat}(\\beta)A\\bigr),\\qquad \\sigma_{\\rm lat}(\\beta):=-\\log\\lambda(\\beta)>0.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $\\beta$ and the lattice string tension $\\sigma_{\\rm lat}$ are dimensionless. The identity holds for every finite container and every stated rectangle; no infinite-area, infinite-volume, or continuum limit is taken, and no continuum confinement or mass-gap claim follows.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-wilson-average statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For one square face with free boundary in the finite $SU(2)$ Wilson model at $0<\\beta\\leq1$, the normalized plaquette holonomy has law $d\\nu_\\beta(U)=z_\\beta^{-1}e^{\\beta\\tau(U)}d\\mu_2(U)$, where $\\tau(U)=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}_2U$ and $z_\\beta=\\int e^{\\beta\\tau}d\\mu_2$. The Wilson action partition factor is $Z_\\square=e^{-\\beta}z_\\beta$, and the normalized fundamental Wilson loop around the square has expectation $\\langle W(\\partial\\square)\\rangle_\\beta=\\lambda(\\beta)$, where $\\lambda(\\beta)=\\int\\tau e^{\\beta\\tau}d\\mu_2/z_\\beta=\\beta/4+O(\\beta^3)$ as $\\beta\\downarrow0$.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-6.pages.json ex-qftx-su2-two-plaquette-wilson-loop statement= 'Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Take a free-boundary $SU(2)$ square lattice containing a rectangle two plaquettes wide and one plaquette high, with $0<\\beta\\leq1$ and the defining normalized Wilson loop. Its enclosed area is $A=2$ plaquettes, so the exact finite-lattice expectation is $\\langle W(C)\\rangle_\\beta=\\lambda(\\beta)^2=\\beta^2/16+O(\\beta^4)$ as $\\beta\\downarrow0$, where $\\lambda(\\beta)$ is the one-plaquette coefficient from the finite SU(2) Wilson measure.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored; authorized inventory expansion recorded; readiness recording follows final scoped checks.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-7.pages.json def-standard-product-torus-as-a-compact-lie-group statement= 'Assume the full Axiom of Choice. For $r\\geq0$, let\n$T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r$ with the quotient topology and coordinatewise\naddition. On each circle factor, for every open interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$\nof length less than $1$, use the chart\n$q(I)\\to I$ given by the unique lift through the quotient map\n$q:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$; give $T$ the product smooth structure.\nWrite $\\mathfrak t=T_0T$. For $1\\leq i\\leq r$, let $e_i$ be the tangent vector\nat $0$ to the one-parameter subgroup\n$s\\mapsto(0,\\ldots,[s],\\ldots,0)$ in the $i$th coordinate. These vectors form\nthe period-one basis of $\\mathfrak t$, and $e_i^*$ denotes its dual basis.\nThe standard Lie exponential is normalized by\n$\\exp_T(s e_i)=(0,\\ldots,[s],\\ldots,0)$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$.\nFor $r=0$, use the one-point group and the empty basis. This convention makes\n$T$ a compact connected abelian Lie group, hence a torus in the sense of the\nstandard compact-Lie-group definition; its identity inclusion is a cofibration,\nso this based group is well-pointed.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-7.pages.json lem-torus-classifying-space-cohomology statement= "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $r\\geq0$, let\n$T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r$, and fix its displayed product decomposition. For\neach factor use the standard quotient Lie-group smooth structure and\nperiod-one tangent basis. Identify $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ with the\nmultiplicative unit circle by $\\epsilon([t])=e^{2\\pi i t}$ and transfer its\nright action to Milnor's countable-join model $E_{S^1}$; put\n$ET=(E_{S^1})^r$ with the coordinatewise right $T$-action and set $BT=ET/T$.\nLet $e_i\\in\\mathfrak t$ be the period-one tangent vector in the $i$th circle\nfactor and $e_i^*$ its dual. Let $u=e(\\gamma_{\\mathbb R})$ be the integral\ndegree-two generator of $H^*(\\mathbb{CP}^{\\infty};\\mathbb Z)$ defined by the\ncomplex orientation of the real rank-two bundle underlying the tautological\ncomplex line, and let $u_{\\mathbb R}$ be its image under change of coefficients\n$\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R$. Under the product quotient identification\n$BT\\cong(\\mathbb{CP}^{\\infty})^r$, write $u_i$ for the pullback of\n$u_{\\mathbb R}$ from the $i$th factor. Then\n$$H^*(BT;\\mathbb R)\\cong\\mathbb R[u_1,\\ldots,u_r],\\qquad |u_i|=2,$$\nand the chosen identification $e_i^*\\mapsto u_i$ is a graded-algebra\nisomorphism from $\\operatorname{Sym}_{\\mathbb R}(\\mathfrak t^*)$, with its\nlinear generators assigned degree two. For $r=0$, both rings are $\\mathbb R$." design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-7.pages.json def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model statement= 'Let $T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r$ be a compact torus with its standard product Lie-group smooth structure and period-one basis, and let $M$ be a smooth manifold without boundary, not assumed compact, with a smooth left action $a:T\\times M\\to M$. Work over $\\mathbb R$ and use the chosen product Milnor model $ET=(E_{S^1})^r$ and $BT=ET/T$ from the preceding coefficient lemma. Give $ET\\times M$ the right diagonal action $(e,p)\\cdot t=(e\\cdot t,t^{-1}\\cdot p)$ and define\n$$ET\\times_T M:=(ET\\times M)/T.$$\nThe **Borel equivariant cohomology** is the graded ring\n$$H_T^*(M;\\mathbb R):=H_{\\mathrm{sing}}^*(ET\\times_T M;\\mathbb R).$$\nFor $M=\\{\\ast\\}$ this gives $H_T^*(\\ast;\\mathbb R)=H^*(BT;\\mathbb R)$; the selected degree-two normalization in $\\operatorname{Sym}_{\\mathbb R}(\\mathfrak t^*)$ is the isomorphism established in the preceding coefficient lemma.\n\nThe quotient map $q:ET\\times M\\to ET\\times_T M$ and projection\n$\\pi([e,p])=[e]$ give the associated-space square\n$$\\begin{array}{ccc}ET\\times M&\\xrightarrow{q}&ET\\times_T M\\\\\\downarrow\\operatorname{pr}_1&&\\downarrow\\pi\\\\ET&\\xrightarrow{q_T}&BT\\end{array}$$\nIts TikZ source is\n```latex\n\\begin{tikzcd}\nET\\times M \\arrow[r,"q"] \\arrow[d,"\\operatorname{pr}_1"\'] & ET\\times_T M \\arrow[d,"\\pi"]\\\\\nET \\arrow[r,"q_T"\'] & BT\n\\end{tikzcd}\n```\nwhere $q_T:ET\\to BT$ is the orbit quotient. The projection $\\pi$ is\nwell-defined because $[e\\cdot t]=[e]$ for every $t\\in T$. The square\ncommutes because both composites send $(e,p)$ to $[e]$.\n\nLet $\\mathfrak t=T_eT$, with basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_r$ and dual basis $u_1,\\ldots,u_r$. Regard $\\operatorname{Sym}_{\\mathbb R}(\\mathfrak t^*)$ as a polynomial algebra by assigning each linear generator degree two. Use the trivial $T$-action on this polynomial factor, and the induced left action $t\\cdot\\alpha=(a_{t^{-1}})^*\\alpha$ on forms. It is a left action because pullback reverses composition: $a_{t^{-1}}^*a_{s^{-1}}^*=(a_{s^{-1}}\\circ a_{t^{-1}})^*=a_{(ts)^{-1}}^*$. The **Cartan complex** is the graded-commutative algebra\n$$C_T(M):=\\bigl(\\operatorname{Sym}_{\\mathbb R}(\\mathfrak t^*)\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\Omega^*(M)\\bigr)^T,$$\nwith multiplication $(P\\otimes\\alpha)(Q\\otimes\\beta)=PQ\\otimes(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)$, where $\\wedge$ is the differential-form wedge product and all tensor products are algebraic. The superscript denotes the fixed subspace for the diagonal action, and a polynomial of degree $j$ tensored with a $k$-form has total degree $2j+k$. For $\\xi\\in\\mathfrak t$, use the library fundamental vector field\n$$X_\\xi(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}\\right|_{s=0}\\exp_T(-s\\xi)\\cdot p.$$\nOn polynomial-valued forms define the degree-one Cartan operator\n$$d_T(P\\otimes\\alpha)=P\\otimes d\\alpha-\\sum_{i=1}^r(u_iP)\\otimes\\iota_{X_{e_i}}\\alpha,$$\nextended linearly. The tensor $\\sum_i u_i\\otimes X_{e_i}$ is basis-independent. The **Cartan equivariant cohomology** in this item means $H^*(C_T(M),d_T)$; the proof below establishes $d_T^2=0$ on $C_T(M)$.\n\nThis definition does not assert a general isomorphism between the Borel and Cartan cohomology rings. It defines neither equivariant Chow groups nor equivariant quotient-stack Borel–Moore homology, and no rational-coefficient comparison is being used in the real Cartan complex.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-7.pages.json ex-qftx-equivariant-cohomology-of-a-point statement= 'Assume AC and let $T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ act trivially on the one-point smooth manifold $\\{\\ast\\}$. Use the period-one tangent generator and its selected degree-two polynomial generator $u$. In the chosen Borel model, $H_T^*(\\{\\ast\\};\\mathbb R)\\cong\\mathbb R[u]$, with $|u|=2$. The Cartan complex is $\\mathbb R[u]$ with zero differential, so its cohomology is also $\\mathbb R[u]$ under the selected coefficient normalization.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-7.pages.json ex-qftx-equivariant-cohomology-of-a-free-circle-action statement= 'Assume AC. Let $T=M=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, with $T$ acting on $M$ by left translation $t\\cdot[x]=[t+x]$. Use the period-one tangent generator $e$, so the library convention gives $X_e=-\\partial_x$, and let $\\eta$ be the global one-form on $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ descended from $-dx$ on $\\mathbb R$. In the chosen Borel model the map $[v,x]\\mapsto v\\cdot x$ identifies $ET\\times_TT$ with $ET$, hence $H_T^0(T;\\mathbb R)=\\mathbb R$ and $H_T^k(T;\\mathbb R)=0$ for $k>0$. The invariant Cartan complex is $\\mathbb R[u]\\oplus\\mathbb R[u]\\eta$, with $|u|=2$, $|\\eta|=1$, $d_TP(u)=0$, and $d_T(P(u)\\eta)=-uP(u)$. Its cohomology is $\\mathbb R$ in degree zero and zero in positive degrees, agreeing with the Borel calculation for this action.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored draft; current readiness is recorded separately.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-compact-torus-fixed-locus-normal-weights statement= 'Assume countable choice. Let \\(r\\geq 0\\), let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) with its product Lie-group structure, and let \\(M\\) be a compact, finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. Let \\(a:T\\times M\\to M\\) be a jointly smooth left action. Then \\(M\\) admits a smooth Riemannian metric invariant under \\(a\\). Its fixed set\n\\[\nF=M^T:=\\{p\\in M:a(t,p)=p\\text{ for every }t\\in T\\}\n\\]\nis a compact embedded smooth submanifold with finitely many connected components, and \\(T_pF=(T_pM)^T\\) for each \\(p\\in F\\). The orthogonal normal bundle \\(\\nu_F=(TF)^\\perp\\subset TM|_F\\) has no nonzero \\(T\\)-fixed vectors in any fiber. On each connected component of \\(F\\), its normal representation has a finite weight decomposition into nontrivial characters of \\(T\\), with constant multiplicities; equivalently, \\(\\nu_F\\) admits a smooth \\(T\\)-invariant complex structure and orientation on each component.' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-compact-torus-equivariant-slice-neighborhoods statement= 'Assume countable choice. Let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) act smoothly on a compact finite-dimensional smooth manifold \\(M\\) without boundary, and let \\(g\\) be a \\(T\\)-invariant Riemannian metric. If \\(S\\subseteq M\\) is a compact embedded \\(T\\)-invariant submanifold, its orthogonal normal bundle \\(\\nu(S)\\) has a \\(T\\)-invariant open disk neighborhood \\(D_\\epsilon(\\nu(S))\\) of the zero section, and the normal exponential map is a \\(T\\)-equivariant diffeomorphism from that disk bundle onto a \\(T\\)-invariant open neighborhood of \\(S\\). In particular, for \\(x\\in M\\), with stabilizer \\(H=T_x\\), orbit \\(O=T\\cdot x\\), and normal fiber \\(V=\\nu_x(O)\\), let \\(H\\) act on \\(V\\) by its differentials. There is an open \\(H\\)-invariant disk \\(D_\\epsilon(V)\\) such that \\([t,v]\\), the equivalence class of \\((t,v)\\) under \\((t,v)\\sim(th,h^{-1}v)\\) for \\(h\\in H\\), maps by \\([t,v]\\mapsto t\\cdot\\exp_x(v)\\) to a \\(T\\)-equivariant diffeomorphism from \\(T\\times_H D_\\epsilon(V)\\) onto an open neighborhood \\(U_x\\) of \\(O\\). Every stabilizer of a point in \\(U_x\\) is a subgroup of \\(H\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-proper-closed-subgroup-has-annihilating-character statement= 'Assume countable choice. Let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) with \\(r\\geq1\\), and let \\(H\\subsetneq T\\) be a closed subgroup. A character means a smooth group homomorphism from \\(T\\) to the multiplicative unit circle \\(S^1=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}\\). Then there is a nontrivial character \\(\\chi:T\\to S^1\\) whose restriction to all of \\(H\\) is the trivial character.' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-module-localization-preserves-exactness statement= "Let \\(R\\) be a commutative ring with identity, let \\(S\\subseteq R\\) contain \\(1\\), be closed under multiplication, and not contain \\(0\\), and let \\(0\\to M'\\xrightarrow{f}M\\xrightarrow{g}M''\\to0\\) be an exact sequence of \\(R\\)-modules. The localization \\(S^{-1}M\\) is the module of fractions \\(m/s\\), with \\((m,s)\\sim(m',s')\\) exactly when some \\(t\\in S\\) satisfies \\(t(s'm-sm')=0\\). Then\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow S^{-1}M'\\xrightarrow{S^{-1}f}S^{-1}M\\xrightarrow{S^{-1}g}S^{-1}M''\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nis exact." design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-rational-torus-classifying-space-cohomology statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let \\(r\\geq0\\), let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\), and use \\(E_T=(E_{S^1})^r\\), the product of the right Milnor join models, with \\(BT=E_T/T\\). For the \\(i\\)th coordinate character \\(\\chi_i([t_1,\\ldots,t_r])=\\exp(2\\pi i t_i)\\), let \\(L_i=E_T\\times_T\\mathbb C_{\\chi_i}\\) be the associated complex line bundle over \\(BT\\), with the right diagonal equivalence \\((e,z)\\cdot t=(e\\cdot t,\\chi_i(t)^{-1}z)\\), and put \\(u_i=c_1(L_i)\\in H^2(BT;\\mathbb Q)\\). Then\n\\[\nBT\\cong(\\mathbb{CP}^{\\infty})^r,\\qquad H^*(BT;\\mathbb Q)\\cong\\mathbb Q[u_1,\\ldots,u_r],\\qquad |u_i|=2.\n\\]\nEvery smooth character \\(\\chi:T\\to S^1\\) is uniquely \\(\\chi_{\\mathbf n}([t])=\\exp(2\\pi i\\sum_i n_it_i)\\) for \\(\\mathbf n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)\\in\\mathbb Z^r\\), and its associated line satisfies \\(c_1(L_{\\chi_{\\mathbf n}})=\\sum_i n_i u_i\\). In particular a nontrivial character has a nonzero degree-two class. For each \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\), \\(H_q(BT;k)\\) is finite-dimensional in every degree \\(q\\). For \\(r=0\\), \\(BT\\) is a point and its rational cohomology is \\(\\mathbb Q\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-singular-cohomology-homotopy-invariance statement= 'Let \\(k\\) be a field, let \\(X,Y\\) be topological spaces, and let \\(f,g:X\\to Y\\) be continuous maps joined by a continuous homotopy \\(H:X\\times[0,1]\\to Y\\). Then \\(f^*=g^*:H^n(Y;k)\\to H^n(X;k)\\) for every integer \\(n\\). Consequently every homotopy equivalence induces an isomorphism on singular cohomology with coefficients in \\(k\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-compact-smooth-manifold-has-finite-good-cover-nerve-model statement= 'Assume AC. Let \\(F\\) be a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold without boundary and equip it with a Riemannian metric. An open set is strongly geodesically convex if every ordered pair of its points is joined by a unique affinely parametrized globally length-minimizing geodesic whose image stays in the set and whose connector depends smoothly on the endpoints. There are finitely many such open sets \\(U_1,\\ldots,U_N\\) covering \\(F\\), every nonempty finite intersection of which is strongly geodesically convex. Let \\(K\\) be the finite simplicial complex whose nonempty faces are the index sets \\(\\sigma\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\) for which \\(U_\\sigma=\\bigcap_{i\\in\\sigma}U_i\\) is nonempty. Then its usual geometric realization \\(|K|\\) is homotopy equivalent to \\(F\\). For \\(F=\\varnothing\\), take \\(N=0\\) and \\(K=\\varnothing\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-compact-manifold-cohomology-positive-ideal-nilpotent statement= 'Assume AC. Let \\(F\\) be a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold without boundary, equipped with a Riemannian metric, and let \\(k\\) be a field. There is a finite CW complex \\(K\\) homotopy equivalent to \\(F\\), and an integer \\(N\\geq1\\) such that \\(H^q(F;k)=0\\) for every \\(q\\geq N\\). Consequently the ideal \\(J=\\bigoplus_{q>0}H^q(F;k)\\) in the graded cohomology ring satisfies \\(J^N=0\\). If \\(F=\\varnothing\\), take \\(K=\\varnothing\\), \\(N=1\\), and \\(J=0\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-borel-base-is-paracompact-of-cw-type statement= 'Assume AC. Let \\(r\\geq0\\), let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) use the product Milnor model \\(BT=(\\mathbb{CP}^{\\infty})^r\\) with its ordinary product topology, and let \\(F\\) be a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. Then \\(BT\\times F\\) is paracompact Hausdorff and has the homotopy type of a CW complex. In fact, there is a finite CW complex \\(K\\) such that \\(BT\\times F\\simeq BT\\times K\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 draft authored; final proof-contract, scoped validation, and readiness evidence pending.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json def-qftx-character-localization statement= '' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-fixed-normal-euler-class-is-unit-after-character-localization statement= 'Assume AC. Let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) act smoothly on a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold \\(M\\) without boundary, and set \\(F=M^T\\). Give the normal bundle \\(\\nu_F=(TF)^\\perp\\) the componentwise \\(T\\)-invariant complex orientation supplied by the fixed-locus normal-weight decomposition. Let \\(\\nu_{F,T}=ET\\times_T\\nu_F\\to BT\\times F\\) be its Borel vector bundle and let \\(e_T(\\nu_F)\\in H_T^*(F;k)=H^*(BT\\times F;k)\\), \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\), be its zero-section Euler class. Then \\(e_T(\\nu_F)\\) is a unit in \\(\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(F;k)\\). On each connected component \\(F_0\\subseteq F\\), restriction to \\(BT\\times\\{p\\}\\), \\(p\\in F_0\\), is the product of the nontrivial character classes of the normal representation at \\(p\\), with multiplicity and the chosen complex orientation.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json lem-qftx-nonfixed-locus-cohomology-is-torsion-after-localization statement= 'Assume AC. Let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) act smoothly on a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable manifold \\(M\\) without boundary, let \\(F=M^T\\), and use the product Milnor Borel construction with coefficients \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\). Let \\(\\mathscr S_T(k)\\) be the multiplicative set generated by the first Chern classes of all nontrivial smooth characters of \\(T\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(M\\setminus F;k)=0.\n\\]\nEquivalently, every class in the Borel equivariant cohomology of the nonfixed locus is annihilated by some finite product of nontrivial character classes.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions statement= 'Assume AC. Let \\(T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r\\) act smoothly on a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold \\(M\\) without boundary, and let \\(F=M^T\\). For \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\), define \\(H_T^*(X;k)=H^*(ET\\times_T X;k)\\) using the product of the right Milnor circle models, let \\(A_T(k)=H^*(BT;k)\\), and let \\(\\mathscr S_T(k)\\) be the multiplicative set generated by the first Chern classes of all nontrivial smooth characters \\(T\\to S^1\\). Then restriction along \\(i:F\\hookrightarrow M\\) induces a graded-ring isomorphism\n\\[\ni^*:\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(M;k)\\xrightarrow{\\ \\cong\\ }\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(F;k).\n\\]\nThe conclusion concerns compact finite-dimensional smooth \\(T\\)-manifolds and ordinary Borel singular cohomology; it makes no noncompact or compact-support assertion.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json ex-qftx-borel-localization-rotation-on-projective-line statement= 'Let \\(T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) act on \\(M=\\mathbb{CP}^1\\) by \\(t\\cdot[z_0:z_1]=[z_0:e^{2\\pi it}z_1]\\). For \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\), use the product Milnor Borel model and let \\(u=c_1(L_\\chi)\\in H^2(BT;k)\\) for \\(\\chi(t)=e^{2\\pi it}\\). The fixed set is \\(F=\\{p_+,p_-\\}\\), where \\(p_+=[1:0]\\) and \\(p_-=[0:1]\\); the tangent weights are \\(+1\\) at \\(p_+\\) and \\(-1\\) at \\(p_-\\). If \\(\\mathscr S_T(k)\\) is generated by nontrivial character classes and \\(A_T(k)=H^*(BT;k)\\), then restriction identifies\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(\\mathbb{CP}^1;k)\\cong\n\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}A_T(k)\\oplus\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}A_T(k),\n\\]\nwith the two summands given by evaluation at \\(p_+\\) and \\(p_-\\).' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json ex-qftx-borel-localization-free-circle-orbit statement= 'Let \\(T=M=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) act on \\(M\\) by left translation \\(t\\cdot[x]=[t+x]\\), and use the product Milnor Borel model with \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\). This action has no fixed point. The map \\([e,x]\\mapsto e\\cdot x\\) identifies \\(ET\\times_TT\\) with \\(ET\\), so \\(H_T^*(T;k)\\cong k\\) in degree zero and is zero in positive degrees. Every positive-degree class in \\(A_T(k)\\) acts as zero, so localization at the nontrivial character classes gives \\(\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(T;k)=0\\), agreeing with restriction to the empty fixed set.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-8.pages.json ex-qftx-borel-localization-positive-dimensional-fixed-components statement= 'Let \\(T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) act on \\(M=\\mathbb{CP}^1\\times\\mathbb{CP}^1\\) by \\(t\\cdot([z_0:z_1],q)=([z_0:e^{2\\pi it}z_1],q)\\), and let \\(k\\in\\{\\mathbb Q,\\mathbb R\\}\\). The fixed set is the disjoint union \\(F=(\\{p_+\\}\\times\\mathbb{CP}^1)\\amalg(\\{p_-\\}\\times\\mathbb{CP}^1)\\), with \\(p_+=[1:0]\\) and \\(p_-=[0:1]\\); the normal weights are \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\), respectively. Let \\(u\\in H^2(BT;k)\\) be the weight-one character class and \\(b\\in H^2(\\mathbb{CP}^1;k)\\) the generator with \\(b^2=0\\). Then localized restriction identifies\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(M;k)\\cong\n\\bigl(\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}A_T(k)\\otimes_k k[b]/(b^2)\\bigr)\\oplus\n\\bigl(\\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}A_T(k)\\otimes_k k[b]/(b^2)\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere the two summands are the Borel cohomology of the two positive-dimensional fixed components.' design_state= 'Step 1 authored conditionally; current batch-7 real-supplier readiness records are missing; readiness escalated pending final evidence.'
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions statement= 'Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^r$ have the standard product presentation, smooth structure, and period-one basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_r$ of the upstream torus definition. Use its selected product Milnor model $ET=(E_{S^1})^r$ with the right diagonal action and $BT=ET/T$. For a smooth left $T$-manifold $X$, define real Borel equivariant cohomology by $H_T^*(X;\\mathbb R)=H^*(ET\\times_T X;\\mathbb R)$ using the quotient convention $(e\\cdot t,x)\\sim(e,t\\cdot x)$. Its coefficient ring is $\\mathcal R=H^*(BT;\\mathbb R)\\cong\\operatorname{Sym}_{\\mathbb R}(\\mathfrak t^*)=\\mathbb R[u_1,\\ldots,u_r]$, where $u_i$ is the class corresponding to the dual period-one basis vector $e_i^*$ and each $u_i$ has degree $2$.\n\nFor an integer vector $m=(m_1,\\ldots,m_r)$, write the character as $\\chi_m(t)=\\exp(2\\pi i\\sum_i m_i t_i)$ and define its degree-two class by $u_{\\chi_m}=\\sum_i m_i u_i$. With the stated quotient convention, this is the Euler class of the Borel line $ET\\times_T\\mathbb C_{\\chi_m}$, where $T$ acts on $\\mathbb C_{\\chi_m}$ by $\\chi_m$. Use the complex-orientation convention $c_1(L)=e(L_{\\mathbb R})$ for a complex line $L$; thus $u_\\chi$ is also the first Chern class of its Borel character line. Let $S\\subset\\mathcal R$ be the multiplicative set generated by these classes for all nontrivial characters, together with $1$; thus $u_\\chi\\ne0$ exactly for nontrivial $\\chi$. Write $S^{-1}H_T^*(X;\\mathbb R)$ and $S^{-1}\\mathcal R$ for the corresponding localizations. The coefficient-ring identification is used under AC: the upstream classifying-space cohomology proof invokes AC in its Künneth and coefficient arguments.\n\nIf $X$ is a compact oriented smooth $T$-manifold without boundary of dimension $n$ and the action preserves its orientation, the Borel projection $\\pi_X:ET\\times_T X\\to BT$ has compact oriented $n$-dimensional fibers. Define equivariant integration $\\int_X\\alpha=(\\pi_X)_!\\alpha$, the cohomological pushforward along those fibers; it lowers degree by $n$ and has values in $\\mathcal R$. For an oriented $T$-equivariant vector bundle $E\\to X$ whose Borel-associated bundle has a normalized Thom class, define its equivariant Euler class $e_T(E)$ as the pullback of that Thom class by the zero section. The Thom class is normalized to restrict on each oriented fiber pair $(D^c,S^{c-1})$ to the positive generator of $H^c(D^c,S^{c-1};\\mathbb R)$. For rank $c=0$, the Thom class and Euler class are $1$. For a complex representation line of character $\\chi$, this convention gives Euler class $u_\\chi$. For a fixed component $F$ of a $T$-action on $M$, the orientation convention is $o(TM|_F)=o(TF)\\wedge o(\\nu_F)$, where $\\nu_F$ is its chosen equivariantly oriented normal bundle. This is the Borel convention; no identification with a Cartan, Chow, or quotient-stack cohomology model is assumed here.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities statement= "Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly on a compact oriented smooth manifold $M$ without boundary. Let $N\\subseteq M^T$ be a compact closed embedded submanifold without boundary of codimension $c$, let $i:N\\hookrightarrow M$ be inclusion, and let its normal bundle $\\nu$ have a $T$-invariant orientation. Orient $N$ by $o(TM|_N)=o(TN)\\wedge o(\\nu)$. If $N=\\varnothing$, the source groups and all asserted maps are zero. Otherwise, with the real Borel conventions of the preceding definition, there is a fiber-normalized equivariant Thom class $\\tau_\\nu\\in H_T^c(\\nu,\\nu\\setminus N;\\mathbb R)$, and cup product with $\\tau_\\nu$ is an isomorphism $H_T^k(N;\\mathbb R)\\to H_T^{k+c}(\\nu,\\nu\\setminus N;\\mathbb R)$ for every $k$. A $T$-invariant tubular neighborhood and excision therefore define a cohomological Gysin map $i_*^T:H_T^k(N;\\mathbb R)\\to H_T^{k+c}(M;\\mathbb R)$. It satisfies\n\n$$i^*i_*^T(\\beta)=e_T(\\nu)\\smile\\beta,\\qquad \\int_M i_*^T(\\beta)=\\int_N\\beta.$$\n\nIf $N'$ is a different fixed component disjoint from $N$, then $i_{N'}^*i_{N*}^T(\\beta)=0$. These maps use the Thom normalization in which every oriented normal fiber integrates to $1$." design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility statement= 'Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let a compact torus $T$ act smoothly on a compact smooth manifold $M$ without boundary, and let $F=M^T$. Then $F$ is a compact smooth embedded submanifold, its connected components $F_j$ are finite in number, and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$ for every $p\\in F$. If $F=\\varnothing$, the component assertions are vacuous. The normal bundle $\\nu_j$ of each component has no trivial $T$-representation in any fiber. Give $\\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation, write $c_j=\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb R}\\nu_j$, and choose an invariant complex structure on it; let $\\epsilon_j\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ compare that complex orientation with the chosen orientation. If the nonzero characters of the normal representation on a fiber, counted with multiplicity, are $\\chi_{j,1},\\ldots,\\chi_{j,c_j/2}$, then the component of $e_T(\\nu_j)$ in $H^0(F_j;\\mathbb R)\\otimes\\mathcal R$ is\n\n$$\\epsilon_j\\prod_{a=1}^{c_j/2}u_{\\chi_{j,a}}.$$\n\nConsequently $e_T(\\nu_j)$ is invertible in $S^{-1}H_T^*(F_j;\\mathbb R)$. When $c_j=0$, the product is empty, equals $1$, and the Euler class is already a unit.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula statement= 'Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly on a compact oriented smooth $n$-manifold $M$ without boundary; use the standard Hausdorff, second-countable manifold convention, and assume the action preserves the orientation. Write $F=M^T=\\coprod_{j=1}^{s}F_j$ for its fixed locus, with $s<\\infty$, and $i_j:F_j\\hookrightarrow M$ for each connected component. Give each normal bundle $\\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation and orient $F_j$ by $o(TM|_{F_j})=o(TF_j)\\wedge o(\\nu_j)$. Use real Borel equivariant cohomology, $\\mathcal R=H^*(BT;\\mathbb R)$, the multiplicative set $S$ generated by nonzero character classes, and the degree-shifting fiber-integration convention of the preceding definition. For every homogeneous $\\alpha\\in H_T^k(M;\\mathbb R)$,\n\n$$\\int_M\\alpha=\\sum_j\\int_{F_j}\\frac{i_j^*\\alpha}{e_T(\\nu_j)}\\quad\\text{in }S^{-1}H^{k-n}(BT;\\mathbb R).$$\n\nIf the fixed locus is nonempty and consists of isolated points, necessarily $n=2m$. Use the ambient orientation on each normal space $\\nu_p=T_pM$ and the positive orientation on each point $p$. Choose a $T$-invariant complex structure on each $T_pM$ and let $\\chi_{p,1},\\ldots,\\chi_{p,m}$ be its nonzero character weights. Let $\\epsilon_p=+1$ when its complex orientation agrees with the orientation of $M$ at $p$ and $-1$ otherwise. Then the same formula specializes to\n\n$$\\int_M\\alpha=\\sum_{p\\in M^T}\\frac{\\epsilon_p\\,i_p^*\\alpha}{\\prod_{a=1}^{m}u_{\\chi_{p,a}}}\\quad\\text{in }S^{-1}\\mathcal R.$$\n\nIf the fixed locus is empty, the fixed-component formula is the empty sum $0$ for every $n$; no parity condition or integer $m$ is needed in that case.\n\nThis is a finite-dimensional formula for compact manifolds; it does not assert a formula for arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces or infinite-dimensional field spaces.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json ex-abbv-projective-line-standard-circle-action statement= 'Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let $T=S^1=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ act on complex-oriented $M=\\mathbb{CP}^1$ by $\\lambda[z_0:z_1]=[z_0:\\lambda z_1]$. Let $u\\in H^2(BT;\\mathbb R)$ be the class corresponding to the period-one weight-$+1$ character under the chosen tautological-Euler normalization, let $\\mathcal O(1)$ be the dual tautological line with its induced equivariant structure, and put $x=e_T(\\mathcal O(1))$. The fixed points are $p_0=[1:0]$ and $p_\\infty=[0:1]$. Their tangent weights are $+1$ and $-1$, while $x|_{p_0}=0$ and $x|_{p_\\infty}=-u$. The ABBV sum is\n\n$$\\frac{0}{u}+\\frac{-u}{-u}=1,$$\n\nwhich agrees with $\\int_{\\mathbb{CP}^1}x=1$.' design_state= None
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-9.pages.json ex-abbv-positive-dimensional-fixed-components statement= 'Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let $T=S^1=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ act on complex-oriented $M=\\mathbb{CP}^1\\times\\mathbb{CP}^1$ by $\\lambda([z_0:z_1],[w_0:w_1])=([z_0:\\lambda z_1],[w_0:w_1])$. Let $\\eta\\in H^2(\\mathbb{CP}^1;\\mathbb R)$ be the positive generator with integral $1$, let $x_1$ be the pullback of the equivariant Euler class of the dual tautological line from the first factor, and set $\\alpha=x_1\\smile\\operatorname{pr}_2^*\\eta$. The fixed components are $F_0=\\{[1:0]\\}\\times\\mathbb{CP}^1$ and $F_\\infty=\\{[0:1]\\}\\times\\mathbb{CP}^1$, with normal weights $+1$ and $-1$. The component formula gives a zero contribution from $F_0$ and $\\int_{\\mathbb{CP}^1}\\eta=1$ from $F_\\infty$, matching $\\int_M\\alpha=1$.' design_state= None
#!/usr/bin/env node
import { existsSync as physicsExistsSync } from 'node:fs';
const physicsToolRoot = fileURLToPath(new URL('../../', import.meta.url));
// content-policy.mjs — enforce future-session provenance and containment rules.
//
//   node tools/physics-support/content-policy.mjs research/level<n>-batch-*.pages.json [--manifest-only] [--json]
//   node tools/physics-support/content-policy.mjs --audit --manifest-only research/audit/wave<k>-*.pages.json
//
// This gate deliberately receives an explicit in-flight batch scope.  The
// library has a large legacy corpus whose provenance was not recorded under the
// present contract; retrospectively guessing those tags would be dishonest.  A
// future authored item, however, must disclose its provenance and cannot make
// an AI-generated Statement/Construction a dependency. Proof provenance is
// deliberately irrelevant: the cited claim, not its local derivation, bounds
// downstream propagation.

import { readFileSync, readdirSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join, basename } from 'node:path';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
import { parseFrontmatter, nested, referenceUrls, openProblemErrors } from './content-policy-lib.mjs';

const REPO = (process.env.PHYSICS_REPO ?? (physicsExistsSync(join(physicsToolRoot, 'PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md')) ? join(physicsToolRoot, 'physics') : physicsToolRoot));
const importedIds = new Set(physicsExistsSync(join(REPO, 'research/math-imports.json')) ? JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(REPO, 'research/math-imports.json'), 'utf8')).files.filter(f => f.path.startsWith('items/')).map(f => f.path.split('/').at(-1).replace(/\.md$/, '')) : []);
const argv = process.argv.slice(2);
const asJson = argv.includes('--json');
// Planning needs to reject an over-cap or malformed manifest *before* authoring.
// The normal policy additionally verifies the corresponding item files after
// Step 3.  Keeping these modes explicit prevents expected missing draft files
// from being misreported as a failed pre-authoring gate.
const manifestOnly = argv.includes('--manifest-only');
// Construction joins inspect existing carriers before canonical transcription.
// This mode binds reuse to their unique actual page home, never just their ID.
const authored = argv.includes('--authored');
if (authored && (!manifestOnly || argv.includes('--audit'))) {
  console.error('--authored requires --manifest-only and cannot be combined with --audit');
  process.exit(2);
}
// `--manifest-only --audit` is the AUDIT's A0 shape. It exists because the two
// modes ask different questions of the same file. For a future build batch,
// manifest-only asks "may these ids be minted here?"; for an audit wave the
// manifest lists ids that are published by construction, so those questions
// answer themselves and the checks that encode them (batch-item-already-exists,
// and the reading-order rules batch-b-leaf-target / batch-forward-dependency,
// which legacy pages predate) fire on every well-formed audit manifest. What
// still means something — shape, an id claimed by two batches, a dangling deps
// target, a plan-spec home collision — is kept. Measured 2026-08-04 on wave 4:
// 95 errors before, 0 after, with no check lost that could ever have been true.
// A B-leaf or backwards edge in published content is a FINDING for the wave to
// read, surfaced by audit-manifest.mjs; it is not a gate failure.
// --audit (owner, 2026-08-02, AUDIT-WORKFLOW.md): published-page audit scope.
// The scope is retro-tagged legacy content, so the future-batch containment
// rules that cannot be applied retroactively (generated roles, the
// ai-generated dependency prohibition, structured external records) downgrade
// to visible warnings routed to genrisk/owner dispositions, while provenance
