Hardie, Kamps and Kieboom show that every 2-morphism in Π2(X) is invert-
ible, and they construct an adjoint equivalence (f, ¯f, ιf, ǫf) for every morphism
f in Π2(X). This is why they call Π2(X) a ‘bigroupoid’. One might also call
this a ‘coherent 2-groupoid’, since such a thing with one object is precisely a
coherent 2-group. Regardless of the terminology, this implies that for any point
x ∈X there is a coherent 2-group whose objects are morphisms f: x →x in
Π2(X), and whose morphisms are those 2-morphisms α: f ⇒g in Π2(X) for
which f, g: x →x. We call this coherent 2-group the fundamental 2-group
Π2(X, x).
In fact, a fundamental 2-group is a special case of an ‘autoequivalence 2-
group’, as defined in Example 35. A point x ∈X is an object of the weak
2-category Π2(X), and the autoequivalence 2-group of this object is precisely
the fundamental 2-group Π2(X, x). Even better, we can turn this idea around:
there is a way to see any autoequivalence 2-group as the fundamental 2-group of
some space, at least up to equivalence! Unfortunately, proving this fact would
take us too far out of our way here. However, the relation between 2-groups
and topology is so important that we should at least sketch the basic idea.
Suppose K is a weak 2-category, and let K0 be its underlying coherent 2-
groupoid — that is, the weak 2-category with the same objects as K, but with
the adjoint equivalences in K as its morphisms and the invertible 2-morphisms
of K as its 2-morphisms. Let |K0| be the geometric realization of the nerve of
K0 as defined by Duskin [23]. Then any object x ∈K gives a point x ∈|K0|,
and the autoequivalence 2-group Auteq(x) is equivalent to Π2(|K0|, x).
In fact, something much stronger than this should be true. According to
current thinking on n-categories and homotopy theory [4], 2-groups should really
be ‘the same’ as connected pointed homotopy 2-types. For example, we should
be able to construct a 2-category Conn2Typ∗having connected pointed CW
complexes with πn = 0 for n > 2 as objects, continuous basepoint-preserving
maps as morphisms, and homotopy classes of basepoint-preserving homotopies
between such maps as 2-morphisms.
